WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:14.480 --> 00:00:22.240
In the year 1815, the English colonial&nbsp;
official Thomas Stamford Raffles would

00:00:22.240 --> 00:00:28.560
travel across the island of&nbsp;
Java in what is today Indonesia.

00:00:31.280 --> 00:00:39.840
At this time, the world was torn by war.&nbsp;
In Europe, the Napoleonic Wars were raging,

00:00:39.840 --> 00:00:48.400
and the Earth's oceans had become just another&nbsp;
battlefront. The lush tropical island of Java

00:00:48.400 --> 00:00:55.200
had been until recently a Dutch colony, but the&nbsp;
Netherlands had been conquered by Napoleon in

00:00:55.200 --> 00:01:01.760
his advance across Europe, and so, the Dutch&nbsp;
colonies had fallen under the control of the

00:01:01.760 --> 00:01:10.800
French. Now the British desired to take Java&nbsp;
for themselves and strike a blow at Napoleon's

00:01:10.800 --> 00:01:20.240
overseas empire. In 1811, British Marines&nbsp;
launched an invasion of Java, landing among

00:01:20.240 --> 00:01:28.400
its coral reefs and sky-blue oceans, clambering&nbsp;
through its shady mangrove forests, and up its

00:01:28.400 --> 00:01:36.640
beaches of black volcanic sand, climbing the steep&nbsp;
cliffs in the shadow of its towering volcanoes.

00:01:37.280 --> 00:01:44.160
From there they overwhelmed the inexperienced&nbsp;
Dutch defenders, and took the island for the

00:01:44.160 --> 00:01:52.720
British Empire. With them came the Englishman&nbsp;
Thomas Stamford Raffles, who later wrote about

00:01:52.720 --> 00:02:00.960
these events. Holland had ceased to be an&nbsp;
independent nation, and in the year 1811,

00:02:00.960 --> 00:02:07.600
the French flag was hoisted over Java. But&nbsp;
on the 17th of September of the same year,

00:02:07.600 --> 00:02:16.240
a capitulation was entered into, by which all the&nbsp;
dependencies fell into the hands of Great Britain.

00:02:16.240 --> 00:02:23.680
Raffles was a lifelong sailor, and had actually&nbsp;
been born at sea on a ship commanded by his father

00:02:23.680 --> 00:02:31.200
off the coast of Jamaica. He became a clerk in the&nbsp;
British East India Company at the age of fourteen,

00:02:31.200 --> 00:02:37.360
and from there became a true company&nbsp;
man. He had spent the best part of the

00:02:37.360 --> 00:02:42.880
last decade furthering the interests&nbsp;
of British colonialism in East Asia,

00:02:42.880 --> 00:02:50.480
and had even become fluent in the language of&nbsp;
Malay. When Java was captured by the British,

00:02:50.480 --> 00:02:55.440
partly as a result of his skill at&nbsp;
languages, Raffles was granted the

00:02:55.440 --> 00:03:02.880
position of Lieutenant Governor. At the&nbsp;
age of just thirty, Raffles found himself

00:03:02.880 --> 00:03:13.520
in charge of an island a thousand kilometers&nbsp;
across, home to more than six million people.

00:03:17.200 --> 00:03:24.400
Raffles was fascinated by Java. He&nbsp;
remarked on its dramatic landscape of

00:03:24.400 --> 00:03:33.280
soaring volcanoes and steamy rainforests, its&nbsp;
fertile rice terraces and remarkable culture,

00:03:33.280 --> 00:03:40.800
as he wrote home to King George III. The&nbsp;
gracious permission which I have received

00:03:40.800 --> 00:03:46.480
affords me an opportunity of interesting your&nbsp;
royal highness in favor of the amiable and

00:03:46.480 --> 00:03:52.320
ingenious people whose country they describe. A&nbsp;
chain of very high mountains runs along the whole

00:03:52.320 --> 00:03:58.560
length of Java. Even in July and August, the&nbsp;
atmosphere is refreshed by occasional showers,

00:03:58.560 --> 00:04:05.600
and the landscape is at all times of the&nbsp;
year covered with the brightest green.

00:04:05.600 --> 00:04:14.400
But most of all, he was fascinated by Java's&nbsp;
antiquities. Everywhere among its forests

00:04:14.400 --> 00:04:20.880
and shaded rice terraces could be seen the&nbsp;
remains of the countless temples, palaces,

00:04:20.880 --> 00:04:28.080
and even whole ancient cities left in ruins.

00:04:28.080 --> 00:04:35.760
A few years later, around 1815, he embarked on&nbsp;
a journey around the island, with the aim of

00:04:35.760 --> 00:04:46.480
surveying these ruins and writing a book. Although&nbsp;
by then Java was an overwhelmingly Muslim land,

00:04:46.480 --> 00:04:53.920
Raffles found everywhere the remains of an&nbsp;
older culture hiding just beneath the surface,

00:04:53.920 --> 00:05:03.200
as he writes in his work, The History of Java. The&nbsp;
Muhammadan religion, as it present exists on Java,

00:05:03.200 --> 00:05:07.520
seems only to have penetrated the surface,&nbsp;
and to have taken but little root in the

00:05:07.520 --> 00:05:12.960
heart of Javans. But the antiquities&nbsp;
of Java consist of ruins of edifices,

00:05:12.960 --> 00:05:18.320
and in particular of temples sacred to the&nbsp;
former worship; images of deities found within

00:05:18.320 --> 00:05:23.600
them and scattered throughout the country,&nbsp;
either sculpted in stone or cast in metal,

00:05:23.600 --> 00:05:31.760
inscriptions on stone and copper in&nbsp;
ancient characters and ancient coins.

00:05:31.760 --> 00:05:38.880
Raffles was fascinated by these remains,&nbsp;
which seemed to confirm stories he had heard,

00:05:38.880 --> 00:05:45.680
stories of a great empire that had risen up&nbsp;
on that island, a thriving combination of

00:05:45.680 --> 00:05:52.400
Hinduism and Buddhism that had flourished for&nbsp;
centuries, spread its influence across the sea

00:05:52.400 --> 00:05:58.640
to countless other islands in the Indonesian&nbsp;
archipelago, and built what may have been

00:05:58.640 --> 00:06:10.480
one of the largest empires in Southeast&nbsp;
Asia. This was the empire of Majapahit.

00:06:10.480 --> 00:06:15.440
The numerous and interesting remains of former&nbsp;
art and grandeur, which exist in the ruins

00:06:15.440 --> 00:06:21.520
of temples and other edifices the abundant&nbsp;
treasures of sculpture and statuary either

00:06:21.520 --> 00:06:26.800
lay entirely buried under rubbish or were&nbsp;
but partially examined. Nothing, therefore,

00:06:26.800 --> 00:06:31.600
of the ancient history of the people, of their&nbsp;
institutions prior to the introduction of

00:06:31.600 --> 00:06:38.080
Muhammadanism, of their magnificence and power,&nbsp;
could be accurately known or fully relied on. The

00:06:38.080 --> 00:06:43.920
grandeur of their ancestors sounds like a fable,&nbsp;
and it is only when it can be traced in monuments,

00:06:43.920 --> 00:06:51.680
which cannot be falsified, that we are led to&nbsp;
give credit to their traditions concerning it.

00:06:51.680 --> 00:06:58.880
Raffles became convinced that he could find the&nbsp;
remnants of the civilization of Majapahit, and so,

00:06:58.880 --> 00:07:05.360
he traveled the length and breadth of the island&nbsp;
and visited every ruined site he could along the

00:07:05.360 --> 00:07:13.680
way. He stopped at the ancient cities of Kediri&nbsp;
and Singhasari, and found there the crumbling

00:07:13.680 --> 00:07:24.240
remains of city walls, palaces, and carved&nbsp;
statues of the Buddha alongside Hindu gods.

00:07:24.240 --> 00:07:31.200
But when he got to the valley of the Brantas&nbsp;
River in East Java, the locals advised him not to

00:07:31.200 --> 00:07:42.080
go near the ruins. They told him that a terrible&nbsp;
curse still lingered over those crumbling stones.

00:07:42.080 --> 00:07:46.480
The natives have a superstition that the&nbsp;
site of this ancient capital cannot be

00:07:46.480 --> 00:07:51.360
visited without some misfortune attaching to the&nbsp;
party who undertake the visit. Those whom I had,

00:07:51.360 --> 00:07:54.800
with some difficulty, induced&nbsp;
to accompany me to the spot,

00:07:54.800 --> 00:08:01.280
did not fail to assure me that I should&nbsp;
lose my government within the year. Finally,

00:08:01.280 --> 00:08:07.280
Raffles visited the great capital of&nbsp;
Majapahit, which his guides told him

00:08:07.280 --> 00:08:17.680
lay buried near the town of Trowulan. At Trowulan,&nbsp;
Raffles hoped to find a magnificent ruined city,

00:08:17.680 --> 00:08:26.160
testifying to the former greatness of this power.&nbsp;
But when he arrived, he would be disappointed.

00:08:26.960 --> 00:08:33.120
He would find little more than a scatter&nbsp;
of broken brickwork and assorted sculpture

00:08:33.120 --> 00:08:43.360
littering the landscape, buried in volcanic&nbsp;
ash, and shaded beneath the towering teak trees.

00:08:43.360 --> 00:08:49.920
A village adjacent is called Tra Wulan, or the&nbsp;
Light of the Moon. The ruins of the palace and

00:08:49.920 --> 00:08:55.040
several gateways of burnt brick are to be&nbsp;
seen, but the whole country for many miles

00:08:55.040 --> 00:09:00.720
is thickly covered with a stately teak forest,&nbsp;
which appears to have been the growth of ages,

00:09:00.720 --> 00:09:05.920
so that it is difficult to trace the outline&nbsp;
of this former capital. Ruins of temples,

00:09:05.920 --> 00:09:11.680
mostly executed in brick, are scattered about the&nbsp;
country for many miles, and attest to the extent

00:09:11.680 --> 00:09:18.480
and grandeur of this pride of Java. The temples&nbsp;
are beautifully decorated with representations

00:09:18.480 --> 00:09:26.720
of flowers and other peculiar ornaments,&nbsp;
which it would be difficult to describe.

00:09:26.720 --> 00:09:32.560
In fact, the local people turned out to&nbsp;
be right, and perhaps Raffles should have

00:09:32.560 --> 00:09:40.080
listened to their warnings. Raffles would only&nbsp;
govern the island of Java for another year.

00:09:40.800 --> 00:09:47.440
The Napoleonic Wars soon came to an end, and&nbsp;
the British returned the colony to the now

00:09:47.440 --> 00:09:54.480
independent Dutch, relieving Raffles&nbsp;
of his post, and he would go on to

00:09:54.480 --> 00:10:01.360
suffer more misfortune in the years that&nbsp;
followed. Just ten years later, he would

00:10:01.360 --> 00:10:07.680
have a stroke likely caused by an underlying&nbsp;
condition in the blood vessels of his brain.

00:10:08.320 --> 00:10:18.320
This stroke would kill him the day before his 45th&nbsp;
birthday. As Raffles wandered about those ruins of

00:10:18.320 --> 00:10:25.920
crumbling red brick at the site of the ancient&nbsp;
city of Majapahit, he had no idea that his fate

00:10:25.920 --> 00:10:33.760
was already sealed by the birth defect lurking in&nbsp;
his head. But perhaps that day he thought about

00:10:33.760 --> 00:10:40.960
life and death as he walked beneath the shade of&nbsp;
that teak forest and listened to the calling of

00:10:40.960 --> 00:10:49.920
koels and myna birds high in the trees. Perhaps&nbsp;
he thought about the rise and fall of empires and

00:10:49.920 --> 00:10:56.640
the changing fortunes of the world. Perhaps&nbsp;
he wondered how such a power had risen up on

00:10:56.640 --> 00:11:04.480
the island of Java and left such a mark on the&nbsp;
memories and imaginations of that land's people.

00:11:04.480 --> 00:11:11.680
Perhaps he asked himself how its people had&nbsp;
constructed these temples and palaces, these walls

00:11:11.680 --> 00:11:21.760
and reservoirs, carved such beautiful and haunting&nbsp;
monuments, and then fallen all at once into ruin.

00:11:56.880 --> 00:12:04.240
My name's Paul Cooper, and you're listening to&nbsp;
the Fall of Civilizations podcast. Each episode,

00:12:04.240 --> 00:12:10.320
I look at a civilization of the past that rose&nbsp;
to glory and then collapsed into the ashes of

00:12:10.320 --> 00:12:17.760
history. I want to ask; what did they have in&nbsp;
common? What led to their fall? And what did

00:12:17.760 --> 00:12:26.560
it feel like to be a person alive at the time who&nbsp;
witnessed the end of their world? In this episode,

00:12:26.560 --> 00:12:34.720
I want to tell the story of the Majapahit Empire&nbsp;
of medieval Indonesia. I want to show how this

00:12:34.720 --> 00:12:42.960
empire of the islands grew up on the volcanic land&nbsp;
of Java, and how it beat the odds to survive in

00:12:42.960 --> 00:12:51.280
a world of medieval superpowers. I want to show&nbsp;
how Majapahit expanded its influence and dominion

00:12:51.280 --> 00:12:59.200
to other lands over the seas, and undertook&nbsp;
voyages that have few precedents in history,

00:12:59.200 --> 00:13:10.480
and I want to show what happened to bring this&nbsp;
empire of the islands finally crashing down.

00:13:19.600 --> 00:13:25.040
The surface of planet Earth&nbsp;
is a thin crust of solid rock,

00:13:25.040 --> 00:13:30.160
which sits over the softer and&nbsp;
much hotter mantle beneath it.

00:13:31.120 --> 00:13:39.920
This crust can be as much as 80 kilometers thick&nbsp;
in places, and as thin as 10 kilometers in others,

00:13:39.920 --> 00:13:52.480
and it is divided into seven major tectonic&nbsp;
plates. The largest of these is the Pacific Plate.

00:13:52.480 --> 00:13:59.760
It is a vast sheet of solid rock more than 100&nbsp;
million square kilometers in size that makes

00:13:59.760 --> 00:14:06.320
up much of the floor of the Pacific Ocean,&nbsp;
and which alone accounts for about a fifth

00:14:06.320 --> 00:14:16.320
of Earth's total surface. The Pacific Plate is&nbsp;
made up almost entirely of oceanic crust, which

00:14:16.320 --> 00:14:24.560
is different to the continental crust that we all&nbsp;
live on. Oceanic crust is typically much thinner,

00:14:24.560 --> 00:14:33.120
but made of denser basalt rock. For this reason,&nbsp;
whenever it impacts with other tectonic plates,

00:14:33.120 --> 00:14:39.040
the Pacific Plate is pushed downwards into&nbsp;
the Earth's upper mantle, creating a zone

00:14:39.040 --> 00:14:49.520
of subduction, and these subduction zones are&nbsp;
renowned for their violent volcanic activity.

00:14:49.520 --> 00:14:56.480
Due to its immense size and density, the&nbsp;
Pacific Plate has the most subduction zones of

00:14:56.480 --> 00:15:07.280
any tectonic plate, and as a result, its borders&nbsp;
are the most volcanically active areas on Earth.

00:15:12.080 --> 00:15:20.880
This ring of raging volcanoes stretches in a vast&nbsp;
horseshoe shape for more than 40,000 kilometers,

00:15:20.880 --> 00:15:27.360
or about the circumference of the Earth, from&nbsp;
the southern tip of South America all around

00:15:27.360 --> 00:15:32.320
the Western shore of the Americas,&nbsp;
and then through the sea down the

00:15:32.320 --> 00:15:40.240
Eastern coast of Asia. To geologists&nbsp;
it is called the Circum-Pacific Belt,

00:15:40.240 --> 00:16:02.080
but in popular usage, it has come to be known&nbsp;
by another name: the Pacific Ring of Fire.

00:16:02.080 --> 00:16:07.840
The 19th-century English geologist&nbsp;
George P. Scrope was one of the

00:16:07.840 --> 00:16:14.640
first to describe this far-ranging phenomenon.

00:16:14.640 --> 00:16:20.480
A striking and constant delineation of&nbsp;
volcanic spiracles is displayed in the

00:16:20.480 --> 00:16:25.520
numerous archipelagos which border&nbsp;
the Eastern coast of Asia. Beginning

00:16:25.520 --> 00:16:30.000
from the peninsula of Kamchatka,&nbsp;
Japan, the Loo-choo, Philippines,

00:16:30.000 --> 00:16:37.600
and Moluccas form a scarcely interrupted linear&nbsp;
series of volcanic islands, amongst which many

00:16:37.600 --> 00:16:44.000
contain vents in habitual activity, and bearing&nbsp;
marks of recent and extremely violent eruptions.

00:16:48.480 --> 00:16:55.200
The Pacific Ring of Fire is home to&nbsp;
three-quarters of all the world's volcanoes,

00:16:55.200 --> 00:17:03.600
and 90% of the earthquakes on Earth take place&nbsp;
along this line. The Ring of Fire has given us

00:17:03.600 --> 00:17:11.920
some of the world's most iconic peaks, including&nbsp;
the elegant cone of Mount Fuji in Japan, and the

00:17:11.920 --> 00:17:19.840
violently explosive Mount St. Helens in the United&nbsp;
States, as well as the volcanoes of Mount Tambora

00:17:19.840 --> 00:17:29.360
and Krakatoa, whose 1815 and 1883 eruptions&nbsp;
were among the largest in recorded history.

00:17:35.600 --> 00:17:41.120
As it is dragged down into the depths&nbsp;
of the planet, the subducting Pacific

00:17:41.120 --> 00:17:47.600
Plate has also created some of the lowest&nbsp;
places on the Earth's surface, giving rise

00:17:47.600 --> 00:17:56.960
to a series of dramatic oceanic trenches. The&nbsp;
deepest of these, known as the Mariana Trench,

00:17:56.960 --> 00:18:05.760
reaches a sea depth of 11 kilometers, deeper than&nbsp;
Mount Everest is tall, and in the areas where the

00:18:05.760 --> 00:18:18.480
Ring of Fire passes through open ocean, it has&nbsp;
also created a huge number of volcanic islands.

00:18:18.480 --> 00:18:24.080
As volcanoes erupt under the sea,&nbsp;
magma spews out of the planet's

00:18:24.080 --> 00:18:32.720
mantle and cools on contact with cold&nbsp;
sea water into solid rock. Over time,

00:18:32.720 --> 00:18:40.000
whole islands form around these volcanoes,&nbsp;
and on these islands, the lives and deaths

00:18:40.000 --> 00:18:47.280
and dramas and tragedies of millions of human&nbsp;
lives have played out throughout history.

00:18:51.440 --> 00:18:55.840
Perhaps the region where the Pacific&nbsp;
Ring of Fire has had the most effect

00:18:55.840 --> 00:19:05.040
in sculpting the landscape is in the area&nbsp;
of what is today Malaysia and Indonesia.

00:19:05.040 --> 00:19:12.320
Here the Ring of Fire has lifted thousands&nbsp;
of islands large and small from the sea,

00:19:12.320 --> 00:19:17.360
and this archipelago has come to&nbsp;
be known by a name that in old

00:19:17.360 --> 00:19:28.960
Javanese meant simply the outer&nbsp;
lands . This name is Nusantara.

00:19:32.880 --> 00:19:41.040
The archipelago today called Nusantara lies&nbsp;
across an area of ocean wider than the continental

00:19:41.040 --> 00:19:50.800
United States, and laid over a map of Europe would&nbsp;
stretch from France as far east as Northern India.

00:19:51.600 --> 00:20:00.960
The archipelago is today home to more than 700&nbsp;
languages, six major religions, and more than 300

00:20:00.960 --> 00:20:09.120
ethnicities, and since modern humans first arrived&nbsp;
on these islands, they have given rise to a huge

00:20:09.120 --> 00:20:18.960
variety of different peoples. One Chinese Buddhist&nbsp;
monk named Faxian, who sailed to India in the 4th

00:20:18.960 --> 00:20:29.680
century in search of rare religious manuscripts,&nbsp;
describes the perils and beauties of this ocean.

00:20:29.680 --> 00:20:36.560
In this sea there are many pirates. The sea&nbsp;
is boundless in extent. It is impossible to

00:20:36.560 --> 00:20:44.640
know east or west, and one can only advance by&nbsp;
observing the sun, moon, or stars. If it is dark,

00:20:44.640 --> 00:20:51.680
rainy weather, you have to follow the wind in&nbsp;
perfect uncertainty. During the darkness of night,

00:20:51.680 --> 00:20:57.280
one only sees the great waves striking&nbsp;
each other and shining like fire,

00:20:57.280 --> 00:21:03.360
whilst shoals of sea monsters of&nbsp;
every description surround the ship.

00:21:05.120 --> 00:21:12.560
Today, the complex geography of this region&nbsp;
can prove confusing to many. As you listen,

00:21:12.560 --> 00:21:18.400
if you find yourself getting lost in the different&nbsp;
names of islands and seas referenced in this

00:21:18.400 --> 00:21:25.600
episode, it's worth remembering that you're&nbsp;
not alone. This is by far the densest region

00:21:25.600 --> 00:21:32.480
for islands anywhere in the world, and in fact,&nbsp;
the government of Indonesia itself has struggled

00:21:32.480 --> 00:21:39.440
to come up with a definitive answer to exactly&nbsp;
how many islands there are within its borders.

00:21:40.080 --> 00:21:49.680
One 2002 study using the satellite images&nbsp;
Landsat identified more than 18,000 islands,

00:21:49.680 --> 00:21:59.440
while another 2010 study gave a lower figure,&nbsp;
noting that only around 9,000 even have names.

00:21:59.440 --> 00:22:07.200
Nearly 90% of Indonesia's islands are tiny,&nbsp;
coming in at less than a kilometer squared.

00:22:08.080 --> 00:22:15.920
Other islands are massive. The archipelago&nbsp;
contains the world's second-largest island of

00:22:15.920 --> 00:22:26.320
Papua or New Guinea, the third-largest of Borneo,&nbsp;
and the sixth-largest of Sumatra. In land area,

00:22:26.320 --> 00:22:35.600
each of these is larger than Japan or the size&nbsp;
of the United Kingdom and France combined. But

00:22:35.600 --> 00:22:43.280
while this archipelago is home to some enormous&nbsp;
islands, these tend to be rocky and mountainous,

00:22:43.280 --> 00:22:51.200
their interiors covered in impenetrable&nbsp;
rainforest, and generally sparsely populated.

00:22:51.200 --> 00:22:58.720
It is the fifth-largest island in the archipelago&nbsp;
that has had perhaps the greatest impact of all,

00:22:58.720 --> 00:23:20.640
and this is the place where our story&nbsp;
begins, the long, narrow island of Java.

00:23:20.640 --> 00:23:28.480
Java is a volcanic island shaped like a&nbsp;
cigar running just south of and roughly

00:23:28.480 --> 00:23:36.800
parallel to the equator. It's little&nbsp;
over 100 kilometers wide north to south,

00:23:36.800 --> 00:23:41.040
but stretches for more than a&nbsp;
thousand kilometers east to west,

00:23:41.040 --> 00:23:48.720
and the center of the island is dominated&nbsp;
by a rocky spine of conical volcanoes.

00:23:49.840 --> 00:23:56.960
A medieval Javanese creation myth called&nbsp;
the Tantu Panggelaran describes how the

00:23:56.960 --> 00:24:08.640
island was once floating free in the ocean&nbsp;
before the gods decided to nail it down.

00:24:12.080 --> 00:24:20.320
The island of Java was unsteady, constantly&nbsp;
moving up and down, as it had no great mountains,

00:24:20.320 --> 00:24:28.000
not to mention human beings. For this reason,&nbsp;
Lord Jagat-Pramana arose and promptly performed

00:24:28.000 --> 00:24:35.360
yoga on the island of Java. The deity&nbsp;
stood facing east. He churned the water,

00:24:35.360 --> 00:24:41.200
and it turned into foam and turned&nbsp;
into mountains. For this reason,

00:24:41.200 --> 00:24:49.120
we have what is called the land above the clouds&nbsp;
now. Now the gods Brahma and Vishnu agreed,

00:24:49.120 --> 00:25:00.240
and made human beings. They kneaded lumps of clay&nbsp;
and formed them into humans of perfect beauty.

00:25:04.560 --> 00:25:12.000
Today, partly as a result of its rich volcanic&nbsp;
soil and its heavy rainfall and strong sun,

00:25:12.000 --> 00:25:21.840
Java is the world's most populous island. It is&nbsp;
home to more than 150 million people, despite its

00:25:21.840 --> 00:25:29.600
land area being only about the size of England,&nbsp;
making it one of the most densely populated places

00:25:29.600 --> 00:25:39.200
on Earth. More people live on Java than in the&nbsp;
whole of Russia, despite it being about 130 times

00:25:39.200 --> 00:25:48.880
smaller, and its capital of Jakarta recently took&nbsp;
the top spot as the world's most populated city.

00:25:50.160 --> 00:25:57.680
This reputation for abundance&nbsp;
goes back to ancient times.

00:25:57.680 --> 00:26:03.680
The Hindu epic poem the Ramayana,&nbsp;
written around two thousand years ago,

00:26:03.680 --> 00:26:13.760
already mentions Java as a place of wealth. The&nbsp;
island of Yava is decorated with seven kingdoms,

00:26:13.760 --> 00:26:26.160
gold and silver islands rich in gold mines, and a&nbsp;
cold mountain that touches the sky with its peak.

00:26:26.160 --> 00:26:34.080
Even in ancient times, the island had a&nbsp;
global reputation. It was prominent enough

00:26:34.080 --> 00:26:40.560
in the 2nd century that the Greek writer and&nbsp;
geographer Claudius Ptolemy included it in his

00:26:40.560 --> 00:26:51.680
work Geographia, right at the farthest edge of the&nbsp;
known world. He describes an island named Yabadiu,

00:26:51.680 --> 00:26:59.280
a name which he says means the island of barley&nbsp;
, possibly referring to the hardy grain millet,

00:26:59.280 --> 00:27:07.280
which has been an important foodstuff there&nbsp;
for centuries before the introduction of rice.

00:27:07.280 --> 00:27:10.800
As a result of its inclusion in Ptolemy's work,

00:27:10.800 --> 00:27:16.960
the island would also appear in a number&nbsp;
of medieval and early modern mappa mundi,

00:27:16.960 --> 00:27:26.240
or maps of the world, although few Europeans&nbsp;
could agree on where exactly the island was.

00:27:26.240 --> 00:27:33.520
In these maps, it usually appears somewhere&nbsp;
wedged between Africa and Asia, and always as

00:27:33.520 --> 00:27:41.520
far away as it was possible to get. But for&nbsp;
the people who lived on the island of Java,

00:27:41.520 --> 00:27:50.240
it was the very center of the world. According to&nbsp;
Javanese legend, it was where the gods had first

00:27:50.240 --> 00:28:01.440
created humanity and taught them the secrets of&nbsp;
civilization, as the Tantu Panggelaran recounts.

00:28:01.440 --> 00:28:09.760
Such were the instructions of Lord Guru to all&nbsp;
the gods; they descended to Java. The God Brahma

00:28:09.760 --> 00:28:17.760
became a blacksmith. The Earth served as his&nbsp;
anvil, water as his tongs, light as his fire, and

00:28:17.760 --> 00:28:27.440
wind as his bellows, and the sky as his hammer.&nbsp;
Lord Wiswakarma descended as a carpenter and made

00:28:27.440 --> 00:28:35.600
a house, and people followed him and made houses.&nbsp;
The Lord Ishwara descended and taught beneficial

00:28:35.600 --> 00:28:45.840
words, in particular about the ten kinds of&nbsp;
good conduct and the five fields of learning.

00:28:45.840 --> 00:28:53.120
Since it sits virtually on the equator, Java&nbsp;
experiences very little variation in sunlight

00:28:53.120 --> 00:29:01.600
throughout the year. In fact, its shortest day&nbsp;
is only 48 minutes shorter than its longest.

00:29:02.240 --> 00:29:09.280
The western side of the island also enjoys very&nbsp;
dependable monsoon rains from the Indian Ocean,

00:29:09.280 --> 00:29:16.880
which combine with the extremely fertile volcanic&nbsp;
soil so that farmers could grow all year round,

00:29:16.880 --> 00:29:26.400
and could often harvest three whole crops of&nbsp;
rice each year. The 14th-century Chinese source,

00:29:26.400 --> 00:29:37.120
the Song Shi or History of the Sung Dynasty,&nbsp;
describes the great abundance of this island.

00:29:37.120 --> 00:29:42.880
The country is flat and fit for agriculture.&nbsp;
Its products are rice, hemp, and peas,

00:29:42.880 --> 00:29:49.120
there being no wheat. Salt is obtained by boiling&nbsp;
sea water, and there is an abundance of fish,

00:29:49.120 --> 00:29:55.440
turtles, poultry, ducks, goats, and cattle,&nbsp;
which they kill for the purpose of eating.

00:29:55.440 --> 00:30:03.600
Their fruit are papaya, coconuts,&nbsp;
plantains, sugarcane, and taro.

00:30:03.600 --> 00:30:08.880
From about the fifth century on,&nbsp;
this vast agricultural potential

00:30:08.880 --> 00:30:15.360
would give birth to thriving societies, and&nbsp;
these would go on to construct elaborate,

00:30:15.360 --> 00:30:23.200
monumental architecture. For nearly the&nbsp;
next thousand years, most of these societies

00:30:23.200 --> 00:30:32.560
would arise in the west of Java. One king of&nbsp;
this time named Purna-varman left one of the

00:30:32.560 --> 00:30:41.680
earliest stone inscriptions carved into a large&nbsp;
boulder in the Ciaruteun River. The powerful,

00:30:41.680 --> 00:30:47.360
illustrious, and brave king, the famous&nbsp;
Purna-varman of the Taruma-nagara kingdom,

00:30:47.360 --> 00:30:54.320
whose print of the foot soles are&nbsp;
the same as those of God Vishnu.

00:30:58.000 --> 00:31:05.120
The inscription is accompanied by a&nbsp;
carved imprint of both of the king's feet,

00:31:05.120 --> 00:31:12.960
symbolically stamping his&nbsp;
ownership on the territory.

00:31:12.960 --> 00:31:21.120
By the sixth or seventh century, a power known&nbsp;
as Kaling seems to have risen up in Central Java,

00:31:21.120 --> 00:31:28.080
and a description of it was included in the&nbsp;
Chinese source known as the Xin Tangshu,

00:31:28.080 --> 00:31:34.080
or the New History of the Tang Dynasty.

00:31:34.080 --> 00:31:41.920
Kaling is also called Java. It is situated in the&nbsp;
southern ocean. The people make fortifications of

00:31:41.920 --> 00:31:49.280
wood, and even the largest houses are covered with&nbsp;
palm leaves. They have couches of ivory and mats

00:31:49.280 --> 00:31:57.200
of the outer skin of bamboo. The land produces&nbsp;
tortoiseshell, gold and silver, rhinoceros horns,

00:31:57.200 --> 00:32:05.600
and ivory. The country is very rich. They have&nbsp;
letters and are acquainted with astronomy.

00:32:07.200 --> 00:32:13.440
These early kingdoms seem to have met&nbsp;
with great success on the island of Java,

00:32:13.440 --> 00:32:17.760
but little has survived of&nbsp;
their records, their literature,

00:32:17.760 --> 00:32:31.280
or their architecture. But all that would change&nbsp;
with the founding of a kingdom known as Mataram.

00:32:48.880 --> 00:32:56.000
The name Mataram comes from the Sanskrit&nbsp;
word matr , meaning mother , and likely

00:32:56.000 --> 00:33:02.960
meant something like motherland .&nbsp;
This kingdom grew up not in the west,

00:33:02.960 --> 00:33:13.920
but in the highlands of Central Java, right in the&nbsp;
shadow of the great volcanoes Merbabu and Merapi.

00:33:13.920 --> 00:33:21.120
The people of Mataram were the first to truly&nbsp;
take advantage of this mountainous landscape,

00:33:21.120 --> 00:33:26.560
and cultivated the steep slopes of&nbsp;
this area using concentric terraces

00:33:26.560 --> 00:33:36.320
of rice fields fed and drained by intricate&nbsp;
systems of canals and stone-lined channels.

00:33:37.840 --> 00:33:45.440
This kingdom of the mountains was originally&nbsp;
Hindu, and worshipped primarily the god Shiva,

00:33:45.440 --> 00:33:51.360
the god of creation and destruction.

00:33:51.360 --> 00:33:58.000
Living as they did on a land constantly torn&nbsp;
apart and remade by the destructive power of

00:33:58.000 --> 00:34:05.600
the volcanoes, it's no mystery why this god&nbsp;
would have held sway in the kingdom of Mataram.

00:34:06.400 --> 00:34:12.400
But with the rise of a dynasty known&nbsp;
as the Shailendras in the 8th century,

00:34:12.400 --> 00:34:23.520
Mataram began to drift further&nbsp;
towards a new religion, Buddhism.

00:34:25.440 --> 00:34:32.560
In this period, Mataram would become what's known&nbsp;
to historians as an Indianized Hindu-Buddhist

00:34:32.560 --> 00:34:41.840
kingdom, with elements of both religions combined&nbsp;
into a syncretic whole. These religions became so

00:34:41.840 --> 00:34:48.800
intertwined over the centuries that followed that&nbsp;
the epic Tantu Panggelaran even recounts the story

00:34:48.800 --> 00:34:56.800
of one god who came down to earth and divided&nbsp;
himself into two holy sages, one Buddhist and one

00:34:56.800 --> 00:35:07.920
Hindu, so that he could spread both religions at&nbsp;
the same time. Let us tell about Mahampu Palyat.

00:35:07.920 --> 00:35:16.240
He returned to the island of Java. He divided&nbsp;
his body into two, and it became a Shivaite and a

00:35:16.240 --> 00:35:26.160
Buddhist. Off they went and arrived in Java. They&nbsp;
headed for Girah, and set up a monastery together.

00:35:26.160 --> 00:35:32.720
The kings of Java would even come to&nbsp;
be known by the title Batara Siwa Buda,

00:35:32.720 --> 00:35:43.440
meaning the divine lord Shiva Buddha , a royal&nbsp;
figure in whom the two sacred beings were fused.

00:35:43.440 --> 00:35:48.560
This was in general a period&nbsp;
of coexistence and unity,

00:35:48.560 --> 00:36:03.520
and it's in this era that some of Java's&nbsp;
most remarkable monuments would be built.

00:36:08.080 --> 00:36:17.600
The kings of the mountain kingdom of Mataram&nbsp;
were ambitious builders. Around the year 850,

00:36:17.600 --> 00:36:25.120
they completed construction of a great temple&nbsp;
to Shiva, the towering Prambanan temple, which

00:36:25.120 --> 00:36:35.360
still sends its eight sturdy spires up into the&nbsp;
skies of southern Java. It is the second-largest

00:36:35.360 --> 00:36:43.840
Hindu structure in Southeast Asia, behind only&nbsp;
Angkor Wat in Cambodia, and it left its mark not

00:36:43.840 --> 00:36:53.760
just on the landscape but in the name of the&nbsp;
year in which its construction was completed.

00:36:53.760 --> 00:37:00.560
By this time, the Javanese had adopted&nbsp;
a unique system of naming each year,

00:37:00.560 --> 00:37:10.080
giving them what are known as moon chronograms,&nbsp;
or in Javanese, chandra-sengkala. This system used

00:37:10.080 --> 00:37:20.400
the Indian Shaka calendar, which assigned numbers&nbsp;
to the years the way we do today, like 1066, 1939,

00:37:20.400 --> 00:37:30.880
or 2026. But the Javanese developed this Indian&nbsp;
calendar with their own poetic flourish. They

00:37:30.880 --> 00:37:39.840
gave each number from 0 to 10 an array of&nbsp;
possible symbols like the sun, moon, fire,

00:37:39.840 --> 00:37:47.040
and other elements which they could choose from to&nbsp;
give the year a name that seemed the most fitting.

00:37:47.840 --> 00:37:55.920
For example, the 18th-century Balinese chronicle&nbsp;
named the Babadgumi gives one year the name Gajah

00:37:55.920 --> 00:38:05.440
Api Anin Watu, or Elephant, Fire, Wind, Stone, due&nbsp;
to a particularly violent eruption of the volcano

00:38:05.440 --> 00:38:14.960
Mount Agung that took place in East Bali. Another&nbsp;
year in which a fierce battle took place was named

00:38:14.960 --> 00:38:23.680
Paksa Sikara Bah Bumi, or the Force of Violence&nbsp;
Moving Upon the Earth. Due to this system,

00:38:23.680 --> 00:38:29.440
we can sometimes get a sense from the names&nbsp;
that years were given of the overriding mood

00:38:29.440 --> 00:38:39.280
in the kingdom; sometimes a mood of joy and&nbsp;
celebration, at others of despair and sorrow.

00:38:40.880 --> 00:38:47.360
On the year that the kings of Mataram completed&nbsp;
the Prambanan temple, they were clearly proud

00:38:47.360 --> 00:38:54.240
of their achievement, and because of its eight&nbsp;
towering main shrines, they named the year in

00:38:54.240 --> 00:39:02.320
honor of it. In our calendar it was 856,&nbsp;
but in the Shaka calendar it was called

00:39:02.320 --> 00:39:12.560
Eight Mountains of the Holy Monks, as recorded&nbsp;
in a text known as the Shavghra Inscription.

00:39:12.560 --> 00:39:19.120
In the time of the Shaka year Eight Mountains of&nbsp;
the Holy Monks, the statue of the god was finished

00:39:19.120 --> 00:39:26.000
and inaugurated. After the Shiva sanctuary&nbsp;
had been completed in its divine splendor,

00:39:26.000 --> 00:39:31.520
the course of the river was changed&nbsp;
so that it rippled along the grounds.

00:39:31.520 --> 00:39:37.360
Then the grounds were inaugurated&nbsp;
as temple grounds with the gods.

00:39:39.120 --> 00:39:46.160
Other temples built by the kings of Mataram, such&nbsp;
as the sprawling temple at Sewu, are thought to

00:39:46.160 --> 00:39:53.120
have been Buddhist since they were built in&nbsp;
the form of a mandala, an important geometrical

00:39:53.120 --> 00:40:01.920
concept in Buddhism that is used to conceptualize&nbsp;
the shape of the universe. But chief among their

00:40:01.920 --> 00:40:11.760
works is the structure that would become what is&nbsp;
still the largest Buddhist temple in the world.

00:40:11.760 --> 00:40:17.680
It's thought that the Javanese of the&nbsp;
time called it Bhumi Shambhara Bhudhara,

00:40:17.680 --> 00:40:24.400
or the Mountain of Combined Virtues&nbsp;
of the Ten Stages of Buddha-hood.

00:40:24.400 --> 00:40:35.720
But today it is known by a name meaning&nbsp;
only the Great Buddha or Borobudur.

00:40:35.720 --> 00:40:41.360
Borobudur rises like a mountain from&nbsp;
the river plains right in the shadow

00:40:41.360 --> 00:40:51.520
of the great volcano Mount Merapi. This vast&nbsp;
complex consists of nine stacked platforms

00:40:51.520 --> 00:41:00.480
rising like a wedding cake up to a height of&nbsp;
35 meters, and topped by a central dome. One

00:41:00.480 --> 00:41:09.600
heritage worker, Gerard Bolla, describes&nbsp;
the appearance of this incredible temple.

00:41:09.600 --> 00:41:14.960
Borobudur, the mountain of the&nbsp;
virtues, is first of all a landscape,

00:41:14.960 --> 00:41:20.560
the landscape which over a thousand years ago&nbsp;
met the eyes of those who came on pilgrimage

00:41:20.560 --> 00:41:28.400
here to seek inner peace. It has hardly changed.&nbsp;
In the distance, two volcanoes, one ever menacing,

00:41:28.400 --> 00:41:35.920
glows red in the night. All around in the middle&nbsp;
distance, a ring of mountains. Built on this

00:41:35.920 --> 00:41:43.680
same volcanic stone, Borobudur emerges from a&nbsp;
delicate green background of tropical vegetation.

00:41:46.480 --> 00:41:54.000
The design of Borobudur, like the temple&nbsp;
at Sewu, is an enormous mandala visible

00:41:54.000 --> 00:42:02.480
from above. But this mandala covers the area of&nbsp;
two football pitches, and guides its pilgrims

00:42:02.480 --> 00:42:10.480
up through a series of concentric walkways and&nbsp;
stairways carved out of black volcanic stone.

00:42:11.280 --> 00:42:19.920
These are all lined with nearly 3,000 carved&nbsp;
friezes and five hundred Buddha statues created by

00:42:19.920 --> 00:42:28.800
the finest craftsmen the kingdom could find. The&nbsp;
archaeologist Professor Raden Soekmono, who was in

00:42:28.800 --> 00:42:39.440
charge of the project to restore the great temple&nbsp;
in modern times, describes its spiritual function.

00:42:39.440 --> 00:42:46.160
For the ardent Buddhist, it is the highest wisdom&nbsp;
that leads to the ultimate salvation, and for the

00:42:46.160 --> 00:42:53.680
technician, the highest degree of expertise that&nbsp;
leads to the appropriate fulfillment of his duty.

00:42:53.680 --> 00:43:05.920
In both cases, Borobudur is the embodiment of&nbsp;
such a deeply felt wish. It is a prayer in stone.

00:43:11.680 --> 00:43:18.080
The symbolism of this structure, shaped like&nbsp;
a mountain, would likely have been obvious

00:43:18.080 --> 00:43:27.440
to the early medieval Javanese. The gods had once&nbsp;
placed the mountains of Java to secure the island

00:43:27.440 --> 00:43:36.640
in place and bring stability to the land. Now&nbsp;
their king did the same thing, placing his own

00:43:36.640 --> 00:43:45.920
mountain on the landscape to act as a testament to&nbsp;
the lasting stability of his reign, and Borobudur

00:43:45.920 --> 00:43:55.840
was built to last. It was constructed entirely&nbsp;
without mortar or cement, carving its two million

00:43:55.840 --> 00:44:02.320
blocks of volcanic stone into intricate&nbsp;
interlocking shapes to hold them together.

00:44:02.960 --> 00:44:08.480
This design has meant that it has largely&nbsp;
survived the countless earthquakes that have

00:44:08.480 --> 00:44:17.120
taken place over the last millennium in one&nbsp;
of the world's most seismically active zones.

00:44:17.120 --> 00:44:23.840
As well as the shifting ground beneath their&nbsp;
feet, the builders also had the heavy rainfall

00:44:23.840 --> 00:44:32.240
of the Javanese monsoon to contend with. For this&nbsp;
reason, they equipped the site of Borobudur with

00:44:32.240 --> 00:44:40.080
more than a hundred spouts for drainage, each&nbsp;
carved in the shape of a giant or sea dragon,

00:44:40.080 --> 00:44:47.680
so that when it rained, water would have poured&nbsp;
from their mouths. But the most remarkable and

00:44:47.680 --> 00:44:56.240
haunting feature of this monument is the thousands&nbsp;
of reliefs carved all around its galleries.

00:45:03.360 --> 00:45:09.360
Many of these carvings show stories from&nbsp;
the lives of the Buddha ascending through

00:45:09.360 --> 00:45:17.600
kilometers of galleries, and in this way, the&nbsp;
monument is a story brought to life in stone.

00:45:18.640 --> 00:45:26.080
But alongside this religious narrative, others&nbsp;
show scenes of simple daily life for all the

00:45:26.080 --> 00:45:35.040
different levels of Javanese society. In these&nbsp;
carvings, we see people carrying woven baskets

00:45:35.040 --> 00:45:43.680
for winnowing and steaming rice, and stirring&nbsp;
milk rice in pots. We see people working in the

00:45:43.680 --> 00:45:50.480
fields and shopping in a bustling urban&nbsp;
marketplace, people carrying baskets of

00:45:50.480 --> 00:45:58.400
tropical fruit and nets full of fish, all of&nbsp;
their faces looking out from the stone, frozen

00:45:58.400 --> 00:46:08.400
in a moment that passed by more than a thousand&nbsp;
years ago. The Chinese History of Liang describes

00:46:08.400 --> 00:46:18.560
the dress and appearance of the people of Java,&nbsp;
which can be seen in the reliefs of Borobudur.

00:46:18.560 --> 00:46:22.960
Men and women have the upper part of the&nbsp;
body naked. Their hair hangs loosely down,

00:46:22.960 --> 00:46:27.840
and around their lower limbs they only&nbsp;
use a sarong of cotton. The king and the

00:46:27.840 --> 00:46:32.480
nobles have a thin, flowered cloth for&nbsp;
covering the upper part of their body.

00:46:32.480 --> 00:46:37.920
They wear a girdle of gold and golden&nbsp;
rings in their ears. Young girls cover

00:46:37.920 --> 00:46:44.320
themselves with a cloth of cotton&nbsp;
and wear an embroidered girdle.

00:46:44.320 --> 00:46:52.320
In these carvings, we also get a sense for courtly&nbsp;
life. We see the king coming and going on his

00:46:52.320 --> 00:47:01.680
elephant, his courtiers, and the palace women.&nbsp;
In some carvings, we see the king and his nobles

00:47:01.680 --> 00:47:12.480
enjoying a splendid feast known as a Mahamangsa.&nbsp;
One 12th-century Javanese epic poem known as

00:47:12.480 --> 00:47:24.720
the Bhomantaka, or Death of Bhoma, describes&nbsp;
the sumptuous sight of one of these feasts.

00:47:29.040 --> 00:47:37.840
The dishes were salted eggs, the pork neck cuts,&nbsp;
ayung-ayung, and pabekan, vegetables in hot sauce,

00:47:37.840 --> 00:47:44.160
lichin and chekah, red mince of tripe with its&nbsp;
various red salads, and sizzling dripping of

00:47:44.160 --> 00:47:51.120
turtle, and the boiled vegetables, langsuban&nbsp;
with marrow and tongue in rolls, mushrooms

00:47:51.120 --> 00:47:58.080
steamed in leaves, crisp flakes of jellyfish,&nbsp;
liver with rump accompanied by roasted lungs,

00:47:58.080 --> 00:48:07.040
breast bone, intestines on skewers with fat and&nbsp;
hot kebabs. The banana heart was sweet, and there

00:48:07.040 --> 00:48:14.400
was no lack of prawns, crabs, salted fish, and&nbsp;
muscles. Pickled meats, grilled meats, wuku and

00:48:14.400 --> 00:48:31.440
various skewered meats, doves, pupuk birds, and&nbsp;
wild pigeon made them clutch in all directions.

00:48:31.440 --> 00:48:39.760
Some of these 9th-century carvings even depict&nbsp;
recipes that are still enjoyed in Indonesia today,

00:48:39.760 --> 00:48:46.720
among them a dish called tumpeng, a tall&nbsp;
cone of rice surrounded by side dishes,

00:48:46.720 --> 00:48:52.160
which is still prepared for special occasions.

00:48:52.160 --> 00:48:56.960
But one particular feature of these&nbsp;
carvings has always captured the

00:48:56.960 --> 00:49:15.040
imagination of those who visit the ruins.&nbsp;
These are the so-called Borobudur ships.

00:49:15.040 --> 00:49:23.120
In among the scenes of daily life are carved&nbsp;
the shapes of five large seagoing vessels,

00:49:23.120 --> 00:49:29.600
graceful and high-sided, which show just&nbsp;
how accomplished the Javanese had become

00:49:29.600 --> 00:49:39.040
as shipbuilders and sailors. And so, it's no&nbsp;
surprise that it was around this time that the

00:49:39.040 --> 00:49:49.840
people of Java began to gain a reputation&nbsp;
around the world as traders and pirates.

00:49:49.840 --> 00:49:58.160
The Javanese of the Mataram period sailed in&nbsp;
large ships that were often built from teak.

00:49:58.880 --> 00:50:06.800
This kind of wood was prized for its strength and&nbsp;
its high oil content, which gives it a natural

00:50:06.800 --> 00:50:15.920
water resistance even without varnishing, and&nbsp;
gives it a distinct rich aroma like fine leather.

00:50:15.920 --> 00:50:24.480
These ships were built without nails, using only&nbsp;
interlocking joints and wooden dowels, and with

00:50:24.480 --> 00:50:34.800
ingenious arrangements of sails. The 3rd-century&nbsp;
Chinese writer Wan Chen, in his work Nanzhou Yiwu

00:50:34.800 --> 00:50:45.360
Zhi, or Strange Things of the Southern Lands,&nbsp;
describes the sight of these majestic vessels.

00:50:45.360 --> 00:50:53.760
The people sometimes rig as many as four sails,&nbsp;
which they carry in a row from bow to stern.

00:50:53.760 --> 00:51:01.600
The four sails do not face directly forward, but&nbsp;
are set obliquely, and so arranged that they can

00:51:01.600 --> 00:51:08.480
all be fixed in the same direction to receive&nbsp;
the wind and to spill it. If it is violent,

00:51:08.480 --> 00:51:14.560
the sailors diminish or augment the surface&nbsp;
of the sails according to the conditions.

00:51:15.120 --> 00:51:20.720
Thus, these ships sail without avoiding&nbsp;
strong winds and dashing waves,

00:51:20.720 --> 00:51:26.560
by the aid of which they can make great speed.

00:51:26.560 --> 00:51:35.040
To Wan Chen and other Chinese writers, these&nbsp;
Javanese ships were so remarkable because China

00:51:35.040 --> 00:51:44.000
itself would not develop large oceangoing vessels&nbsp;
until around the 9th century, or 600 years later.

00:51:45.200 --> 00:51:52.160
In fact, the Chinese held the ships of these&nbsp;
islanders in such reverence that no foreign

00:51:52.160 --> 00:51:59.360
craft were permitted to sail within range&nbsp;
of the main port of Guangdong or Canton,

00:51:59.360 --> 00:52:08.560
as the 16th-century Portuguese&nbsp;
diplomat Tom Pires recounts.

00:52:08.560 --> 00:52:13.360
They say that the Chinese made this law&nbsp;
about not being able to go to Canton for

00:52:13.360 --> 00:52:18.640
fear of the Javanese and Malays, for it is&nbsp;
certain that one of these people's ships

00:52:18.640 --> 00:52:26.480
would rout twenty Chinese ships. Such&nbsp;
is their fear of Malays and Javanese.

00:52:28.640 --> 00:52:37.280
This fear appears to have been warranted. The&nbsp;
inscriptions of the Cham people of Vietnam and

00:52:37.280 --> 00:52:45.040
the Khmer in Cambodia frequently reference&nbsp;
the arrival of fearsome seagoing raiders,

00:52:45.040 --> 00:52:51.760
who are thought to have sailed&nbsp;
from islands like Sumatra and Java.

00:52:51.760 --> 00:53:04.160
One inscription in the Po Nagar temple in&nbsp;
Vietnam describes one such raid in the year 774.

00:53:04.160 --> 00:53:09.680
Ferocious, pitiless, dark-skinned men&nbsp;
born in other countries, whose food

00:53:09.680 --> 00:53:14.320
was more horrible than corpses,&nbsp;
and who were vicious and furious,

00:53:14.320 --> 00:53:20.560
came in ships, took away the temple&nbsp;
statue, and set fire to the temple.

00:53:23.280 --> 00:53:30.480
The Javanese were in part able to become&nbsp;
such successful sailors due to the extremely

00:53:30.480 --> 00:53:38.880
predictable wind patterns that take place in this&nbsp;
part of the world. During the rainy season from

00:53:38.880 --> 00:53:46.480
December to March, the winds blow in over Java&nbsp;
from the Asian mainland in the northwest, picking

00:53:46.480 --> 00:53:54.000
up moisture over the South China Sea and Pacific&nbsp;
Ocean, and bringing the monsoon. But during the

00:53:54.000 --> 00:54:01.280
dry season from April to September, the winds&nbsp;
reverse direction and blow from the southeast

00:54:01.280 --> 00:54:10.480
and the deserts of Australia, bringing dry air&nbsp;
and a lower probability of rain. This meant you

00:54:10.480 --> 00:54:20.640
could travel whichever direction you wanted, so&nbsp;
long as you wisely chose your season to sail.

00:54:20.640 --> 00:54:23.680
As their confidence grew on the waves,

00:54:23.680 --> 00:54:35.040
we have evidence that the Javanese also&nbsp;
undertook some even more ambitious voyages.

00:54:40.080 --> 00:54:49.200
One Italian explorer who arrived in Java around&nbsp;
1505, named Ludovico Di Varthema of Bologna,

00:54:49.200 --> 00:54:56.880
sailed to the island of Java, and was amazed to&nbsp;
see the captain of the ship navigate even when

00:54:56.880 --> 00:55:03.680
the North Star was no longer visible. When the&nbsp;
chartered vessel was supplied with provisions,

00:55:03.680 --> 00:55:09.600
we took our way towards the beautiful island&nbsp;
called Java, at which we arrived in five days,

00:55:09.600 --> 00:55:14.960
sailing towards the south. My companion asked the&nbsp;
Christians, "Now that we have lost the North Star,

00:55:14.960 --> 00:55:20.080
how does he steer us? Is there any other&nbsp;
North Star than this by which we steer?"

00:55:20.080 --> 00:55:25.200
The Christians asked the captain of the ship this&nbsp;
same thing, and he showed us four or five stars,

00:55:25.200 --> 00:55:30.080
among which there was one which he said&nbsp;
was the opposite to our North Star.

00:55:31.840 --> 00:55:38.000
This was the so-called Southern Cross,&nbsp;
the distinctive pattern of stars that

00:55:38.000 --> 00:55:45.280
could be seen in the southern hemisphere in the&nbsp;
direction of the South Pole. The Europeans were

00:55:45.280 --> 00:55:52.160
amazed at this discovery, but the captain&nbsp;
then told him that the Javanese had sailed

00:55:52.160 --> 00:56:02.880
into the south following these stars, and&nbsp;
had there come upon an icy and frozen land.

00:56:02.880 --> 00:56:07.440
He also told us that on the other side of&nbsp;
the said island towards the south there

00:56:07.440 --> 00:56:12.720
are some other races who navigate by the&nbsp;
said four or five stars opposite to ours,

00:56:12.720 --> 00:56:18.160
and moreover, they gave us to understand that&nbsp;
beyond the said island, the day does not last

00:56:18.160 --> 00:56:26.320
more than four hours, and that there it was&nbsp;
colder than in any other part of the world.

00:56:26.320 --> 00:56:33.600
If this is true, it means that the Javanese&nbsp;
had already sailed to and around Australia,

00:56:33.600 --> 00:56:41.680
and set out into the Antarctic Ocean.&nbsp;
But tantalizingly, we have only this

00:56:41.680 --> 00:56:50.320
secondhand story to go by, and the rest&nbsp;
will have to be left to our imaginations.

00:56:50.320 --> 00:56:56.560
But as the centuries of the first millennium&nbsp;
went on, there is a great deal more evidence

00:56:56.560 --> 00:57:04.720
that the people of Java were making&nbsp;
their mark in even further lands.

00:57:08.720 --> 00:57:13.840
7,000 kilometers away on&nbsp;
the shores of East Africa,

00:57:13.840 --> 00:57:20.400
the 10th-century Persian writer Buzurg&nbsp;
Ibn Shahriyar gives an account of a series

00:57:20.400 --> 00:57:28.960
of raids undertaken by a people from a&nbsp;
faraway land that he calls the Wakwak.

00:57:30.000 --> 00:57:38.880
The Wakwak set out with a thousand ships to launch&nbsp;
a determined attack upon the town of Kanbaloh, but

00:57:38.880 --> 00:57:46.080
they were unable to capture it, for the town was&nbsp;
strongly fortified and was surrounded by an arm of

00:57:46.080 --> 00:57:55.680
the sea, in the midst of which Kanbaloh rose like&nbsp;
a fortress. When the people of the African coast

00:57:55.680 --> 00:58:03.520
eventually came to terms with the attackers, they&nbsp;
learned more about why they had come and how far

00:58:03.520 --> 00:58:11.360
they had traveled. The people of that country&nbsp;
demanded why they had come to Kanbaloh rather

00:58:11.360 --> 00:58:18.000
than to any other place. They replied that it was&nbsp;
because the country possessed merchandise of value

00:58:18.000 --> 00:58:25.760
in the Wakwak country and in China, such as ivory,&nbsp;
tortoiseshell, panther skins, and ambergris,

00:58:25.760 --> 00:58:32.000
and also because they wanted to obtain some&nbsp;
of the Zenj people, who, being strong men,

00:58:32.000 --> 00:58:39.600
are able to stand heavy labor. Their voyage,&nbsp;
they said, had lasted a year. If the story told

00:58:39.600 --> 00:58:49.680
by these people be true, this proves that the&nbsp;
Wakwak islands are situated opposite to China.

00:58:49.680 --> 00:58:55.440
The location of these Wakwak&nbsp;
islands has long been debated.

00:58:55.440 --> 00:59:00.960
One clue can be found in the&nbsp;
Persian chronicler Ibn Khordadbeh,

00:59:00.960 --> 00:59:10.800
whose description has made many historians believe&nbsp;
that they may have been the islands of Indonesia.

00:59:10.800 --> 00:59:16.000
East of China are the lands of Wakwak, which are&nbsp;
so rich in gold that the inhabitants make the

00:59:16.000 --> 00:59:21.840
chains for their dogs and the collars for their&nbsp;
monkeys of this metal. They manufacture tunics

00:59:21.840 --> 00:59:32.880
woven with gold. Excellent ebony wood is found&nbsp;
there. Gold and ebony are exported from Wakwak.

00:59:32.880 --> 00:59:40.160
This detail of dogs wearing collars made of&nbsp;
gold was also mentioned by early European

00:59:40.160 --> 00:59:50.160
explorers who visited Java, leading many to&nbsp;
conclude that the Wakwak really were Javanese.

00:59:50.160 --> 00:59:56.000
If this story does relate an attack on&nbsp;
Eastern Africa by the people of Java,

00:59:56.000 --> 01:00:01.280
then it shows the extent of their&nbsp;
shipbuilding and seafaring prowess,

01:00:01.280 --> 01:00:06.320
and that they had become a formidable&nbsp;
threat for all the peoples who lived

01:00:06.320 --> 01:00:13.920
around the Indian Ocean. From these raids,&nbsp;
they would have brought back gold, ivory,

01:00:13.920 --> 01:00:36.160
and slaves to Java. But in other places,&nbsp;
they didn't simply raid and sail away.

01:00:36.160 --> 01:00:42.320
In the large island of Madagascar, just off the&nbsp;
coast of Eastern Africa, the people's language

01:00:42.320 --> 01:00:50.400
has always puzzled linguists. The Madagascan&nbsp;
language of Malagasy shares little in common

01:00:50.400 --> 01:00:55.600
with those of the African mainland, and&nbsp;
it was only when the grammar and syntax

01:00:55.600 --> 01:01:01.840
were compared with Indonesian languages&nbsp;
that a remarkable similarity was noticed.

01:01:02.400 --> 01:01:09.120
Although separated by the entire Indian Ocean,&nbsp;
the language of Malagasy shares a grammatical

01:01:09.120 --> 01:01:15.840
structure with several Indonesian languages like&nbsp;
Javanese, and they even have a number of different

01:01:15.840 --> 01:01:26.400
words in common. Just one example is the word for&nbsp;
island; nosee in Malagasy and nusa in Javanese.

01:01:27.360 --> 01:01:33.840
In fact, the closest living relative of Malagasy&nbsp;
is thought to be a language called Maanyan,

01:01:33.840 --> 01:01:41.760
which originated on the south coast of Borneo.&nbsp;
The most modern analysis of mitochondrial DNA in

01:01:41.760 --> 01:01:47.600
Madagascar has confirmed this connection,&nbsp;
and shows that the people there can trace

01:01:47.600 --> 01:01:54.960
their lineage back to about thirty mothers who&nbsp;
arrived on the island from what is now Indonesia.

01:01:56.080 --> 01:02:02.000
This suggests a single boatload of settlers&nbsp;
who had traveled across one of the world's

01:02:02.000 --> 01:02:11.360
widest oceans to find a new home. They also&nbsp;
brought plants and crops with them. Botanists

01:02:11.360 --> 01:02:20.480
estimate that a full 7% of the plant life on&nbsp;
Madagascar today originated in Indonesia. These

01:02:20.480 --> 01:02:27.520
include bananas, plantains, taro, and yams,&nbsp;
and all of these were already well established

01:02:27.520 --> 01:02:37.600
there by the medieval period. In 1602, another&nbsp;
Portuguese explorer, Diogo do Couto, described

01:02:37.600 --> 01:02:46.320
these far-reaching journeys of the Javanese. The&nbsp;
Javanese are all men very experienced in the art

01:02:46.320 --> 01:02:51.920
of navigation, to the point that they claim&nbsp;
to be the most ancient of all. It is certain

01:02:51.920 --> 01:02:57.200
that they formally navigated to Africa's Cape&nbsp;
of Good Hope, and were in communication with

01:02:57.200 --> 01:03:02.880
the east coast of the island of Madagascar,&nbsp;
where there are many brown and Javanese-like

01:03:02.880 --> 01:03:11.680
natives who say they are descended from them. The&nbsp;
Javanese of this period navigated using the stars,

01:03:11.680 --> 01:03:19.200
but also magnetic compasses and maps written&nbsp;
on the bark of the paper mulberry tree.

01:03:21.520 --> 01:03:28.160
In 1512, only twenty years after Columbus&nbsp;
had first reached the Americas, and twelve

01:03:28.160 --> 01:03:34.960
years after the first Europeans set foot in&nbsp;
Brazil, a Portuguese conquistador fighting

01:03:34.960 --> 01:03:41.520
on the Malay Peninsula captured a&nbsp;
sea chart from a Javanese sailor.

01:03:41.520 --> 01:03:47.920
He was amazed to find that it had already been&nbsp;
updated with all the new discoveries that had

01:03:47.920 --> 01:03:58.800
been made on the other side of the world, as he&nbsp;
wrote in one letter to King Manuel I of Portugal.

01:03:58.800 --> 01:04:04.720
We found a large map of a Javanese pilot&nbsp;
containing the Cape of Good Hope, Portugal,

01:04:04.720 --> 01:04:10.560
and the land of Brazil, the Red Sea and&nbsp;
the Sea of Persia, the Clove Islands,

01:04:10.560 --> 01:04:16.560
the navigation of the Chinese and Japanese, and&nbsp;
how the kingdoms border on each other. It seems

01:04:16.560 --> 01:04:21.280
to me, sir, that this was the best thing I&nbsp;
have ever seen, and your highness will be

01:04:21.280 --> 01:04:31.200
very pleased to see it. In 2003, a modern team&nbsp;
was able to pilot a replica of an old Javanese

01:04:31.200 --> 01:04:39.360
sailing ship from Indonesia to Madagascar and&nbsp;
around the Cape of Good Hope in South Africa,

01:04:39.360 --> 01:04:48.160
thus proving that this journey was possible&nbsp;
with the technology available at the time.

01:04:48.160 --> 01:04:55.120
But despite its remarkable achievements in&nbsp;
architecture and its voyages of discovery,

01:04:55.120 --> 01:05:00.560
the Shailendra dynasty of Mataram&nbsp;
would eventually decline, and all

01:05:00.560 --> 01:05:08.720
of its grand monuments would decline with it.

01:05:13.760 --> 01:05:21.520
The reasons for the fall of Mataram are unclear,&nbsp;
since no true chronicles have survived from

01:05:21.520 --> 01:05:28.880
this time. But the kingdom's decline may have&nbsp;
been related to a series of dramatic volcanic

01:05:28.880 --> 01:05:36.240
eruptions that occurred sometime in&nbsp;
the 10th and early 11th centuries.

01:05:38.080 --> 01:05:46.080
The people of Java have always lived with a&nbsp;
particular paradox, that the immense productivity

01:05:46.080 --> 01:05:55.440
of their land also comes from its most destructive&nbsp;
force. Many landscapes that experience a monsoon

01:05:55.440 --> 01:06:03.840
season as heavy as Java can suffer from poor soil&nbsp;
quality, as the seasonal rains wash away topsoil

01:06:03.840 --> 01:06:13.600
and leech nutrients away. But in much of Java,&nbsp;
the hundreds of active volcanoes counteract this

01:06:13.600 --> 01:06:21.360
effect. Due to the island's particular geology,&nbsp;
its volcanoes are especially beneficial for the

01:06:21.360 --> 01:06:28.960
soil, filling it with minerals like magnesium,&nbsp;
potassium, and phosphorus that plants thrive on,

01:06:28.960 --> 01:06:37.760
all blasted out of the depths of the earth&nbsp;
itself. Volcanic soil is also uniquely porous,

01:06:37.760 --> 01:06:44.640
and retains moisture exceptionally well, while&nbsp;
allowing air circulation for plant roots.

01:06:46.080 --> 01:06:54.720
But of course, a volcano is a violent phenomenon.&nbsp;
When they erupt, they scorch the earth with

01:06:54.720 --> 01:07:03.760
pyroclastic flows, spew lava, and rain huge&nbsp;
boulders down from the sky across a large area.

01:07:04.560 --> 01:07:08.960
This poses an obvious problem, that the areas of

01:07:08.960 --> 01:07:17.360
the greatest agricultural productivity on&nbsp;
the island were also the most dangerous.

01:07:17.360 --> 01:07:23.440
This meant that while the volcanoes overall&nbsp;
effect was positive, a year in which a great

01:07:23.440 --> 01:07:30.160
eruption took place could wipe out a good&nbsp;
proportion of any kingdom's food supplies.

01:07:30.880 --> 01:07:35.680
And of all these volcanoes, by&nbsp;
far the most active was the one

01:07:35.680 --> 01:07:53.920
that the Javanese call by a name which&nbsp;
means the Mountain of Fire, or Merapi.

01:07:53.920 --> 01:08:00.560
Rising to nearly 3,000 meters or 10,000&nbsp;
feet, Mount Merapi is the most active

01:08:00.560 --> 01:08:05.520
volcano in the whole of Indonesia, and&nbsp;
is considered one of the most active

01:08:05.520 --> 01:08:12.720
and dangerous in the world. It has erupted&nbsp;
regularly for at least the last 500 years,

01:08:12.720 --> 01:08:20.720
with small eruptions occurring every two to three&nbsp;
years, and larger ones every ten to fifteen.

01:08:20.720 --> 01:08:30.960
Then every few hundred years, the mountain&nbsp;
explodes in a truly catastrophic fashion.

01:08:30.960 --> 01:08:38.240
The Javanese Chronicle, the Babad Tanah&nbsp;
Jawi, records the devastation caused by one

01:08:38.240 --> 01:08:47.760
eruption of the great Mount Merapi that took&nbsp;
place in the 16th century. Then Mount Merapi

01:08:47.760 --> 01:08:56.400
erupted with fire. It made a terrifying thundering&nbsp;
noise. A rain of ash fell. A volcanic mudflow ran

01:08:56.400 --> 01:09:05.200
down the Ompak River. Big rocks rose from the&nbsp;
flow and landed on the land. When the piles of

01:09:05.200 --> 01:09:12.640
wood on the Gunung Kidul mountains were set on&nbsp;
fire, the mountains became a blaze of fire. The

01:09:12.640 --> 01:09:24.240
Gong Kyai Bicak was beaten ceaselessly. All tried&nbsp;
to save their lives and ran in all directions.

01:09:24.240 --> 01:09:30.560
Added to this obvious physical destruction,&nbsp;
the Javanese also held the volcanoes in

01:09:30.560 --> 01:09:37.120
great spiritual reverence, and viewed&nbsp;
eruptions as signs and portents from the

01:09:37.120 --> 01:09:44.320
gods. After the deaths of great kings,&nbsp;
Javanese chronicles often describe the

01:09:44.320 --> 01:09:52.640
mountains rumbling in mourning. At the death&nbsp;
of his majesty, the wailing in the palace was

01:09:52.640 --> 01:10:01.360
as loud as thunder. Mount Merapi rumbled,&nbsp;
joining in with the sound of a rainstorm.

01:10:02.960 --> 01:10:09.840
It was believed that the eruptions, earthquakes,&nbsp;
and various rumblings of the land were not just

01:10:09.840 --> 01:10:17.040
seismic events, but actually one of the primary&nbsp;
ways that the gods weighed in on the affairs

01:10:17.040 --> 01:10:24.720
of humanity. For this reason, their timing&nbsp;
could have real life-or-death consequences.

01:10:25.600 --> 01:10:36.000
One chronicle recounts the story of a lucky&nbsp;
prisoner held by a 17th-century Javanese king.

01:10:36.000 --> 01:10:42.080
The king wanted to kill the prisoner Raden&nbsp;
Mas Surya-ku-suma, but this did not happen,

01:10:42.080 --> 01:10:48.400
because every time they wanted to kill him,&nbsp;
Mount Merapi flared up with a terrifying noise.

01:10:48.400 --> 01:11:02.640
In the end, the man was set free and promoted&nbsp;
and given the lordship of 1,000 rice fields.

01:11:02.640 --> 01:11:09.360
A particularly devastating eruption would not&nbsp;
only cause immense economic damage to the kingdom;

01:11:09.360 --> 01:11:14.800
it could also be considered a clear sign&nbsp;
that the gods were angry with the current

01:11:14.800 --> 01:11:22.000
ruler. For any king who didn't have a firm&nbsp;
grasp on power, this could be a devastating

01:11:22.000 --> 01:11:30.800
blow. From some inscriptions left in Java, we&nbsp;
know that as the 9th century came to an end,

01:11:30.800 --> 01:11:41.120
the kingdom of Mataram descended into civil&nbsp;
war, with three kings ruling in only two years.

01:11:41.120 --> 01:11:47.920
When the dust of this civil war settled&nbsp;
in the year 929, a king named Rake Hino

01:11:47.920 --> 01:11:55.760
pu Sindok took to the throne. But curiously,&nbsp;
it seems the kingdom of Mataram had by this

01:11:55.760 --> 01:12:04.080
point moved. All of the inscriptions issued&nbsp;
by this king would be written in East Java,

01:12:04.080 --> 01:12:10.080
far away from its original heartlands&nbsp;
around the volcano Mount Merapi.

01:12:10.800 --> 01:12:17.440
It seems that around the beginning of the 10th&nbsp;
century, the entire Mataram kingdom simply upped

01:12:17.440 --> 01:12:26.960
sticks and fled its original wealthy province,&nbsp;
leaving its towering temples and cities abandoned,

01:12:26.960 --> 01:12:32.640
and what's perhaps most significant is&nbsp;
that no other power seems to have swept

01:12:32.640 --> 01:12:39.360
in to the empty lands. This suggests that&nbsp;
some kind of cataclysm took place there.

01:12:47.280 --> 01:12:53.520
Archaeological evidence shows that the early&nbsp;
10th-century layer of human occupation at the

01:12:53.520 --> 01:13:01.040
great temple of Borobudur, for instance, is&nbsp;
sealed by up to half a meter of volcanic ash,

01:13:01.040 --> 01:13:10.480
suggesting a major eruption. The peoples of this&nbsp;
land were no strangers to erupting volcanoes,

01:13:10.480 --> 01:13:16.720
so we can imagine that it must have taken&nbsp;
one of particular violence or a series of

01:13:16.720 --> 01:13:23.600
eruptions taking place in frighteningly quick&nbsp;
succession to cause the wholesale abandonment

01:13:23.600 --> 01:13:30.720
of this kingdom and its reestablishment&nbsp;
hundreds of kilometers to the east.

01:13:31.920 --> 01:13:43.120
One inscription known as the Pucangan Stone&nbsp;
describes a kind of disaster sweeping the land.

01:13:43.120 --> 01:13:47.440
In the time of the great catastrophe&nbsp;
that overwhelmed the island of Java in

01:13:47.440 --> 01:13:52.080
the Saka year 928, when King Wurawari came forth

01:13:52.080 --> 01:13:57.680
from Lwaram, the whole of Java seemed&nbsp;
transformed into a single raging sea.

01:13:58.240 --> 01:14:07.280
Many of the foremost men of the realm perished.&nbsp;
Chief among the dead was the divine king.

01:14:07.280 --> 01:14:12.640
Unfortunately, the exact dating of&nbsp;
volcanic eruptions is difficult,

01:14:12.640 --> 01:14:18.880
and it's hard to say whether this was&nbsp;
truly the cause of Mataram's downfall.

01:14:18.880 --> 01:14:25.280
But whether from volcanic causes or not,&nbsp;
it's clear that for one reason or another,

01:14:25.280 --> 01:14:33.200
life had become unlivable for the&nbsp;
people of Mataram in Central Java.

01:14:33.200 --> 01:14:39.760
The kingdom eventually reached a level of&nbsp;
stability in its new home in East Java,

01:14:39.760 --> 01:14:46.480
away from the destruction it had faced,&nbsp;
but its confidence in itself and in the

01:14:46.480 --> 01:14:53.360
gods approval of its rulers had been&nbsp;
fatally reduced. Within a century,

01:14:53.360 --> 01:15:00.480
the remains of Mataram was facing destruction,&nbsp;
now at the hands of human enemies.

01:15:01.120 --> 01:15:08.160
An invasion by a neighboring king wiped out&nbsp;
what was left of it, and the kingdom of Mataram

01:15:08.160 --> 01:15:16.480
disappeared, living on only in the stone carvings&nbsp;
left lining the walls of its great temples,

01:15:16.480 --> 01:15:27.280
thousands of moments of everyday life now&nbsp;
sealed beneath a thick layer of volcanic ash.

01:15:27.280 --> 01:15:51.120
After the fall of Mataram, a number of successor&nbsp;
kingdoms would rise up. In the 11th century,

01:15:51.120 --> 01:15:57.440
much of Java was reunited again under&nbsp;
the rule of a king named Airlangga,

01:15:57.440 --> 01:16:05.840
whose name meant he who leapt over the water&nbsp;
. He was born on the small island of Bali,

01:16:05.840 --> 01:16:13.920
and crossed the sea to Java as a young&nbsp;
man. The Pucangan Stone describes how this

01:16:13.920 --> 01:16:22.720
king was spared from the destruction that&nbsp;
swept the land during the fall of Mataram.

01:16:22.720 --> 01:16:27.760
At that time, his majesty was only sixteen&nbsp;
years old, still too young to have mastered

01:16:27.760 --> 01:16:32.560
the art of war. Yet, because he was&nbsp;
the very manifestation of Vishnu,

01:16:32.560 --> 01:16:39.440
all the gods protected him from being&nbsp;
swept away by the great destruction.

01:16:39.440 --> 01:16:45.120
Airlangga would rule successfully for&nbsp;
several decades, but when he died,

01:16:45.120 --> 01:16:52.080
the land divided again. The kingdom that&nbsp;
would truly give birth to Java's golden

01:16:52.080 --> 01:17:03.200
era was named Singhasari. Singhasari derived its&nbsp;
name from the Sanskrit word simha , meaning lion,

01:17:03.200 --> 01:17:16.080
and this lion kingdom was founded&nbsp;
by a king named Ken Angrok.

01:17:16.080 --> 01:17:24.960
Ken Angrok's name means the man who overturned&nbsp;
everything , and he was a revolutionary force on

01:17:24.960 --> 01:17:33.520
Java. In the centuries since he lived,&nbsp;
the legend of Ken Angrok has grown to

01:17:33.520 --> 01:17:41.440
mythic proportions, and he has inspired&nbsp;
theater, song, and poetry in Indonesia.

01:17:42.480 --> 01:17:51.280
The story of his life is largely told in a&nbsp;
fragmentary chronicle known as the Pararaton,

01:17:51.280 --> 01:18:01.200
or the Book of Kings, written sometime later in&nbsp;
the 16th century. According to this chronicle,

01:18:01.200 --> 01:18:07.840
Ken Angrok was actually the son of the&nbsp;
Hindu god Brahma, who appeared to his

01:18:07.840 --> 01:18:22.960
mother, a poor woman named Ken Endok, and&nbsp;
informed her of her immaculate conception.

01:18:22.960 --> 01:18:31.040
The god Brahma descended there and lay with Ken&nbsp;
Endok. This meeting took place in the fields of

01:18:31.040 --> 01:18:39.280
Lalaten. The god made a vow with the woman.&nbsp;
You must not go to your husband again. If you

01:18:39.280 --> 01:18:48.400
lie with him, he will die, and my child will&nbsp;
be corrupted. That child is named Ken Angrok,

01:18:48.400 --> 01:18:56.320
and he will one day rule the land&nbsp;
of Java. With that, Brahma vanished.

01:19:01.840 --> 01:19:11.920
The woman held true to her word, and soon Ken&nbsp;
Angrok was born. But the family was extremely

01:19:11.920 --> 01:19:20.400
poor and couldn't provide for the little boy.&nbsp;
His parents abandoned him in a grave, but he was

01:19:20.400 --> 01:19:34.800
found by a common thief named Lembong, who was&nbsp;
perhaps plundering the graveyard for valuables.

01:19:34.800 --> 01:19:42.640
When her time was full, she gave birth&nbsp;
to a boy. Ken Endok abandoned him in a

01:19:42.640 --> 01:19:48.720
burial ground for children. Not long&nbsp;
after, a thief named Lembong wandered

01:19:48.720 --> 01:19:54.400
into the same place. There he saw&nbsp;
something glowing. Drawn to it,

01:19:54.400 --> 01:20:01.360
he heard the sound of a child crying. When he&nbsp;
came closer, he saw that the light was coming

01:20:01.360 --> 01:20:11.840
from the child himself. Lembong picked him up,&nbsp;
carried him home, and raised him as his own son.

01:20:18.240 --> 01:20:25.760
Brought up by this thief, Ken Angrok would&nbsp;
learn all the tricks of his trade until he

01:20:25.760 --> 01:20:35.520
was the most accomplished thief in Java. But one&nbsp;
day he met an Indian holy man who identified him

01:20:35.520 --> 01:20:46.960
as the son of Brahma and an embodiment of the god&nbsp;
Vishnu. This holy man convinced Ken Angrok to give

01:20:46.960 --> 01:20:56.800
up his sinful life. As a reward, Ken Angrok&nbsp;
gained a magical weapon, a kind of Javanese

01:20:56.800 --> 01:21:07.280
knife known as a keris. These are long daggers&nbsp;
often created with a distinctive rippled blade,

01:21:07.280 --> 01:21:15.280
and they were worn by Javanese men as a sign&nbsp;
of status, as a talisman against various evils,

01:21:15.280 --> 01:21:22.720
and of course as a practical sidearm for&nbsp;
self-defense. One Chinese visitor to Java

01:21:22.720 --> 01:21:32.640
at the start of the 15th century named Ma Huan&nbsp;
writes about the importance of these blades.

01:21:32.640 --> 01:21:39.520
From little boys of three years to old men of&nbsp;
a hundred years, they all have these knives,

01:21:39.520 --> 01:21:46.320
which are all made of steel, with most intricate&nbsp;
patterns drawn in very delicate lines. For the

01:21:46.320 --> 01:21:51.680
handles, they use gold or rhinoceros&nbsp;
horn or elephants teeth engraved with

01:21:51.680 --> 01:22:03.120
representations of human forms or devils faces,&nbsp;
the craftsmanship being very fine and skilful.

01:22:03.120 --> 01:22:09.680
With this magical sword by his side,&nbsp;
Ken Angrok rose to prominence in Java,

01:22:09.680 --> 01:22:12.960
and eventually, through the overwhelming force of

01:22:12.960 --> 01:22:26.160
his various virtues, became its&nbsp;
king, as the Pararaton describes.

01:22:26.160 --> 01:22:34.800
Not long afterwards, Ken Angrok was acclaimed&nbsp;
as king of Tumapel. His realm was called

01:22:34.800 --> 01:22:42.080
Singhasari. His enthronement was witnessed by&nbsp;
learned men of both the Shaivite and Buddhist

01:22:42.080 --> 01:22:49.040
traditions of Kediri. Those who had once&nbsp;
shown Ken Angrok kindness in his days of

01:22:49.040 --> 01:22:58.000
hardship were summoned, granted protection,&nbsp;
and generously rewarded for their loyalty.

01:23:01.200 --> 01:23:10.480
Not everyone easily accepted the rule of this&nbsp;
commoner. In the nearby rival kingdom of Kediri,

01:23:10.480 --> 01:23:18.880
the king was named Dandhang Gendis, and he&nbsp;
refused to accept Ken Angrok as his king.

01:23:19.920 --> 01:23:26.240
Dandhang Gendis gathered his armies&nbsp;
and marched to fight Ken Angrok,

01:23:26.240 --> 01:23:37.920
but he was no match for the&nbsp;
divine power of the god made man.

01:23:37.920 --> 01:23:44.720
He fell because he was attacked on all sides&nbsp;
by the forces of Singhasari, who came on like

01:23:44.720 --> 01:23:52.320
a flood pouring down from the mountains. The army&nbsp;
of Kediri scattered like a swarm of bees, fleeing

01:23:52.320 --> 01:24:01.600
in panic, abandoning the field like startled&nbsp;
goats, casting aside their parasols. King Dandhang

01:24:01.600 --> 01:24:08.320
Gendis withdrew from the battle and fled into the&nbsp;
realm of the gods, rising up into the air with his

01:24:08.320 --> 01:24:15.600
horse, his grooms, his parasol-bearers, and those&nbsp;
who carried his drinking vessels and his mats,

01:24:15.600 --> 01:24:33.840
all of them ascending into the sky. Thus,&nbsp;
Kediri was utterly defeated by Ken Angrok.

01:24:37.040 --> 01:24:44.480
Needless to say, this section of the Pararaton&nbsp;
Chronicle is considered to be largely mythical,

01:24:44.480 --> 01:24:52.400
and reflects the propaganda of this&nbsp;
king's new regime. But this story

01:24:52.400 --> 01:24:59.280
would be repeated across the towns and&nbsp;
villages of Java by trained storytellers,

01:24:59.280 --> 01:25:08.480
who together wove a new national myth around&nbsp;
the figure of Ken Angrok. One of the most

01:25:08.480 --> 01:25:18.560
important mediums in which this story was&nbsp;
told was that of the Javanese shadow puppets.

01:25:22.160 --> 01:25:30.080
The exact origin of Javanese shadow puppetry&nbsp;
is a mystery, but it's clear from a number of

01:25:30.080 --> 01:25:36.800
oblique references in Javanese literature that&nbsp;
it was already a well-established practice by

01:25:36.800 --> 01:25:45.760
at least the 10th century. This form of&nbsp;
shadow theater is known as wayang. Wayang

01:25:45.760 --> 01:25:53.120
in Javanese means shadow or ghost , but&nbsp;
it can also mean imagination , and these

01:25:53.120 --> 01:26:00.400
plays would retell stories from the Hindu&nbsp;
epics like the Mahabharat, local Javanese

01:26:00.400 --> 01:26:09.200
stories such as how rice came to the earth,&nbsp;
and the stories of their kings and ancestors.

01:26:10.640 --> 01:26:15.200
The Chinese visitor to Java at&nbsp;
the beginning of the 15th century,

01:26:15.200 --> 01:26:22.160
Ma Huan, describes witnessing these performances.

01:26:22.160 --> 01:26:27.600
Each time the performer unrolls and exposes a&nbsp;
section of the picture, he thrusts it forward

01:26:27.600 --> 01:26:32.560
toward the audience, and speaking with a loud&nbsp;
voice in the foreign language, he explains

01:26:32.560 --> 01:26:38.800
the derivation of this section. The crowd sits&nbsp;
around and listens to him, sometimes laughing,

01:26:38.800 --> 01:26:46.960
sometimes crying, exactly as if the narrator&nbsp;
were reciting one of our popular romances.

01:26:46.960 --> 01:26:53.680
These shadow plays would typically&nbsp;
start at nightfall and go on until dawn,

01:26:53.680 --> 01:26:59.680
and would be conducted behind a screen of&nbsp;
white cotton fabric using a lamp burning

01:26:59.680 --> 01:27:07.600
with coconut oil, usually cast in metal&nbsp;
in the form of the sacred bird garuda.

01:27:08.640 --> 01:27:16.320
The puppets were typically made of goat hide&nbsp;
or buffalo skin, mounted on bamboo canes,

01:27:16.320 --> 01:27:23.920
and placed in a trunk of banana palm lying&nbsp;
behind the screen. The puppets had jointed

01:27:23.920 --> 01:27:31.280
limbs and were punctured all over with intricate&nbsp;
patterns of holes that allowed the light through

01:27:31.280 --> 01:27:39.520
so that their shadows could then be cast onto a&nbsp;
screen. These puppet shows were often accompanied

01:27:39.520 --> 01:27:46.720
by music. In small village performances, this&nbsp;
could simply be the drumming of the puppeteer

01:27:46.720 --> 01:27:53.360
on the box where the puppets were kept. But&nbsp;
in the royal court, a puppeteer would perform

01:27:53.360 --> 01:28:03.280
alongside a full orchestra of musicians known as a&nbsp;
gamelan, with a whole host of fifteen instruments.

01:28:04.400 --> 01:28:12.640
These night-long plays would unfold according to&nbsp;
strict traditions. In the hours between sunset

01:28:12.640 --> 01:28:19.280
and midnight, the puppeteer introduced the&nbsp;
characters and set up the conflict, and then

01:28:19.280 --> 01:28:26.800
at the stroke of midnight, the drama burst out&nbsp;
into a frenzied clash between good and evil.

01:28:27.440 --> 01:28:36.560
The Portuguese traveller and apothecary Tom Pires,&nbsp;
who visited Java around 1513, wrote about seeing

01:28:36.560 --> 01:28:45.600
these forms of theater and shadow puppetry.&nbsp;
The land of Java is a land of performers and

01:28:45.600 --> 01:28:52.880
masks of various Javanese kinds, and both men&nbsp;
and women do thus. They have entertainments of

01:28:52.880 --> 01:29:00.160
dancing and stories. They mime. They wear actors&nbsp;
dresses and all their clothes. They are certainly

01:29:00.160 --> 01:29:07.120
graceful. They have music of bells. The sound of&nbsp;
all of them playing together is like an organ.

01:29:07.120 --> 01:29:14.080
These actors show a thousand graces like these&nbsp;
by day and night. At night, they make shadows

01:29:14.080 --> 01:29:24.000
of various shapes. Over time, the performance of&nbsp;
these shadow plays took on a spiritual meaning.

01:29:25.200 --> 01:29:32.640
The puppeteer, or dalang, was thought to become&nbsp;
possessed by the power of the gods as he worked,

01:29:32.640 --> 01:29:39.680
and in the performance, all the holy elements&nbsp;
were represented; the banana palm representing the

01:29:39.680 --> 01:29:47.680
earth, the cloth screen the sky, the fire in the&nbsp;
coconut oil lamp, and of course, the interplay of

01:29:47.680 --> 01:29:58.320
light and darkness, good and evil, as the stories&nbsp;
of gods and demons played out across the screen.

01:29:58.320 --> 01:30:02.480
These performances were&nbsp;
considered to have real power.

01:30:02.480 --> 01:30:09.600
Some such as the Brata Yudha, or a Javanese&nbsp;
version of the Indian epic the Mahabharat, were

01:30:09.600 --> 01:30:20.000
considered too potent to ever perform in their&nbsp;
entirety, as Thomas Stamford Raffles records.

01:30:20.000 --> 01:30:24.160
Although the wayang may represent&nbsp;
any portion of the Brata Yudha,

01:30:24.160 --> 01:30:28.160
there is a prevalent superstition that&nbsp;
a great war will be the inevitable

01:30:28.160 --> 01:30:32.880
consequence of performing at one&nbsp;
sitting the whole of this poem.

01:30:32.880 --> 01:30:38.480
It is gravely asserted that a chief of Kendal&nbsp;
tried this experiment about fifty years ago,

01:30:38.480 --> 01:30:45.760
but that the performance was no sooner completed&nbsp;
than his country was laid to waste and destroyed.

01:30:47.360 --> 01:30:53.200
Other of these shadow plays were performed&nbsp;
at auspicious times in order to bring

01:30:53.200 --> 01:31:00.720
about certain effects, with stories of the&nbsp;
goddess of rice told during the harvests,

01:31:00.720 --> 01:31:08.400
and the great myths told during times of&nbsp;
trouble and strife. One particular shadow

01:31:08.400 --> 01:31:15.520
play was reserved solely for times when a plague&nbsp;
of mice or rats had descended on a village,

01:31:15.520 --> 01:31:29.520
and its goal was to convince the rodents to&nbsp;
depart and dwell in the kingdom of vermin instead.

01:31:29.520 --> 01:31:44.000
Over the next centuries, the story of Ken Angrok,&nbsp;
the first king of Singhasari, would be told again

01:31:44.000 --> 01:31:51.920
and again in these kinds of performances, and&nbsp;
would take on more and more fantastical elements;

01:31:51.920 --> 01:31:57.600
and for that reason, it's impossible to say&nbsp;
definitively which parts may have a grain of

01:31:57.600 --> 01:32:05.120
truth to them. But it's likely that some&nbsp;
elements are true, that Ken Angrok was a

01:32:05.120 --> 01:32:13.360
common man of uncertain birth and lineage, that&nbsp;
he had been a thief or brigand of some kind,

01:32:13.360 --> 01:32:20.960
that he rose to power in the kingdom in a violent&nbsp;
upheaval, and crushed the nearby kingdom of Kediri

01:32:20.960 --> 01:32:30.080
in open battle. But after he comes to the throne,&nbsp;
the tone of the chronicle shifts and becomes more

01:32:30.080 --> 01:32:38.080
grounded in historical reality. Many events&nbsp;
recorded during his reign are dated, and so

01:32:38.080 --> 01:32:49.120
this second half of the chronicle is considered to&nbsp;
contain more historically verifiable information.

01:33:00.960 --> 01:33:05.920
What we know for sure is that over&nbsp;
the course of the 13th century,

01:33:05.920 --> 01:33:12.960
Ken Angrok's new kingdom of Singhasari&nbsp;
would gradually expand its power, wealth,

01:33:12.960 --> 01:33:21.360
and prestige. But it was under the reign of his&nbsp;
great grandson, the fifth king of Singhasari,

01:33:21.360 --> 01:33:30.160
that it would grow into a true empire. His name&nbsp;
was King Kertanegara, and during his time on the

01:33:30.160 --> 01:33:37.760
throne, he would order expeditions to extend the&nbsp;
kingdom's influence not just over most of Java,

01:33:37.760 --> 01:33:45.120
but across the sea to the large island of Borneo&nbsp;
to the north, the small island of Bali to the

01:33:45.120 --> 01:33:53.840
east, and even conduct a successful invasion of&nbsp;
Java's sister island of Sumatra in the northwest.

01:33:54.480 --> 01:34:00.800
Now the kingdom of Singhasari had&nbsp;
grown to be a true seagoing empire,

01:34:00.800 --> 01:34:06.560
and it controlled virtually all of&nbsp;
the region's most crucial waterways,

01:34:06.560 --> 01:34:22.160
and the most important of these was the narrow&nbsp;
sea corridor known as the Strait of Malacca.

01:34:22.160 --> 01:34:29.680
The Malay Peninsula is a long body of land&nbsp;
that stretches southeast from the southern

01:34:29.680 --> 01:34:38.640
edge of the Asian continent where Thailand meets&nbsp;
Malaysia, and just off its southernmost point and

01:34:38.640 --> 01:34:47.200
following the line of the continental shelf is&nbsp;
the island of Sumatra and then Java. Together,

01:34:47.200 --> 01:34:54.320
these form a sea wall of more than 3,000&nbsp;
kilometers that stands between the Indian

01:34:54.320 --> 01:35:03.840
Ocean and the South Pacific and South China&nbsp;
Sea. In this massive wall, there are only

01:35:03.840 --> 01:35:11.920
two narrow gates, the southerly one known as&nbsp;
the Straits of Sunda, and the northern one,

01:35:11.920 --> 01:35:21.840
the Straits of Malacca. The American sailor and&nbsp;
writer Herman Melville, writing in the mid 19th

01:35:21.840 --> 01:35:31.520
century, describes the sight of these straits.&nbsp;
Those narrow Straits of Sunda stand midway in

01:35:31.520 --> 01:35:40.560
that vast rampart of islands like the central&nbsp;
gateway opening into some vast, walled empire.

01:35:40.560 --> 01:35:49.600
The inexhaustible wealth of spices and silks and&nbsp;
jewels and gold and ivory, with which the thousand

01:35:49.600 --> 01:36:01.760
islands of that oriental sea are enriched, are&nbsp;
thus guarded from the all-grasping Western world.

01:36:01.760 --> 01:36:09.040
For much of history, it has been the wider and&nbsp;
deeper Strait of Malacca that has been used most

01:36:09.040 --> 01:36:17.840
heavily. In fact, today it is the busiest shipping&nbsp;
channel in the world, seeing nearly 100,000 ships

01:36:17.840 --> 01:36:27.280
pass through it each year. Around a quarter of all&nbsp;
global trade passes through this narrow waterway,

01:36:27.280 --> 01:36:36.800
where the massive cities of Singapore and Kuala&nbsp;
Lumpur are today located. And in the 13th century,

01:36:36.800 --> 01:36:46.880
during the time of the Singhasari kingdom, this&nbsp;
waterway was no less crucial or lucrative. Around

01:36:46.880 --> 01:36:55.840
the year 1515, the Portuguese diplomat Tom Pires&nbsp;
passed through the Yemeni port of Aden on the Red

01:36:55.840 --> 01:37:03.520
Sea, and wrote that goods transported thousands&nbsp;
of miles from Malacca featured prominently in

01:37:03.520 --> 01:37:11.440
the city's markets, where merchants would carry&nbsp;
them on to Egypt, and from there into Europe.

01:37:12.320 --> 01:37:18.000
The merchants from Cairo bring the merchandise&nbsp;
which comes from Italy and Greece and Damascus

01:37:18.000 --> 01:37:25.680
to Aden, while the merchants of Aden take back&nbsp;
with them all the products of Malacca; cloves,

01:37:25.680 --> 01:37:35.760
nutmeg, mace, sandalwood, brazilwood, silks,&nbsp;
seed pearls, musk, porcelain, and other things.

01:37:35.760 --> 01:37:45.120
Control of the narrow waterway of the Malacca&nbsp;
strait gave King Kertanegara immense power,

01:37:45.120 --> 01:37:52.400
despite the relatively small size of the&nbsp;
kingdom of Singhasari. Now, anyone wishing

01:37:52.400 --> 01:38:00.400
to sail between China and India or the rest of the&nbsp;
world either did so with Kertanegara's permission,

01:38:00.400 --> 01:38:08.160
or had to take a detour of at least 2,000&nbsp;
kilometers through rough and stormy seas around

01:38:08.160 --> 01:38:19.360
this island wall. As a result of this control, the&nbsp;
power and wealth of Singhasari grew dramatically.

01:38:19.360 --> 01:38:26.000
But there were much larger empires in the region&nbsp;
who had a strong interest in making sure that

01:38:26.000 --> 01:38:34.320
the flow of goods by sea could pass through these&nbsp;
narrow straits. And as the 13th century came to an

01:38:34.320 --> 01:38:45.760
end, the kingdom of Singhasari came into direct&nbsp;
conflict with one of these much larger powers.

01:39:07.440 --> 01:39:12.400
In the year 1289, King Kertanegara of the Javanese

01:39:12.400 --> 01:39:18.240
kingdom of Singhasari received a&nbsp;
group of ambassadors at his court.

01:39:19.040 --> 01:39:26.720
They came finely dressed, haughty and proud,&nbsp;
and said that they had come to speak on behalf

01:39:26.720 --> 01:39:42.560
of the world's most powerful man. That was&nbsp;
the Mongol emperor of China, Kublai Khan.

01:39:44.400 --> 01:39:51.360
As we saw in our 19th episode, from their&nbsp;
humble beginnings on the Mongolian steppe,

01:39:51.360 --> 01:39:59.360
over the last century, the Mongols had embarked on&nbsp;
a remarkable series of campaigns to conquer all of

01:39:59.360 --> 01:40:08.400
China, Persia, and the Middle East, along with&nbsp;
much of what is today Russia and Eastern Europe.

01:40:08.400 --> 01:40:15.600
Founded by the warlord Chinggis Khan, the Mongol&nbsp;
Empire was the largest contiguous land empire

01:40:15.600 --> 01:40:22.480
in history, and combined the speed and power&nbsp;
of Mongol horsemen with the craftsmanship of

01:40:22.480 --> 01:40:33.440
Persia and the ingenuity and manpower of China. By&nbsp;
this point, the enormous Mongol Empire had split

01:40:33.440 --> 01:40:40.800
into four separate domains, but the largest of&nbsp;
these was still the world's biggest empire at the

01:40:40.800 --> 01:40:52.800
time. That was the one ruled from China by the&nbsp;
grandson of Chinggis Khan, the emperor Kublai.

01:40:52.800 --> 01:41:03.680
This hybrid Chinese-Mongolian power was known as&nbsp;
the Yuan Dynasty. But by this time, the empire

01:41:03.680 --> 01:41:12.240
had begun to struggle. The emperor Kublai was&nbsp;
now well into his seventies, and his domain

01:41:12.240 --> 01:41:21.200
was plagued by political and economic problems.&nbsp;
The Mongol Empire had been built on conquest,

01:41:21.200 --> 01:41:29.200
but now its terrifying engine seemed to have&nbsp;
run out of fuel. Eight years earlier, Kublai's

01:41:29.200 --> 01:41:37.280
forces had for the second time failed to capture a&nbsp;
foothold on the islands of Japan. His invasions of

01:41:37.280 --> 01:41:46.000
Burma and Vietnam had also fizzled out, with the&nbsp;
Mongol forces struggling in the tropical heat.

01:41:46.000 --> 01:41:53.200
The aging Mongol emperor desperately needed a&nbsp;
victory, and he had been increasingly casting

01:41:53.200 --> 01:42:01.920
his gaze southwards, across the sea, and&nbsp;
towards the wealthy island of Java. The

01:42:01.920 --> 01:42:11.200
Chinese chronicle the Yuanshi, or History of&nbsp;
Yuan, describes this strange and faraway land.

01:42:12.320 --> 01:42:18.240
The customs and products of this land are not&nbsp;
much known, but as a rule, the barbarian countries

01:42:18.240 --> 01:42:24.720
over the sea produce many rare and valuable&nbsp;
things which fetch a high price in China.

01:42:24.720 --> 01:42:32.400
The inhabitants are strange, and their nature&nbsp;
and speech are not understood by the Chinese.

01:42:32.400 --> 01:42:40.240
By that time, Java's King Kertanegara had&nbsp;
been defying Kublai Khan for nearly a decade.

01:42:41.200 --> 01:42:48.480
Two previous ambassadorial visits to his court had&nbsp;
been sent home without any tribute for the Mongol

01:42:48.480 --> 01:42:57.280
Khan, and the defiance of this small island&nbsp;
was beginning to make the empire look weak.

01:42:59.600 --> 01:43:07.120
For this reason, in 1289, Kublai sent&nbsp;
a new party of envoys to Java to meet

01:43:07.120 --> 01:43:14.000
with the king and give him a final&nbsp;
ultimatum. They demanded that King

01:43:14.000 --> 01:43:20.880
Kertanegara swear an oath of fealty to the&nbsp;
Mongol emperor, pay him a hefty tribute,

01:43:20.880 --> 01:43:28.320
and send members of his royal family to the&nbsp;
Mongol court in China to act as hostages.

01:43:28.320 --> 01:43:37.600
But when these ambassadors arrived, they would&nbsp;
not find King Kertanegara in an agreeable mood.

01:43:39.600 --> 01:43:44.960
The Javanese of the time considered&nbsp;
repeated requests of this kind to be

01:43:44.960 --> 01:43:55.200
exceedingly rude. Tom Pires writes about&nbsp;
the proud nature of the Javanese people.

01:43:55.200 --> 01:44:00.000
The Javanese are men who, if they&nbsp;
write once and do not get a reply,

01:44:00.000 --> 01:44:06.080
do not write again, although it may be of&nbsp;
great importance to them. The Javanese are

01:44:06.080 --> 01:44:16.320
daring men and determined to die. They&nbsp;
are gamblers and play for high stakes.

01:44:16.320 --> 01:44:24.480
King Kertanegara was no different, and he would&nbsp;
now make a tremendous gamble for the highest

01:44:24.480 --> 01:44:33.200
stakes possible. He ordered that the Mongol&nbsp;
ambassadors be punished. By some accounts,

01:44:33.200 --> 01:44:38.960
their faces were branded with&nbsp;
hot irons and their ears cut off,

01:44:38.960 --> 01:44:44.160
a punishment typically reserved&nbsp;
for bandits and pirates.

01:44:44.880 --> 01:44:51.920
We can only imagine the rage of the Mongol&nbsp;
emperor Kublai Khan upon hearing this news

01:44:51.920 --> 01:45:04.320
and seeing the mutilated faces of his ambassadors.&nbsp;
The History of Yuan describes what happened next.

01:45:04.320 --> 01:45:09.760
In the second month of the 29th year of the&nbsp;
Yuan Dynasty, the emperor issued an order

01:45:09.760 --> 01:45:14.800
to the governor of Fujian, directing&nbsp;
him to send an army to subdue Java,

01:45:14.800 --> 01:45:20.080
to collect soldiers to the number of&nbsp;
20,000, to send out a thousand ships,

01:45:20.080 --> 01:45:25.760
and to equip them with provisions for&nbsp;
a year, and with 40,000 bars of silver.

01:45:26.320 --> 01:45:32.160
The emperor said to them, "When you arrive at&nbsp;
Java, you must clearly proclaim that they have

01:45:32.160 --> 01:45:41.840
lately cut the face of the imperial envoy, Meng&nbsp;
Chi, and that you have come to punish them."

01:45:41.840 --> 01:45:51.760
This expedition set sail three years later in the&nbsp;
year 1292, but it was to be a difficult voyage.

01:45:52.400 --> 01:46:00.640
The History of Yuan describes the troubles&nbsp;
they encountered on the Java Sea. The wind

01:46:00.640 --> 01:46:06.640
was strong and the sea very rough, so that the&nbsp;
ships rolled heavily and the soldiers could

01:46:06.640 --> 01:46:12.960
not eat for many days. The forces to attack&nbsp;
Java were driven by the wind to one island,

01:46:12.960 --> 01:46:17.360
and many ships were wrecked.&nbsp;
Over a hundred men who were

01:46:17.360 --> 01:46:22.960
ill from the long beating about in the storm were&nbsp;
unable to leave with the rest of the expedition,

01:46:22.960 --> 01:46:31.040
and were left there. And today, the Chinese&nbsp;
live mixed up with the native families.

01:46:31.040 --> 01:46:36.160
Despite their troubles on the voyage,&nbsp;
the Mongol forces eventually arrived on

01:46:36.160 --> 01:46:44.240
the shores of Java in the next year of 1293,&nbsp;
ready to punish King Kertanegara for defying

01:46:44.240 --> 01:46:52.160
the empire and mistreating its ambassadors.&nbsp;
But when they arrived, they would find their

01:46:52.160 --> 01:46:59.920
enthusiasm for revenge had been somewhat&nbsp;
deflated. In fact, in the preceding year,

01:46:59.920 --> 01:47:19.760
the defiant King Kertanegara had been overthrown&nbsp;
and killed by a usurper named Jaya-Katong.

01:47:19.760 --> 01:47:28.320
Jaya-Katong was the ruler of a city called Kediri,&nbsp;
which controlled a province covering much of

01:47:28.320 --> 01:47:37.600
Central Java. For a long time, Kediri had actually&nbsp;
been the predominant power on the island, but

01:47:37.600 --> 01:47:44.960
after its king, Dandan Gendhis, had been defeated&nbsp;
in battle by the hero Ken Angrok, Kediri had been

01:47:44.960 --> 01:47:55.680
eclipsed by the rise of Singhasari. Now Kediri was&nbsp;
a vassal of King Kertanegara, but it was by far

01:47:55.680 --> 01:48:04.960
the most powerful of his subjects, and as a result&nbsp;
never sat comfortably within his kingdom. The epic

01:48:04.960 --> 01:48:17.040
poem the Deshawarnana describes the new ruler of&nbsp;
Kediri, Jaya-Katong, as cunning and malevolent.

01:48:22.480 --> 01:48:28.400
There was a vassal king, a villain known&nbsp;
by the name of Jaya-Katong, who there,

01:48:28.400 --> 01:48:43.760
in the land of Kediri, desired to replace the king&nbsp;
and was outstandingly clever at laying schemes.

01:48:43.760 --> 01:48:50.720
At the time, the majority of King&nbsp;
Kertanegara's forces were away from Java,

01:48:50.720 --> 01:48:56.320
fighting to increase his territory&nbsp;
across the sea in Sumatra. And so,

01:48:56.320 --> 01:49:06.160
Jaya-Katong saw his chance. According to the&nbsp;
Pararaton or Book of Kings, Jaya-Katong was

01:49:06.160 --> 01:49:18.800
encouraged to seize the opportunity by one of&nbsp;
his allies, who sent him a thinly-veiled message.

01:49:22.640 --> 01:49:28.560
The secret letter said, "My&nbsp;
lord, I respectfully tell you,

01:49:28.560 --> 01:49:36.240
if his majesty has any consideration and desire&nbsp;
to hunt, now his majesty has the chance to so

01:49:36.240 --> 01:49:43.760
hunt. It will be the best and the most opportune&nbsp;
moment. There is no danger. There are no tigers,

01:49:43.760 --> 01:50:00.480
wild buffaloes, nor snakes, or even thorns.&nbsp;
There is only one tiger, but it has no teeth.

01:50:00.480 --> 01:50:10.000
Urged on by this coded message, King Jaya-Katong&nbsp;
rose in rebellion. His city of Kediri was not far

01:50:10.000 --> 01:50:17.360
from the capital of Singhasari, but it was on&nbsp;
the other side of a number of towering volcanic

01:50:17.360 --> 01:50:27.200
cones, mounts Kelud, Arjuna, and Kawi. To&nbsp;
strike at Singhasari and depose the king,

01:50:27.200 --> 01:50:32.560
he could either march north through the&nbsp;
easier territory of the northern plains,

01:50:32.560 --> 01:50:42.560
or south through the rugged hill country riven&nbsp;
by gorges and ravines. The cunning Jaya-Katong

01:50:42.560 --> 01:50:53.760
considered his options and came up with a plan.&nbsp;
In 1292, alarming news reached King Kertanegara's

01:50:53.760 --> 01:51:00.960
court. Messengers would have burst into his&nbsp;
throne room and breathlessly told him that an

01:51:00.960 --> 01:51:08.400
army had been seen rampaging through the farmland&nbsp;
to the north, flying the distinctive white and red

01:51:08.400 --> 01:51:17.440
banners of Kediri. They had taken the toothless&nbsp;
tiger King Kertanegara completely by surprise.

01:51:23.040 --> 01:51:28.560
Now, King Jaya-Katong set out to&nbsp;
attack. His army, coming from the north,

01:51:28.560 --> 01:51:39.760
consisted of bad people. They were full of flags&nbsp;
and sounds. The area to the north was devastated.

01:51:43.120 --> 01:51:48.160
Dismayed at the destruction, the king&nbsp;
appointed one of his son-in-laws to

01:51:48.160 --> 01:51:57.280
lead what forces he had remaining&nbsp;
in Java, a man named Raden Wijaya.

01:51:57.280 --> 01:52:02.160
This young man marched north with&nbsp;
the remains of the royal army,

01:52:02.160 --> 01:52:10.960
and met Jaya-Katong's rampaging forces in battle,&nbsp;
as one Javanese inscription carved at a place

01:52:10.960 --> 01:52:22.320
called Kudadu describes. When King Jaya-Katong's&nbsp;
troops reached the village of Jasun-Wungkal,

01:52:22.320 --> 01:52:30.880
King Kertanegara sent his son-in-law, Raden&nbsp;
Wijaya, to fight the enemy. After the battle,

01:52:30.880 --> 01:52:42.080
the enemy fled while suffering a great&nbsp;
loss. Seemingly they disappeared forever.

01:52:42.080 --> 01:52:48.480
Raden Wijaya had succeeded in the north&nbsp;
and defended his father-in-law's kingdom,

01:52:48.480 --> 01:52:59.120
which he was set to one day inherit. But now&nbsp;
the true strategy of Jaya-Katong was revealed.

01:52:59.120 --> 01:53:05.280
While this loud northern force had been burning&nbsp;
and sacking their way through the north of the

01:53:05.280 --> 01:53:13.200
kingdom, a much larger second force had also&nbsp;
advanced in the south. This army moved much

01:53:13.200 --> 01:53:25.520
more stealthily through the ravines and valleys of&nbsp;
the central highlands, as the Pararaton describes.

01:53:27.280 --> 01:53:33.520
Then a large army descended from Kediri,&nbsp;
coming from the banks of the Aksa River,

01:53:33.520 --> 01:53:39.280
heading towards Lawor. They were not&nbsp;
allowed to carry flags or make any

01:53:39.280 --> 01:53:50.880
noise. Upon arrival at Sidabawana,&nbsp;
they headed straight for Singhasari.

01:53:55.600 --> 01:53:59.360
This force would go completely unnoticed, and they

01:53:59.360 --> 01:54:08.640
apparently arrived at the kingdom's capital&nbsp;
of Kutaraja while King Kertanegara was drunk.

01:54:10.640 --> 01:54:16.960
Drinking in Java was a serious business,&nbsp;
and a large number of alcoholic beverages

01:54:16.960 --> 01:54:24.720
were consumed for pleasure, especially on&nbsp;
special occasions. One Javanese poem known

01:54:24.720 --> 01:54:34.160
as the Sumanasaantaka describes the drinks&nbsp;
available at an idealized wedding. The drinks

01:54:34.160 --> 01:54:41.600
poured continuously from rock crystal pitches&nbsp;
like fountains of palm syrup. Sugar cane, wine,

01:54:41.600 --> 01:54:53.680
and rice beer were drunk one after the other like&nbsp;
holy water and mead welling up from glass vessels.

01:54:53.680 --> 01:55:00.240
But the influence of tantric Buddhism meant&nbsp;
that drinking was more than just for fun.

01:55:00.240 --> 01:55:08.960
In fact, it was at times a spiritual practice, and&nbsp;
even a royal duty. The king was required to take

01:55:08.960 --> 01:55:15.440
part in ceremonies known as chakra-puja,&nbsp;
which involved the drawing of geometric

01:55:15.440 --> 01:55:22.480
designs on the earth with colored powder,&nbsp;
after which the ritual could take place.

01:55:23.440 --> 01:55:32.560
The historian Miranda Shaw describes&nbsp;
the unfolding of one of these rituals.

01:55:32.560 --> 01:55:39.040
An elaborate array of offerings and foods are laid&nbsp;
out. The participants don special insignia-like

01:55:39.040 --> 01:55:45.600
bone ornaments and crowns, and use musical&nbsp;
instruments of archaic design such as skull drums,

01:55:45.600 --> 01:55:52.160
thighbone trumpets, and conch horns for inducing&nbsp;
heightened awareness. Practitioners sit in a

01:55:52.160 --> 01:55:58.480
circle and partake of sacramental meat and wine&nbsp;
served in skull cups. The feast culminates in

01:55:58.480 --> 01:56:06.800
the performance of tantric dances and music&nbsp;
that must never be disclosed to outsiders.

01:56:06.800 --> 01:56:13.040
While this ceremony was a crucial part&nbsp;
of King Kertanegara's royal calendar,

01:56:13.040 --> 01:56:22.080
the timing couldn't have been worse. Jaya-Katong's&nbsp;
concealed force arrived outside the city walls

01:56:22.080 --> 01:56:28.400
without warning. They took the garrison by&nbsp;
surprise, and poured over the walls of the

01:56:28.400 --> 01:56:41.120
city while the king was thus engaged. The city&nbsp;
was taken, and King Kertanegara was killed.

01:56:45.120 --> 01:56:52.400
The king's son-in-law, Raden Wijaya, was&nbsp;
still celebrating his victory in the north,

01:56:52.400 --> 01:57:00.160
and he must have been distraught when the news&nbsp;
reached him. He had won his battle, but lost the

01:57:00.160 --> 01:57:09.600
war, and now everything he had stood to inherit&nbsp;
had also been taken by the usurper Jaya-Katong.

01:57:12.160 --> 01:57:17.360
Raden Wijaya now retreated to&nbsp;
the rainforests of the northeast,

01:57:17.360 --> 01:57:26.800
and left the capital and the throne in the hands&nbsp;
of the rebel Jaya-Katong. The city of Kutaraja,

01:57:26.800 --> 01:57:34.400
the heart of the Singhasari kingdom, was looted&nbsp;
and depopulated, and many of its people taken

01:57:34.400 --> 01:57:45.600
back to Kediri. Others simply fled into the&nbsp;
safety of the countryside and never returned.

01:57:45.600 --> 01:57:51.440
Some stonemasons working on a new temple&nbsp;
in the city simply dropped their tools

01:57:51.440 --> 01:57:58.240
in the middle of carving their reliefs, and&nbsp;
never came back to finish them. After that,

01:57:58.240 --> 01:58:07.120
the city was left to crumble and be&nbsp;
taken over by the encroaching forest.

01:58:07.120 --> 01:58:15.440
Now the dispossessed prince Raden Wijaya and&nbsp;
the rebel usurper Jaya-Katong faced off against

01:58:15.440 --> 01:58:23.360
one another, but it seems neither side of&nbsp;
this civil war was confident in victory.

01:58:23.360 --> 01:58:30.160
Raden Wijaya wasn't strong enough to march&nbsp;
south and take his enemy's capital of Kediri,

01:58:30.160 --> 01:58:36.480
and meanwhile, Jaya-Katong couldn't leave&nbsp;
his city and risk losing his shaky grasp on

01:58:36.480 --> 01:58:44.240
the throne. Both parties entered into&nbsp;
an uneasy negotiation, and eventually

01:58:44.240 --> 01:58:53.680
the rebel king settled for buying off Raden&nbsp;
Wijaya. In exchange for laying down his arms,

01:58:53.680 --> 01:59:02.320
the prince would be granted a piece of land,&nbsp;
and there he would be allowed to build a city.

01:59:02.320 --> 01:59:09.520
Raden Wijaya, now completely out of other&nbsp;
options and stranded in the wild rainforest,

01:59:09.520 --> 01:59:21.920
had no choice but to agree, but it must have&nbsp;
been with no small amount of bitterness.

01:59:21.920 --> 01:59:28.400
The prince must have felt bitter, too, when he&nbsp;
reached the piece of land that Jaya-Katong had

01:59:28.400 --> 01:59:36.560
granted him. It was a wild overgrown jungle&nbsp;
in the middle of a sparsely-inhabited area

01:59:36.560 --> 01:59:45.040
known as the Tarik Forest. But his fate was&nbsp;
sealed, and he swallowed his bitterness and

01:59:45.040 --> 01:59:54.320
ordered his people to begin clearing&nbsp;
the land for construction and farming.

01:59:54.320 --> 02:00:05.680
The old growth forests of Java were dense and&nbsp;
wild, fed by the volcanic soil. Colorful hornbills

02:00:05.680 --> 02:00:13.280
roosted in the treetops, while hidden high among&nbsp;
the branches, gibbons greeted the rising sun with

02:00:13.280 --> 02:00:23.040
their long, mournful songs. Troops of long-tailed&nbsp;
macaque monkeys leapt through the trees,

02:00:23.040 --> 02:00:32.080
while sambar deer crept through the shade of the&nbsp;
forest. Great pythons lay coiled in the trees,

02:00:32.080 --> 02:00:42.960
and dangerous animals like the Javan leopard and&nbsp;
wild boar lurked hidden in the green undergrowth.

02:00:42.960 --> 02:00:48.880
Everywhere could be heard the creaking of&nbsp;
cicadas and frogs calling to one another

02:00:48.880 --> 02:00:57.600
from hidden pools. At sunset, fruit bats rose&nbsp;
in their thousands from their hidden caves,

02:00:57.600 --> 02:01:05.600
while fireflies would have been visible at&nbsp;
night. The trees were the most ancient life

02:01:05.600 --> 02:01:13.920
of the forest. Towering hardwoods like teak,&nbsp;
ebony, and tamarind would have risen here,

02:01:13.920 --> 02:01:21.840
interspersed with bamboo groves, strangler&nbsp;
figs, palms, and tangled liana vines,

02:01:21.840 --> 02:01:30.320
all competing for every shaft of sunlight.&nbsp;
But when they began cutting down this forest,

02:01:30.320 --> 02:01:36.960
Raden Wijaya's men found that a particular&nbsp;
kind of tree grew there in great quantities.

02:01:38.400 --> 02:01:46.320
This was the tree that grows a fruit which in&nbsp;
Indonesia is called maja, and is known elsewhere

02:01:46.320 --> 02:01:56.560
as a wood apple, elephant apple, or Indian quince.&nbsp;
These maja fruits are pear-shaped and the size of

02:01:56.560 --> 02:02:04.320
a large grapefruit, with a thick, hard, woody&nbsp;
rind that needs to be cracked with a hammer.

02:02:05.360 --> 02:02:13.280
Inside is an orangey-yellow pulp that gives off&nbsp;
the scent of roses but tastes sweet and bitter,

02:02:13.280 --> 02:02:21.520
a little like marmalade. The word for&nbsp;
bitter in Javanese is pahit , and due

02:02:21.520 --> 02:02:26.000
to the large numbers of these maja&nbsp;
fruits growing in this region,

02:02:26.000 --> 02:02:40.960
the people named this place Majapahit, or&nbsp;
bitter wood apple, as the Pararaton describes.

02:02:40.960 --> 02:02:46.880
When the Madurese were building the village, a&nbsp;
hungry man, lacking supplies while cutting down

02:02:46.880 --> 02:02:55.200
the forest, ate a maja fruit. He found it bitter&nbsp;
and threw away all the maja fruit he had picked.

02:02:55.200 --> 02:03:11.600
The maja fruit there became known for its bitter&nbsp;
taste, and the place was then named Majapahit.

02:03:11.600 --> 02:03:19.040
For Raden Wijaya and his people, the name&nbsp;
Majapahit was a perfect encapsulation of the

02:03:19.040 --> 02:03:27.840
bitterness of their situation. They had lost&nbsp;
a kingdom and gained a plot of wild jungle in

02:03:27.840 --> 02:03:36.640
return, and all because of the treachery&nbsp;
of Jaya-Katong. They all must have shared

02:03:36.640 --> 02:03:47.360
a single desire; to throw out the rebel king&nbsp;
and unite Java once more. Their chance would

02:03:47.360 --> 02:04:09.680
come in the year 1293, when the sails of&nbsp;
the Mongol fleet appeared on the horizon.

02:04:19.120 --> 02:04:24.800
The Mongol fleet that had been sent&nbsp;
to punish the people of Java was led

02:04:24.800 --> 02:04:33.040
by a commander named Shih-bi. When he&nbsp;
heard of the death of King Kertanegara,

02:04:33.040 --> 02:04:39.760
he must have found himself in a quandary.&nbsp;
His emperor demanded vengeance, and he and

02:04:39.760 --> 02:04:48.480
his men had sailed for months to get there. He&nbsp;
must have feared leaving empty-handed. It was

02:04:48.480 --> 02:04:55.600
while he was puzzling over this situation that&nbsp;
Shih-bi was approached by a messenger from the

02:04:55.600 --> 02:05:03.440
bitter lord of the new city of Majapahit,&nbsp;
the dispossessed prince Raden Wijaya.

02:05:04.160 --> 02:05:13.360
The Chinese History of Yuan describes&nbsp;
the deal that the Mongols struck.

02:05:13.360 --> 02:05:18.160
At that time, Java carried on an old&nbsp;
feud with the neighboring country,

02:05:18.160 --> 02:05:25.360
and the old king of Java had already been killed.&nbsp;
The son-in-law of the former, Raden Wijaya,

02:05:25.360 --> 02:05:29.360
had attacked Jaya-Katong,&nbsp;
but could not overcome him.

02:05:29.920 --> 02:05:36.400
He therefore retired to Majapahit, and when he&nbsp;
heard that Shih-bi and his army had arrived,

02:05:36.400 --> 02:05:42.800
he sent envoys with an account of his rivers&nbsp;
and seaports and a map of the country, offering

02:05:42.800 --> 02:05:52.960
his submission and asking for assistance.&nbsp;
Shih-bi then advanced with all his forces.

02:05:52.960 --> 02:05:58.480
For the Mongols, the arrangement was&nbsp;
a neat way to solve their problem.

02:05:59.440 --> 02:06:06.160
They would help Raden Wijaya overthrow the rebel&nbsp;
Jaya-Katong, and place him on the throne as a

02:06:06.160 --> 02:06:14.880
kind of puppet king, swearing allegiance to the&nbsp;
Mongols in China. Then Shih-bi could return to

02:06:14.880 --> 02:06:23.600
Kublai Khan and say that he had brought the&nbsp;
defiant little island back under control.

02:06:23.600 --> 02:06:32.960
But among Raden Wijaya's men, it must have been a&nbsp;
controversial proposal. The Javanese were a proud

02:06:32.960 --> 02:06:42.880
people, and few had any desire to be ruled by&nbsp;
the far-off Mongol Emperor. Some may even have

02:06:42.880 --> 02:06:55.840
called Raden Wijaya a coward and a traitor. But&nbsp;
all this time, he was hatching a plot of his own.

02:06:55.840 --> 02:07:12.160
The later chronicle of the Pararaton describes the&nbsp;
ensuing war, referring to the Mongols as Tatars.

02:07:15.600 --> 02:07:22.320
The Tatar army came out from the north, while&nbsp;
the Majapahit armies came out from the east.

02:07:22.320 --> 02:07:29.440
King Jaya-Katong was confused, unsure which to&nbsp;
guard. Then he was attacked heavily from the

02:07:29.440 --> 02:07:37.040
north by the Tatar army. King Jaya-Katong, who&nbsp;
was fighting to the north, armed with a shield,

02:07:37.040 --> 02:07:53.920
was attacked together by the Tatar people, and&nbsp;
was finally caught and imprisoned by the Tatars.

02:07:53.920 --> 02:08:01.280
The Mongols had now become kingmakers in&nbsp;
Java. The 13th-century Persian historian

02:08:01.280 --> 02:08:10.480
Abdullah Wassaf describes how they put&nbsp;
Raden Wijaya on the Javanese throne.

02:08:10.480 --> 02:08:15.920
His majesty Kublai put the old king's&nbsp;
son-in-law, Wijaya, on the steps of the

02:08:15.920 --> 02:08:23.920
throne. He bestowed on him a ceremonial dress of&nbsp;
honor, and conferred on him much grace, and in

02:08:23.920 --> 02:08:35.440
exchange for the payment of tribute and taxes of&nbsp;
pearls and gold, he left the island in his hands.

02:08:35.440 --> 02:08:42.000
The Mongol emperor Kublai must have been&nbsp;
ecstatic when he heard the news. Finally,

02:08:42.000 --> 02:08:49.040
here was a victory he could show to his people.&nbsp;
The wealthy island of Java would now be added

02:08:49.040 --> 02:08:57.040
to his imperial domain, along with its control of&nbsp;
major shipping lanes, and perhaps now the fortunes

02:08:57.040 --> 02:09:07.120
of his teetering empire would finally turn around.&nbsp;
But that was not to be the case. That's because

02:09:07.120 --> 02:09:15.200
the new king, Raden Wijaya, had no intention&nbsp;
of ruling Java as a vassal of Kublai Khan,

02:09:15.200 --> 02:09:21.600
and the moment he got to power, he turned on&nbsp;
the Mongol forces who had helped him seize

02:09:21.600 --> 02:09:31.680
the throne. The Chinese History of Yuan gives&nbsp;
a terse account of this disastrous turnaround.

02:09:33.200 --> 02:09:38.880
Raden Wijaya asked permission to return to&nbsp;
his country in order to prepare a new letter

02:09:38.880 --> 02:09:45.680
of submission to the emperor, and to take the&nbsp;
precious articles in his possession back to court.

02:09:45.680 --> 02:09:53.760
Shih-bi consented to this, and sent two officers&nbsp;
with 200 men to go with him. But on the way,

02:09:53.760 --> 02:10:00.320
Raden Wijaya killed the two officers and&nbsp;
revolted again. After which, he availed

02:10:00.320 --> 02:10:10.400
himself of the circumstance that the main army&nbsp;
was returning to attack it from both sides.

02:10:10.400 --> 02:10:15.520
The Mongol army believed their mission to&nbsp;
be complete, and were already packing their

02:10:15.520 --> 02:10:24.000
ships to leave. The seasonal winds were about to&nbsp;
change direction, and if they didn't move fast,

02:10:24.000 --> 02:10:31.840
they might become stranded on Java for another&nbsp;
six months. When Raden Wijaya's soldiers burst out

02:10:31.840 --> 02:10:39.920
of the forest in ambush, firing arrows and darts&nbsp;
from their blow guns, the Mongol Yuan troops were

02:10:39.920 --> 02:10:50.640
taken completely by surprise. The Javanese epic&nbsp;
poem Bhomantaka describes a battle of this kind.

02:10:53.760 --> 02:11:00.320
They were thrown into disarray, and in fear the&nbsp;
demon army fled, and many of the demons and their

02:11:00.320 --> 02:11:07.200
leaders were struck dead. They withdrew in haste&nbsp;
to the shelter of the fort, but were pursued.

02:11:07.200 --> 02:11:14.080
Others, shuddering, sought a way of resisting,&nbsp;
but lacked the strength and, hit by arrows,

02:11:14.080 --> 02:11:21.600
floundered about, and all of them left in fear&nbsp;
and ran away. The whole fort was in uproar,

02:11:21.600 --> 02:11:35.760
and all shouted in confusion. The fires glowed and&nbsp;
blazed up frightfully like an erupting volcano.

02:11:35.760 --> 02:11:40.480
Thousands were killed, and the&nbsp;
remains of the Mongol army fled

02:11:40.480 --> 02:11:47.840
in disarray back to the ships,&nbsp;
as the History of Yuan describes.

02:11:51.920 --> 02:11:56.400
Shih-bi was left behind and was cut off from&nbsp;
the rest of the army. He was forced to fight

02:11:56.400 --> 02:12:02.000
his way back to the ships for a distance of&nbsp;
more than 100 miles. At last, he embarked again,

02:12:02.000 --> 02:12:13.520
and reached China on a voyage of sixty-eight days.&nbsp;
Of his soldiers, more than 3,000 men had died.

02:12:13.520 --> 02:12:22.560
When news of this betrayal reached the emperor&nbsp;
Kublai Khan, we can only imagine his rage. He

02:12:22.560 --> 02:12:30.400
ordered the general Shih-bi to receive seventeen&nbsp;
lashes, and commanded a third of his property to

02:12:30.400 --> 02:12:37.840
be confiscated as punishment for his failure.&nbsp;
He ordered his ministers to begin immediate

02:12:37.840 --> 02:12:46.880
preparations for a full-scale invasion of Java&nbsp;
with an even larger army of 100,000 men. But

02:12:46.880 --> 02:12:55.520
these plans would never come to fruition. The&nbsp;
disaster in Java would be the last campaign of

02:12:55.520 --> 02:13:06.160
Kublai Khan's reign, and he died the next year&nbsp;
in February of 1294 at the age of seventy-eight.

02:13:06.160 --> 02:13:14.160
After that, the Mongol Yuan Dynasty would enter&nbsp;
into a steady and irrevocable decline, and would

02:13:14.160 --> 02:13:24.080
collapse entirely little more than seventy years&nbsp;
later. One great empire was coming to an end,

02:13:24.080 --> 02:13:30.640
but in its collapse, it gave way for the rise&nbsp;
of what was now the undisputed major power on

02:13:30.640 --> 02:13:39.600
the island of Java and in the archipelago&nbsp;
of Nusantara, the new power of Majapahit.

02:13:47.760 --> 02:13:54.560
On the 15th day of the month of Kaartikka,&nbsp;
the 8th month of the Hindu calendar,

02:13:54.560 --> 02:14:00.560
Raden Wijaya was crowned king&nbsp;
in the palace of Majapahit.

02:14:00.560 --> 02:14:07.520
What had once been a stretch of wild jungle&nbsp;
was now the beating heart of Java's most

02:14:07.520 --> 02:14:15.120
powerful kingdom. The Chinese History of Sung&nbsp;
describes the appearance of the kings of Java,

02:14:15.120 --> 02:14:24.720
and from this we might imagine how this new&nbsp;
king looked on the day of his coronation.

02:14:24.720 --> 02:14:32.080
The king has hair in a knot upon the&nbsp;
top of his head. He wears golden bells,

02:14:32.080 --> 02:14:39.600
a silk robe, and shoes of leather. He sits&nbsp;
on a square couch, and his functionaries,

02:14:39.600 --> 02:14:46.560
who see him daily, salute him three&nbsp;
times on going away. When he goes out,

02:14:46.560 --> 02:14:55.440
he rides on an elephant or sits in a carriage,&nbsp;
and from 500 to 700 soldiers follow him.

02:14:56.000 --> 02:15:10.800
When the people see the king, they&nbsp;
squat down until he has passed.

02:15:10.800 --> 02:15:16.560
Raden Wijaya would rule as the king&nbsp;
of Majapahit for sixteen years,

02:15:16.560 --> 02:15:22.240
but his reign would be a troubled one.

02:15:25.600 --> 02:15:30.080
From the earliest days of his&nbsp;
rule, he faced rebellions,

02:15:30.080 --> 02:15:37.520
many of them from men who had fought alongside&nbsp;
him against Jaya-Katong and the Mongols.

02:15:38.400 --> 02:15:44.320
Many of these disagreements were sparked over the&nbsp;
question of who would become the king's mahapatih,

02:15:45.360 --> 02:15:52.640
a powerful and influential position that combined&nbsp;
elements of a prime minister and head general,

02:15:52.640 --> 02:16:01.760
and essentially acted as the king's right hand.&nbsp;
Whatever his skills as a diplomat, it seems Raden

02:16:01.760 --> 02:16:09.360
Wijaya struggled to manage the ambitions of the&nbsp;
men who served under him. For this reason, he

02:16:09.360 --> 02:16:17.680
spent much of his rule putting down rebellions all&nbsp;
over Java. In an effort to cement the legitimacy

02:16:17.680 --> 02:16:23.440
of his new dynasty, he would marry the remaining&nbsp;
three daughters of his dead father-in-law,

02:16:23.440 --> 02:16:31.040
Kertanegara, and ensure that the royal line&nbsp;
of succession would pass entirely through him.

02:16:31.040 --> 02:16:40.880
But still the problems persisted, and the new&nbsp;
kingdom teetered on the brink of destruction.

02:16:40.880 --> 02:16:47.760
When Raden Wijaya died in the year 1309,&nbsp;
he must have hoped that his only son,

02:16:47.760 --> 02:16:54.800
Jayanegara, would set things right. But&nbsp;
due to the apparently deficient nature

02:16:54.800 --> 02:16:59.760
of Jayanegara's character,&nbsp;
this would not be the case.

02:17:05.280 --> 02:17:11.439
King Jayanegara would rule for nearly&nbsp;
twenty years after the death of his father,

02:17:11.439 --> 02:17:18.240
but he was a widely disliked character, so&nbsp;
much so that the author of the chronicle the

02:17:18.240 --> 02:17:25.599
Pararaton would give him the nickname Kala&nbsp;
Gemet, meaning weak villain . Meanwhile,

02:17:25.600 --> 02:17:32.000
the Javanese folk tradition has remembered&nbsp;
him by the name Bajang Ratu, or dwarf

02:17:32.000 --> 02:17:42.080
king , referencing either his short stature&nbsp;
or simply the uninspiring nature of his rule.

02:17:42.080 --> 02:17:49.280
Jayanegara faced multiple attempts on his life&nbsp;
and widespread rebellions even worse than his

02:17:49.280 --> 02:17:58.719
father s, the most serious of which was led by a&nbsp;
man named Kuti. This uprising became so dangerous

02:17:58.720 --> 02:18:06.319
that the king was forced to abandon his capital&nbsp;
for a time, and it's during this time of upheaval

02:18:06.319 --> 02:18:13.439
that a man would walk onto the historical stage&nbsp;
who would turn around the fortunes of this new

02:18:13.439 --> 02:18:21.759
and struggling kingdom. He was a soldier who led&nbsp;
the king's bodyguard during the Kuti rebellion,

02:18:21.760 --> 02:18:42.160
and gained a position of trust in the&nbsp;
royal court. His name was Gajah Mada.

02:18:47.359 --> 02:18:56.080
Gajah Mada's name came from the old Javanese word&nbsp;
gaja , meaning elephant , and mada , which is a

02:18:56.080 --> 02:19:04.000
difficult to translate word describing a state&nbsp;
of furious intoxication. This probably alluded

02:19:04.000 --> 02:19:12.000
to the period of a few weeks every year when male&nbsp;
elephants enter their breeding cycle called musth,

02:19:12.000 --> 02:19:20.479
and their testosterone levels explode. During&nbsp;
this time, a bull elephant seeps liquid from a

02:19:20.479 --> 02:19:28.719
gland around its ears, and becomes extremely&nbsp;
aggressive and dangerous. Elephants had long

02:19:28.720 --> 02:19:36.240
been used for warfare in Java. They formed the&nbsp;
centerpiece of any army, and allowed royals

02:19:36.240 --> 02:19:45.040
and generals to command the battlefield from an&nbsp;
elevated position. Although prehistoric fossils of

02:19:45.040 --> 02:19:51.920
native elephants have been found in Java, this&nbsp;
species had long been extinct by this point,

02:19:51.920 --> 02:20:00.720
and during the medieval period, elephants were&nbsp;
imported from other lands. They were enormous,

02:20:00.720 --> 02:20:07.760
expensive, and prestigious, their tusks sometimes&nbsp;
adorned with metal implements to make them even

02:20:07.760 --> 02:20:14.240
more deadly, and their sides often oiled before&nbsp;
going into battle to prevent the enemy from

02:20:14.240 --> 02:20:21.679
climbing on their backs. So, this commander's&nbsp;
name would have generated images of strength,

02:20:21.680 --> 02:20:34.479
vitality, and an unstoppable force, and&nbsp;
by all accounts, it would be a good fit.

02:20:34.479 --> 02:20:39.839
Although the elephant general Gajah Mada would&nbsp;
eventually rise through the new kingdom of

02:20:39.840 --> 02:20:47.520
Majapahit to become one of its most influential&nbsp;
statesmen, his birth was relatively unexceptional.

02:20:48.160 --> 02:20:53.760
We know little about his early life, but&nbsp;
he was likely born into a poor family in

02:20:53.760 --> 02:21:00.479
the area around the city of Majapahit, and his&nbsp;
father was possibly killed in the war with the

02:21:00.479 --> 02:21:08.080
Mongols. But he would prove his worth and&nbsp;
become the head of the king's elite royal

02:21:08.080 --> 02:21:15.359
bodyguard, the Bhayangkara. After successfully&nbsp;
protecting the king through the Kuti rebellion,

02:21:15.359 --> 02:21:21.599
Gajah Mada would be rewarded with the&nbsp;
governorship of a small province. Finally,

02:21:21.600 --> 02:21:29.760
he would be named mahapatih,&nbsp;
or the right hand of the king.

02:21:29.760 --> 02:21:39.281
The epic poem the Deshawarnana describes this&nbsp;
warrior statesman in the following terms.

02:21:43.760 --> 02:21:48.960
Gajah Mada is the excellent party&nbsp;
of Majapahit, an officer valiant,

02:21:48.960 --> 02:21:54.160
discerning in tactics, with a firm foundation&nbsp;
in loyalty and devotion to the king,

02:21:54.160 --> 02:22:00.639
eloquent and skilled in speech, polite&nbsp;
and patient, constantly exerting himself,

02:22:00.640 --> 02:22:07.201
unremitting; he watches over the king's&nbsp;
position as supreme ruler of the world.

02:22:14.399 --> 02:22:21.359
King Jayanegara was clearly a man ill-suited&nbsp;
to rule, but with Gajah Mada's help,

02:22:21.359 --> 02:22:26.960
he was able to keep hold of his kingdom&nbsp;
by his fingernails, and it's in the last

02:22:26.960 --> 02:22:34.960
decade of his reign that a visitor from a&nbsp;
far-off land arrived on the shores of Java.

02:22:36.080 --> 02:22:41.280
He was a traveller from the&nbsp;
northern Italian region of Friulia,

02:22:41.280 --> 02:22:58.399
then a territory of the Holy Roman Empire,&nbsp;
and his name was Odoric of Pordenone.

02:22:58.399 --> 02:23:06.000
Around the year 1318, Odoric had departed&nbsp;
from Venice and spent the next decade or

02:23:06.000 --> 02:23:11.600
so traveling all over Asia, passing&nbsp;
through Persia, and seeing the ruins

02:23:11.600 --> 02:23:19.520
of the ancient city of Persepolis. He then&nbsp;
sailed through the Strait of Hormuz to India,

02:23:19.520 --> 02:23:27.439
and from there through the Straits of&nbsp;
Malacca to Borneo, Sumatra, and Java.

02:23:27.439 --> 02:23:34.479
When he returned home, he dictated the memoirs&nbsp;
of his travels in a work entitled the Relatio

02:23:34.479 --> 02:23:41.759
de Mirabilibus Orientalium Tatarorum, or&nbsp;
the Account of the Marvels of the Eastern

02:23:41.760 --> 02:23:52.960
Tatars. In this text he provides a remarkable&nbsp;
snapshot of life in Java around the year 1320.

02:23:54.640 --> 02:24:04.080
There is an island which is called Java, which is&nbsp;
a good 3,000 miles around. The king of this island

02:24:04.080 --> 02:24:12.000
has under him seven crowned kings as vassals.&nbsp;
This island is very populous and is the second

02:24:12.000 --> 02:24:20.479
best of all islands that be on the Earth. One&nbsp;
finds there cloves, pepper, nutmegs, and many

02:24:20.479 --> 02:24:30.639
other precious spices that they grind there,&nbsp;
and all manner of necessities in great quantity.

02:24:30.640 --> 02:24:35.600
Odoric was particularly impressed&nbsp;
by the royal palace of Jayanegara,

02:24:35.600 --> 02:24:42.080
which was richly decorated in gold&nbsp;
mined from the volcanic mountain ranges.

02:24:43.520 --> 02:24:50.560
The king of this island lives in a marvellous&nbsp;
and very large palace. Its steps are of gold

02:24:50.560 --> 02:24:58.240
and silver alternately. The paving tiles&nbsp;
are likewise one gold and the next silver.

02:24:58.240 --> 02:25:04.880
The walls are covered with plates of gold,&nbsp;
and there are mounted men sculpted out of

02:25:04.880 --> 02:25:10.720
gold on them with golden circles around&nbsp;
their heads like the diadems we make for

02:25:10.720 --> 02:25:17.840
the figures of saints, and these circles are&nbsp;
full of precious stones. To put it briefly,

02:25:17.840 --> 02:25:25.600
this is the richest and loveliest&nbsp;
palace that there be in the whole world.

02:25:26.640 --> 02:25:33.680
Odoric also records that many still remembered&nbsp;
the war with the Mongol Yuan Dynasty of China,

02:25:33.680 --> 02:25:39.120
now nearly thirty years in the past.

02:25:39.120 --> 02:25:46.240
The Great Khan of Cathay, who is the sovereign&nbsp;
emperor of all the Tatars, has often made war

02:25:46.240 --> 02:25:53.920
with this king and often brought battle to&nbsp;
him, but this king has always defeated him.

02:25:55.520 --> 02:26:00.160
But despite the glowing report&nbsp;
of Odoric and the ongoing help

02:26:00.160 --> 02:26:07.840
of the elephant general Gajah Mada,&nbsp;
Jayanegara's reign was a poor one.

02:26:09.920 --> 02:26:16.560
He was so insecure in his rule that he at one&nbsp;
point seems to have considered marrying two of his

02:26:16.560 --> 02:26:25.120
own sisters, named Dyah Wiyat and Dyah Gitarja,&nbsp;
to ensure that no rivals could arise from the

02:26:25.120 --> 02:26:34.479
royal line. When opposition to this plan exploded&nbsp;
in the court and their mother loudly protested,

02:26:34.479 --> 02:26:50.879
King Jayanegara gave up on this idea, but he still&nbsp;
forbade his sisters from marrying anyone else.

02:26:50.880 --> 02:26:55.760
King Jayanegara had two sisters&nbsp;
born of the same mother.

02:26:56.319 --> 02:27:03.200
He did not permit them to be married to others,&nbsp;
wishing to keep them for himself. Therefore,

02:27:03.200 --> 02:27:10.559
no prince dared remain openly at Majapahit.&nbsp;
Any man who appeared was driven away,

02:27:10.560 --> 02:27:20.640
lest he seek their hands, and the other&nbsp;
nobles kept themselves hidden in fear.

02:27:24.240 --> 02:27:31.040
Jayanegara confined these two princesses for&nbsp;
years in the women's quarters of the palace,

02:27:31.040 --> 02:27:38.399
and prevented anyone from seeing&nbsp;
them. It was a microcosm of a rule

02:27:38.399 --> 02:27:46.799
that seems to have been at every turn&nbsp;
paranoid, petty, and authoritarian.

02:27:46.800 --> 02:27:54.800
Over the course of his reign, the kingdom of&nbsp;
Majapahit would falter. The island of Bali,

02:27:54.800 --> 02:28:01.359
just a short hop over the Sunda Strait,&nbsp;
slipped out of his control around this time,

02:28:01.359 --> 02:28:06.319
and people must have feared that the&nbsp;
whole kingdom would now disintegrate

02:28:06.319 --> 02:28:14.880
and the land would return to destructive&nbsp;
civil war. But that was not to be the case.

02:28:15.840 --> 02:28:22.880
That's because of a woman who had made&nbsp;
her name standing up to King Jayanegara,

02:28:22.880 --> 02:28:44.399
the mother of the two imprisoned&nbsp;
princesses, a woman named Dyah Gayatri.

02:28:44.399 --> 02:28:53.359
Dyah was a title that in old Javanese was given&nbsp;
to princes and princesses of the royal line,

02:28:53.359 --> 02:29:02.319
and Dyah Gayatri had been the youngest daughter&nbsp;
of King Kertanegara, the last king of Singhasari.

02:29:02.319 --> 02:29:09.120
She had also been the most senior wife of&nbsp;
the founder of Majapahit, Raden Wijaya,

02:29:09.120 --> 02:29:17.519
and all her life she had been a quiet force&nbsp;
behind the lives of these great men. As a girl,

02:29:17.520 --> 02:29:25.200
she had watched her father brand the face of the&nbsp;
Mongol envoys, and then saw her home of Singhasari

02:29:25.200 --> 02:29:33.359
destroyed by the rebellion of Jaya-Katong. She had&nbsp;
fled through the rice fields to safety while her

02:29:33.359 --> 02:29:42.160
home burned behind her. As a young woman, she&nbsp;
had watched the war with the Mongols unfold,

02:29:42.160 --> 02:29:48.559
then seen her husband found the kingdom of&nbsp;
Majapahit, which now stood as his lasting

02:29:48.560 --> 02:29:57.359
legacy. By now she was a widow in her fifties, and&nbsp;
had retired to become a Buddhist nun in a forest

02:29:57.359 --> 02:30:06.000
monastery on the slope of a nearby mountain. But&nbsp;
she was still known by the title Rajapatni, a word

02:30:06.000 --> 02:30:16.640
which means wife of kings , and which gave her the&nbsp;
prestige of a royal matriarch. The bumbling king

02:30:16.640 --> 02:30:26.160
Jayanegara was her late husband's son by another&nbsp;
less senior wife, and we can only imagine how Dyah

02:30:26.160 --> 02:30:34.720
Gayatri felt now watching from her monastery as he&nbsp;
veered the empire from crisis to crisis and kept

02:30:34.720 --> 02:30:43.840
her two daughters under lock and key. She must&nbsp;
have feared that the legacy that she and Raden

02:30:43.840 --> 02:30:54.160
Wijaya had built was about to be destroyed,&nbsp;
but she refused to let that happen. To help

02:30:54.160 --> 02:31:01.599
her free her daughters, reclaim the kingdom, and&nbsp;
put it back on course, this royal matriarch would

02:31:01.600 --> 02:31:09.600
enlist the help of the man who had recently been&nbsp;
rising in prominence in the kingdom of Majapahit,

02:31:09.600 --> 02:31:17.680
the raging elephant who had become indispensable&nbsp;
to the feckless king, the soldier and statesman

02:31:17.680 --> 02:31:39.680
who went by the name Gajah Mada. In the&nbsp;
year 1328, it seems they took their chance.

02:31:39.680 --> 02:31:45.600
According to the official story related&nbsp;
in the royal chronicle the Pararaton,

02:31:45.600 --> 02:31:51.359
that year King Jayanegara was suffering&nbsp;
from an unpleasant condition, a painful

02:31:51.359 --> 02:31:58.559
boil or swelling of some kind, and when&nbsp;
he summoned his doctor, a man named Tanca,

02:31:58.560 --> 02:32:06.081
he apparently forgot that he had previously&nbsp;
insulted or taken advantage of this man's wife.

02:32:06.880 --> 02:32:22.560
Enraged at this, the doctor took his&nbsp;
revenge, as the Pararaton records.

02:32:22.560 --> 02:32:29.600
Tanca's wife spread word that the king&nbsp;
had treated her dishonorably. Tanca was

02:32:29.600 --> 02:32:37.600
summoned before Gajah Mada. At that time, King&nbsp;
Jayanegara was suffering from a swelling, and

02:32:37.600 --> 02:32:44.240
could not leave his chambers. Tanca was ordered&nbsp;
to perform an operation with a sharp lancet,

02:32:44.240 --> 02:32:50.800
and he approached beside the royal bed. Tanca&nbsp;
stabbed the king's boil once, then again with

02:32:50.800 --> 02:32:58.480
the blade. But the weapon would not burst it,&nbsp;
for the king wore an amulet of protection.

02:32:59.120 --> 02:33:05.519
Then the king was asked to set this&nbsp;
aside. He took off the charm and laid

02:33:05.520 --> 02:33:14.080
it beside the bed. Tanca stabbed him again,&nbsp;
and this time the blade burst the boil. But

02:33:14.080 --> 02:33:24.800
Tanca continued stabbing him and he did not&nbsp;
stop until the king died there upon the bed.

02:33:35.200 --> 02:33:42.080
The doctor-turned-assassin, Tanca, was&nbsp;
swiftly killed by Gajah Mada, which meant,

02:33:42.080 --> 02:33:49.680
perhaps conveniently, that his side of the&nbsp;
story could never be told. As historians,

02:33:49.680 --> 02:33:56.000
we can never actually know what happened in&nbsp;
that room. But the slightly fanciful story

02:33:56.000 --> 02:34:02.319
involving a magical amulet and a vengeful&nbsp;
doctor has left many historians wondering

02:34:02.319 --> 02:34:10.559
if the true story wasn't a simpler one, that&nbsp;
Gajah Mada and the dowager queen Dyah Gayatri

02:34:10.560 --> 02:34:18.640
had simply plotted together to kill&nbsp;
the king, framing the poor doctor.

02:34:18.640 --> 02:34:24.720
Whether they had planned the whole thing or&nbsp;
just seized the opportunity on a stroke of good

02:34:24.720 --> 02:34:33.439
luck, Gajah Mada and the dowager matriarch Dyah&nbsp;
Gayatri went on to seize control of the kingdom,

02:34:33.439 --> 02:34:40.879
and it seems King Jayanegara was sufficiently&nbsp;
disliked that few people were all that concerned

02:34:40.880 --> 02:34:48.399
about what might actually have happened&nbsp;
to him. But the royal matriarch and the

02:34:48.399 --> 02:34:57.200
general prime minister faced a problem. Gajah&nbsp;
Mada himself had risen from humble beginnings,

02:34:57.200 --> 02:35:02.800
and as a commoner, he had absolutely no&nbsp;
legitimacy to rule under Javanese law.

02:35:03.600 --> 02:35:10.720
And while Dyah Gayatri was officially the next in&nbsp;
line to the throne, she had already taken a vow to

02:35:10.720 --> 02:35:18.720
become a Buddhist nun, a position that prevented&nbsp;
her from holding any positions of earthly power,

02:35:18.720 --> 02:35:27.280
and she had no sons who might take the throne.&nbsp;
In fact, the histories record that she had only

02:35:27.280 --> 02:35:33.920
given birth to her two daughters, who were&nbsp;
now allowed to leave the palace for the first

02:35:33.920 --> 02:35:46.960
time in years. The Deshawarnana describes&nbsp;
these women in conventionally poetic terms.

02:35:49.680 --> 02:35:56.319
And then the king, Jayanegara, was left behind&nbsp;
as king of Majapahit with his princesses,

02:35:56.319 --> 02:36:00.160
his youngest sisters, who were the&nbsp;
daughters of the worthy Rajapatni

02:36:00.960 --> 02:36:06.160
the incomparable. They were both of the&nbsp;
greatest beauty, surpassing the women of

02:36:06.160 --> 02:36:14.240
heaven. The elder was princess of Jiwana,&nbsp;
and the younger ruled as princess of Daha.

02:36:20.880 --> 02:36:26.399
The Rajapatni Dyah Gayatri soon&nbsp;
appointed her young daughter,

02:36:26.399 --> 02:36:33.839
Dyah Gitarja, to rule as queen in her place.

02:36:36.399 --> 02:36:40.399
Verily in the shaka-year Moon Arrows Two Form,

02:36:40.399 --> 02:36:45.519
the princess of Jiwana succeeded&nbsp;
in Majapahit as queen. It was the

02:36:45.520 --> 02:36:51.040
disposition of the great Rajapatni that she&nbsp;
should be the auspicious leader of the world,

02:36:51.040 --> 02:37:00.000
distinguished from others. It was she who made&nbsp;
her ruler and watched over all her affairs.

02:37:05.439 --> 02:37:12.319
The princess soon took to the throne, and with&nbsp;
her mother watching over her, she would be a

02:37:12.319 --> 02:37:22.080
far more capable ruler than her feckless brother.&nbsp;
She would take a royal name that meant the exalted

02:37:22.080 --> 02:37:41.280
goddess of the three worlds, who the glory of&nbsp;
Vishnu radiates ; Tribhuwana Wijayatungadewi.

02:37:41.280 --> 02:37:47.359
Tribhuwana was her mother's daughter. She&nbsp;
was a natural stateswoman, and was able

02:37:47.359 --> 02:37:53.759
to stabilize the kingdom after the period&nbsp;
of poor leadership that had preceded her,

02:37:53.760 --> 02:38:00.640
and she seems to have learned from the mistakes of&nbsp;
both her father and brother. Early in her reign,

02:38:00.640 --> 02:38:09.040
she faced a rebellion by two men named Sadeng and&nbsp;
Keta, and a conflict arose in her court over which

02:38:09.040 --> 02:38:16.960
of her generals would lead her army into battle.&nbsp;
These kinds of political disputes had plagued the

02:38:16.960 --> 02:38:25.680
last two kings, but Tribhuwana resolved it&nbsp;
in a simple way. Rather than show favor to

02:38:25.680 --> 02:38:43.359
one general over the other, she simply led the&nbsp;
army herself, as the Deshawarnana describes.

02:38:43.359 --> 02:38:48.080
In shaka-year Fires Arrows Suns, their enemies&nbsp;
were destroyed. Sadeng and Keta were defeated,

02:38:48.080 --> 02:38:55.280
overcome by her personal retinue. Further&nbsp;
in the shaka-year Arrows Seasons Eyes Navel,

02:38:55.280 --> 02:38:59.439
the king of Bali was evil and&nbsp;
base. He was attacked with an army,

02:38:59.439 --> 02:39:09.439
broken and completely crushed. Every kind of&nbsp;
evildoer was fearful, and made off quickly.

02:39:13.840 --> 02:39:20.720
Tribhuwana's early successes on the&nbsp;
battlefield gave her a taste for conquest,

02:39:20.720 --> 02:39:28.160
and over her rule, this queen of Java would&nbsp;
embark on a massive expansion of Majapahit,

02:39:28.160 --> 02:39:34.399
to spread its influence all over the archipelago.

02:39:34.399 --> 02:39:41.200
To embark on this mission, she appointed as her&nbsp;
mahapatih, or prime minister, the general who

02:39:41.200 --> 02:39:47.679
had done more than perhaps anyone else to keep&nbsp;
the young kingdom together, and who had worked

02:39:47.680 --> 02:40:01.120
with her mother to depose the previous king. This&nbsp;
was the rampaging elephant himself, Gajah Mada.

02:40:01.120 --> 02:40:08.720
By all accounts, Gajah Mada rose to this&nbsp;
challenge. Spurred on by his warlike queen,

02:40:08.720 --> 02:40:21.120
the Pararaton records him swearing a remarkable&nbsp;
oath of conquest before the entire royal court.

02:40:21.120 --> 02:40:29.040
Gajah Mada swore, "Until I have conquered the&nbsp;
entire Nusantara archipelago, I will eat no

02:40:29.040 --> 02:40:36.319
spices nor take any rest. If I have conquered&nbsp;
the Gorom Islands and the island of Seram,

02:40:36.319 --> 02:40:44.160
the kingdom of Tanjung-Pura, the kingdoms&nbsp;
of Haru and Pahang, the Dompu regency,

02:40:44.160 --> 02:40:51.760
the island of Bali, the lands of Sunda, along&nbsp;
with the Tumasik and Palembang of Sumatra,

02:40:51.760 --> 02:40:59.040
then and only then will I&nbsp;
eat spices and take my rest.

02:40:59.040 --> 02:41:09.200
Over the next three decades, Gajah Mada would&nbsp;
lead the armies of Majapahit across the seas,

02:41:09.200 --> 02:41:16.240
and would bring ever more lands under the&nbsp;
influence and control of the empire. The

02:41:16.240 --> 02:41:21.760
Italian traveller Ludovico di Varthema&nbsp;
describes the weapons of the Javanese

02:41:21.760 --> 02:41:29.680
that he saw when he visited sometime&nbsp;
later around 1505. They carry bows,

02:41:29.680 --> 02:41:36.000
and the greater part darts of cane. Some also use&nbsp;
blowpipes, with which they throw poison darts,

02:41:36.000 --> 02:41:42.399
and they throw them with the mouth, and however&nbsp;
little they draw blood, the wounded person dies.

02:41:43.359 --> 02:41:51.839
The 12th-century Javanese poem, the Bhomantaka,&nbsp;
describes an idealized army marching to battle,

02:41:51.840 --> 02:41:55.600
and gives a sense for what it would&nbsp;
have been like to see the forces

02:41:55.600 --> 02:42:05.600
of Gajah Mada landing on the shores of&nbsp;
each island in turn and preparing for war.

02:42:05.600 --> 02:42:13.040
Their weapons were spears and lances, and there&nbsp;
were also thousands of bows. Then came the riding

02:42:13.040 --> 02:42:20.399
elephants, and their weapons were spears, lances,&nbsp;
javelins, and clubs. Many heroes were mounted on

02:42:20.399 --> 02:42:28.240
horses, skilled riders in horseback combat. The&nbsp;
spears and very sharp knives, when whirled, glowed

02:42:28.240 --> 02:42:36.479
brightly. Men came bearing lances and shields,&nbsp;
and others with slingshots. Their casings shone,

02:42:36.479 --> 02:42:51.040
wrapped in silk and gilded, and their various&nbsp;
jackets were like clouds traveling over the earth.

02:42:51.040 --> 02:42:58.640
The Majapahit forces of Queen Tribhuwana&nbsp;
Wijayatungadewi crossed the sea to the east

02:42:58.640 --> 02:43:07.280
and reconquered the small island of Bali&nbsp;
in 1343. After that, they went northwest

02:43:07.280 --> 02:43:15.439
and absorbed most of the coastal regions of the&nbsp;
larger island of Sumatra into her growing empire.

02:43:16.560 --> 02:43:22.880
Other kingdoms were incorporated peacefully&nbsp;
into the empire as tributary subjects,

02:43:22.880 --> 02:43:29.040
such as the large island of Sulawesi,&nbsp;
whose name means iron island on account

02:43:29.040 --> 02:43:35.600
of the high quality iron it produced&nbsp;
and exported around the region.

02:43:37.680 --> 02:43:43.840
The later poem, the Deshawarnana, lists&nbsp;
at length all of the island territories

02:43:43.840 --> 02:43:53.040
that were either conquered by Majapahit or&nbsp;
agreed to pay it tribute without a fight.

02:43:53.040 --> 02:43:56.640
The islands and lesser islands in the&nbsp;
first place coming under the territory

02:43:56.640 --> 02:44:05.040
of the Malay islands are Jambi and Palembang,&nbsp;
Keritang, Teba, and Dharmasraya. Follow Kandis,

02:44:05.040 --> 02:44:14.000
Kahwas, Manangkabwa, Siyak, Rekan, Kampar and&nbsp;
Pane, Kampe, Haru and Mandahiling, Tumihang,

02:44:14.000 --> 02:44:20.479
Parlak and Barat, Lewas and Samudra, as well&nbsp;
as Lamuri, Batan, Lampung, and Barus. Those

02:44:20.479 --> 02:44:27.439
are the main ones among the Malay islands.&nbsp;
All these countries are subject and obedient.

02:44:29.359 --> 02:44:35.759
The great queen Tribhuwana Wijayatungadewi&nbsp;
would rule for twenty-two years,

02:44:35.760 --> 02:44:47.439
and her reign saw the rise of Majapahit to a&nbsp;
true thalassocracy, or an empire of the sea.

02:44:47.439 --> 02:44:54.240
But she must have known that&nbsp;
her reign couldn't last forever.

02:44:58.640 --> 02:45:07.840
In 1350, her mother, the great royal matriarch&nbsp;
Dyah Gayatri, died, most likely in her forest

02:45:07.840 --> 02:45:17.520
monastery after a long and accomplished life. Her&nbsp;
daughter Tribhuwana had been ruling ostensibly on

02:45:17.520 --> 02:45:25.200
behalf of her mother, and it seems that once the&nbsp;
old woman died, her situation would also become

02:45:25.200 --> 02:45:34.800
untenable. But luckily by this time she had had&nbsp;
a son, who was now the first in the royal line,

02:45:34.800 --> 02:45:49.680
and he had just come of age at sixteen&nbsp;
years old. His name was Hayam Wuruk.

02:45:49.680 --> 02:45:53.920
When this young prince Hayam&nbsp;
Wuruk came to the throne,

02:45:53.920 --> 02:46:00.640
he would have been crowned with enormous&nbsp;
ceremony and celebration. The historian

02:46:00.640 --> 02:46:08.241
Herald Van Der Linde describes the rituals&nbsp;
associated with a Javanese coronation.

02:46:09.680 --> 02:46:15.840
At sunset on the eve of the first day of the&nbsp;
first month of the year, priests entered the inner

02:46:15.840 --> 02:46:23.840
pavilion of the royal palace, followed by acolytes&nbsp;
chanting sacred Sanskrit texts. King Hayam Wuruk

02:46:23.840 --> 02:46:30.800
sat on a throne in the amazing pavilion, where&nbsp;
matted-haired rishi hermits and religious leaders

02:46:30.800 --> 02:46:38.960
sprinkled holy water over him to wash away the&nbsp;
evils of the past. As darkness descended, oil

02:46:38.960 --> 02:46:47.680
lamps were lit around the palace, a spectacular&nbsp;
sight. At the striking of a gong, Gajah Mada

02:46:47.680 --> 02:47:02.560
led the senior ministers to a large pavilion in&nbsp;
front of the palace gates before a growing crowd.

02:47:02.560 --> 02:47:10.399
Hayam Wuruk was a multi-talented king. He was&nbsp;
apparently an accomplished singer and dancer, and

02:47:10.399 --> 02:47:18.719
a lover of shadow puppet plays. The poet of the&nbsp;
Deshawarnana describes the auspicious omens that

02:47:18.720 --> 02:47:28.880
took place upon the birth of this prince, as the&nbsp;
island's great volcanoes rumbled their approval.

02:47:28.880 --> 02:47:35.840
At his birth in the shaka-year Seasons Arrow&nbsp;
Suns, the prince was already destined to be king.

02:47:35.840 --> 02:47:41.279
The signs of his miraculous and extraordinary&nbsp;
nature were that the Earth quaked and rumbled.

02:47:41.279 --> 02:47:47.279
There was a rain of ash. Thunder and lightning&nbsp;
zigzagged through the sky. Mount Kampud erupted,

02:47:47.279 --> 02:47:53.439
and the wretched evildoers around it were&nbsp;
annihilated and died without a gasp. Such is the

02:47:53.439 --> 02:48:03.200
nature of his reign that the whole of the land of&nbsp;
Java has been humbled and bows down in submission.

02:48:03.200 --> 02:48:08.240
Like her mother before her, Tribhuwana&nbsp;
did not completely vanish from political

02:48:08.240 --> 02:48:13.840
life when she stepped away from her&nbsp;
throne. Throughout the rest of her life,

02:48:13.840 --> 02:48:19.120
she would act as a counsellor and&nbsp;
advisor for her son, and together

02:48:19.120 --> 02:48:28.399
they would usher in what is widely regarded&nbsp;
as the golden age of the empire of Majapahit.

02:48:28.399 --> 02:48:34.799
When Hayam Wuruk came to the throne, he&nbsp;
inherited a capital city of no little

02:48:34.800 --> 02:48:42.240
magnificence. Over the preceding decades, it&nbsp;
had been the site of constant construction,

02:48:42.240 --> 02:48:49.840
and now bloomed with palaces and temples complete&nbsp;
with pillared audience halls and pavilions.

02:48:50.399 --> 02:48:57.200
In this era, all roads led to Majapahit,&nbsp;
and its status as a crossroads of Java

02:48:57.200 --> 02:49:08.240
wasn't just a metaphor. The epic poem&nbsp;
Deshawarnana describes the royal palace.

02:49:08.240 --> 02:49:12.399
Let us describe the layout of the&nbsp;
awe-inspiring royal palace. Its

02:49:12.399 --> 02:49:17.839
wall is of red brick. It goes right around&nbsp;
and is thick and high. On the western side,

02:49:17.840 --> 02:49:23.600
the palace mouth faces onto the great square in&nbsp;
the midst of deep encircling with buddhi trees in

02:49:23.600 --> 02:49:29.520
rows and neatly shaped, all thick with&nbsp;
festoons. On the north is the ceremonial gate,

02:49:29.520 --> 02:49:35.439
splendid and extraordinary, its doors of&nbsp;
iron adorned with countless designs. To the

02:49:35.439 --> 02:49:41.120
south of this is the crossroads,&nbsp;
otherworldly in its elegance.

02:49:42.960 --> 02:49:50.640
From these crossroads, great highways snaked&nbsp;
off into the country in all directions.

02:49:51.520 --> 02:49:58.080
It was part of the usual ceremony of kingship&nbsp;
that the king of Majapahit would routinely make

02:49:58.080 --> 02:50:06.160
journeys around the whole of Java, meeting his&nbsp;
citizens, and seeing the sights of the land,

02:50:06.160 --> 02:50:15.040
visiting villages, holy shrines, and reminding&nbsp;
vassal kingdoms about who was in charge.

02:50:16.240 --> 02:50:24.080
It was on one of these journeys that King&nbsp;
Hayam Wuruk brought his court poet, a man

02:50:24.080 --> 02:50:33.279
named Prapanca, who would go on to be the author&nbsp;
of the poem Deshawarnana. Prapanca speaks of the

02:50:33.279 --> 02:50:42.479
honor in his poem, describing himself in the third&nbsp;
person. In the shaka-year Moon Snake Sun, in the

02:50:42.479 --> 02:50:49.120
month of Bhadra-pada, at the fullness of the moon,&nbsp;
King Hayam Wuruk went on a journey to Lamajang to

02:50:49.120 --> 02:50:55.200
take note of all there was to see. All the princes&nbsp;
of Java with their wives again went with the king,

02:50:55.200 --> 02:51:00.319
with servants and vehicles, officials&nbsp;
and officers from the whole of Majapahit,

02:51:00.319 --> 02:51:05.519
and in particular the king's priests and court&nbsp;
poets accompanied him. So this man by the pen

02:51:05.520 --> 02:51:13.840
name of Prapanca joined in the pursuit of&nbsp;
beauty, attending at the feet of the king.

02:51:13.840 --> 02:51:20.560
According to his own account, Prapanca seems&nbsp;
to have embarked on writing the Deshawarnana

02:51:20.560 --> 02:51:28.319
because he felt unable to write the kind of poetry&nbsp;
that the more senior court poets were producing,

02:51:28.319 --> 02:51:37.599
usually heroic epics about the Hindu gods,&nbsp;
as he describes in the following extract.

02:51:37.600 --> 02:51:42.640
His motive was, after all, to follow the king&nbsp;
wherever he went in the hope of being favored.

02:51:42.640 --> 02:51:46.240
But he still does not know how to&nbsp;
achieve poetic effects, and is most

02:51:46.240 --> 02:51:51.599
unlikely to succeed in composing a song on the&nbsp;
writing tablet. And this is the reason why he

02:51:51.600 --> 02:51:57.840
describes all the places passed through and&nbsp;
sets out their names in order along the road.

02:51:59.600 --> 02:52:06.880
Ironically, the Deshawarnana is today one of the&nbsp;
most famous pieces of literature to survive from

02:52:06.880 --> 02:52:15.439
the Javanese golden era, and it is by far the&nbsp;
most useful to historians. Today, the poem is a

02:52:15.439 --> 02:52:23.759
remarkable source that captures a snapshot of life&nbsp;
on Java during the reign of Hayam Wuruk. And for

02:52:23.760 --> 02:52:33.359
all of this, we have the poet Prapanca's apparent&nbsp;
inferiority complex to thank. Along their journey,

02:52:33.359 --> 02:52:42.080
Prapanca watched the king carefully and noted&nbsp;
down the beauty of the land they passed through.

02:52:42.080 --> 02:52:48.240
At Dampar and at Patun-jungan, the king relaxed&nbsp;
and made his way along the beach. To the east,

02:52:48.240 --> 02:52:52.399
his path followed the level sands, which was&nbsp;
so firm that the carriages could drive over

02:52:52.399 --> 02:52:57.599
them. He made a halt on the banks of a lake&nbsp;
thick with white and red lotuses in flower,

02:52:57.600 --> 02:53:01.760
and was absorbed in watching the giant&nbsp;
shrimps moving about in the limpid water,

02:53:01.760 --> 02:53:12.960
clearly visible in the depths. But the&nbsp;
royal journey wasn't always an easy one.

02:53:12.960 --> 02:53:18.560
In his text, we get a sense for the&nbsp;
challenges of moving over land during

02:53:18.560 --> 02:53:25.439
the rainy season. The road, just at the&nbsp;
point where it runs northward from the sea,

02:53:25.439 --> 02:53:31.359
over its whole length was rough and narrow. On&nbsp;
top of this, it rained, so that the steep places

02:53:31.359 --> 02:53:38.559
became slippery, and some of the carriages&nbsp;
came loose and collided with each other.

02:53:38.560 --> 02:53:44.240
Due to these challenges, travel by road&nbsp;
was largely only possible during the dry

02:53:44.240 --> 02:53:49.840
season. For the rest of the year,&nbsp;
goods were transported by river,

02:53:49.840 --> 02:53:59.520
taking rice and other export crops downstream&nbsp;
to the great port cities on barges. These boats

02:53:59.520 --> 02:54:07.121
would then sail back up the rivers with luxury&nbsp;
goods imported from China, India, and elsewhere.

02:54:08.080 --> 02:54:14.559
These port cities were the beating heart of&nbsp;
the Majapahit Empire, and the large numbers

02:54:14.560 --> 02:54:25.279
of Chinese coins found in their archaeological&nbsp;
sites testify to the booming trade that went on.

02:54:25.279 --> 02:54:30.880
At each of these ports, the sails of&nbsp;
hundreds of ships would be visible,

02:54:30.880 --> 02:54:38.160
coming and going with countless trade goods. And&nbsp;
among these, the most lucrative were the spices

02:54:38.160 --> 02:54:44.240
grown on the islands of the archipelago&nbsp;
that were prized all around the world,

02:54:44.240 --> 02:55:02.399
such as pepper, cardamom, and ginger. But&nbsp;
the rarest of all were cloves and nutmeg.

02:55:02.399 --> 02:55:09.519
Cloves and nutmeg were brought to Majapahit on&nbsp;
large ships that sailed from a small group of

02:55:09.520 --> 02:55:21.279
islands which are called the islands of Malaku,&nbsp;
and would come to be known as the Spice Islands.

02:55:21.279 --> 02:55:28.960
While cloves were grown all over these islands,&nbsp;
nutmeg was extremely limited in cultivation,

02:55:28.960 --> 02:55:37.439
occurring only on ten islands in the tiny&nbsp;
archipelago of Banda. Wild nutmeg had long

02:55:37.439 --> 02:55:44.319
been harvested by the peoples native to the Banda&nbsp;
Islands, but it was a particularly difficult plant

02:55:44.319 --> 02:55:52.960
to cultivate. Nutmeg seeds will fail to germinate&nbsp;
if they aren't planted in soil within a week,

02:55:52.960 --> 02:55:59.279
making them difficult to transport by sea,&nbsp;
and the particular environment of volcanic

02:55:59.279 --> 02:56:06.319
soil that they evolved in means that they are&nbsp;
fragile and difficult to nurture to full size.

02:56:07.520 --> 02:56:12.960
As a result of these difficulties, the&nbsp;
Banda islanders claimed that nutmeg was

02:56:12.960 --> 02:56:19.840
simply a gift of the gods and could not&nbsp;
be cultivated by human hands. Instead,

02:56:19.840 --> 02:56:26.319
they would travel to the parts of the forest&nbsp;
where it grew wild to collect its prized seeds,

02:56:26.319 --> 02:56:32.240
and they kept the location of these&nbsp;
groves a carefully guarded secret.

02:56:33.680 --> 02:56:40.240
But despite its extreme rarity, the fragrant&nbsp;
nutmeg seeds that these islanders harvested

02:56:40.240 --> 02:56:47.679
traveled all around the world. The spice&nbsp;
would reach Constantinople in the 6th century,

02:56:47.680 --> 02:56:52.800
and instantly became a sought-after&nbsp;
commodity and mark of luxury.

02:56:53.359 --> 02:57:01.120
Nutmeg would even be mentioned as a spice to&nbsp;
flavor English ale in Chaucer's Canterbury Tales,

02:57:01.120 --> 02:57:08.000
which was written during the time of the&nbsp;
Majapahit Empire. At times nutmeg would

02:57:08.000 --> 02:57:18.880
sell on the other side of the world for&nbsp;
more than an equivalent weight of gold.

02:57:18.880 --> 02:57:24.160
As a result of this global trade, the&nbsp;
rulers of the Banda Islands would come

02:57:24.160 --> 02:57:30.399
to be known around the region as&nbsp;
the orang kaya , or rich men , and

02:57:30.399 --> 02:57:38.479
the entirety of this trade passed&nbsp;
through the major ports of Majapahit.

02:57:38.479 --> 02:57:45.679
To transport these goods across the seas and&nbsp;
oceans, the sailors of Majapahit voyaged in

02:57:45.680 --> 02:57:53.520
large vessels known as djongs. These&nbsp;
were the descendants of the Borobudur

02:57:53.520 --> 02:57:59.840
ships that had sailed to Madagascar and&nbsp;
Eastern Africa in the preceding centuries,

02:57:59.840 --> 02:58:06.800
and by this time, some of these ships had&nbsp;
reportedly grown to phenomenal proportions.

02:58:08.880 --> 02:58:16.319
Reports on the true size of these vessels vary,&nbsp;
but some sources record djongs that reached a

02:58:16.319 --> 02:58:24.479
size of 80 meters long. They were built with&nbsp;
layered hulls that made them exceptionally strong,

02:58:24.479 --> 02:58:31.359
and were able to transport a thousand soldiers&nbsp;
and more than a thousand tons of cargo.

02:58:31.920 --> 02:58:38.479
The sight of these massive djongs astonished&nbsp;
European explorers when they arrived in the

02:58:38.479 --> 02:58:48.000
archipelago in the 16th century. One Portuguese&nbsp;
admiral and explorer, Alfonso de Albuquerque,

02:58:48.000 --> 02:58:54.319
wrote about encountering one of&nbsp;
these enormous vessels in 1511.

02:58:55.680 --> 02:59:01.279
The whole fleet went back along the coast&nbsp;
in the same order as they had first come,

02:59:01.279 --> 02:59:06.240
and when they were in the latitude of&nbsp;
the Powder Island, they sighted two very

02:59:06.240 --> 02:59:12.000
large junks and gave chase to them.&nbsp;
One of these, which was from India,

02:59:12.000 --> 02:59:18.399
surrendered immediately, but&nbsp;
the other junk would not do so.

02:59:20.960 --> 02:59:29.040
The Portuguese gave chase to the massive Javanese&nbsp;
ship and made several attempts to board it,

02:59:29.040 --> 02:59:37.519
but these were all unsuccessful. The Europeans&nbsp;
even opened fire on the djong with their cannons,

02:59:37.520 --> 02:59:44.240
but found that the three or four layers of wood&nbsp;
with which the Javanese built their ships simply

02:59:44.240 --> 02:59:54.160
absorbed the cannon fire, and the vast vessel&nbsp;
sailed on. In a letter written sometime later,

02:59:54.160 --> 03:00:05.840
one of the Portuguese captains, Fernao Pires de&nbsp;
Andrade, describes the massive Javanese ship.

03:00:05.840 --> 03:00:12.000
The junk was the largest seen by men of&nbsp;
these parts so far. It carried a thousand

03:00:12.000 --> 03:00:17.600
fighting men on board, and your lordship can&nbsp;
believe me that it was an amazing thing to see,

03:00:17.600 --> 03:00:22.479
because beside it, our own vessel, the&nbsp;
Anunciada, did not look like a ship at

03:00:22.479 --> 03:00:27.919
all. We attacked it with bombards, but&nbsp;
even the shots of the largest cannon did

03:00:27.920 --> 03:00:38.479
not pierce it below the waterline. It was so&nbsp;
monstrous that no man had ever seen the like.

03:00:38.479 --> 03:00:45.519
Eventually, the smaller and more maneuverable&nbsp;
Portuguese ships were able to disable the larger

03:00:45.520 --> 03:00:54.400
djong by breaking its two massive rudders. This&nbsp;
finally brought the men on board to surrender.

03:00:55.040 --> 03:01:00.880
The Europeans were so impressed with the size&nbsp;
of these Javanese djongs that they sailed one

03:01:00.880 --> 03:01:07.840
of them all the way back home, where it was used&nbsp;
for a time as a warship in the fleet guarding

03:01:07.840 --> 03:01:16.800
the Gibraltar Strait. Although we have little&nbsp;
reliable data on when the ships of the Javanese

03:01:16.800 --> 03:01:22.720
reached such a massive scale, it is reasonable&nbsp;
to assume that they were at least approaching

03:01:22.720 --> 03:01:33.439
that size during the reign of Hayam Wuruk,&nbsp;
when the power of Majapahit was at its peak.

03:01:33.439 --> 03:01:39.839
These enormous ships would have been the&nbsp;
crucial backbone of the Majapahit Empire,

03:01:39.840 --> 03:01:47.840
with their enormous capacity for cargo and their&nbsp;
ability to transport thousands of men, horses,

03:01:47.840 --> 03:01:54.960
and even elephants from island to island&nbsp;
wherever they were needed. And of course,

03:01:54.960 --> 03:02:06.080
they would have made a powerful statement about&nbsp;
the power and prestige of the empire of Majapahit.

03:02:06.080 --> 03:02:12.479
By now, the Majapahit sphere of influence&nbsp;
stretched over the sea for thousands of

03:02:12.479 --> 03:02:18.479
kilometers, and they had monopolized the&nbsp;
lucrative spice routes from the Malaku

03:02:18.479 --> 03:02:27.439
Islands so that their wealth would have swelled to&nbsp;
previously unimagined heights. Over this period,

03:02:27.439 --> 03:02:34.799
Majapahit began to take on the form of&nbsp;
what has been called a mandala empire.

03:02:47.200 --> 03:02:55.359
In Sanskrit, the word mandala means circle , and&nbsp;
it has come to describe a style of traditional

03:02:55.359 --> 03:03:03.279
geometric pattern used to represent a kind of&nbsp;
map of the universe. Reflected in the shapes

03:03:03.279 --> 03:03:10.800
of the Buddhist temples at Sewu and Borobudur,&nbsp;
these are designed to help an adherent achieve

03:03:10.800 --> 03:03:18.560
a state of meditation. Although the forms of&nbsp;
these mandalas differ, many show the sacred

03:03:18.560 --> 03:03:26.640
mountain Meru at the center of the world,&nbsp;
sometimes surrounded by the world ocean.

03:03:26.640 --> 03:03:33.439
Around this is a square palace with four gates,&nbsp;
and then a circle representing the continents

03:03:33.439 --> 03:03:41.279
outside it, the heavens and the world of the&nbsp;
gods forming the outside of the pattern. The

03:03:41.279 --> 03:03:48.800
mandala presents the vision of a world ordered in&nbsp;
a strict hierarchy radiating out from the center,

03:03:48.800 --> 03:03:55.440
and so, it's no surprise it has long been&nbsp;
used as a way of visualizing royal power.

03:03:56.319 --> 03:04:03.759
As early as the 4th century BC, the Indian&nbsp;
writer Kautilya, in his work the Arthshastra,

03:04:03.760 --> 03:04:11.200
described what he called the Raja Mandala, or&nbsp;
Mandala of Kings, in which a powerful ruler

03:04:11.200 --> 03:04:19.279
will bring other less powerful ones under his&nbsp;
control. This idea has become popular among modern

03:04:19.279 --> 03:04:25.599
historians seeking to understand how Southeast&nbsp;
Asian states of this time were organized,

03:04:25.600 --> 03:04:30.240
and as some evidence that this is also how&nbsp;
the Javanese of the time would have thought

03:04:30.240 --> 03:04:39.439
of their world. The epic poem the Deshawarnana&nbsp;
describes the structure of the Majapahit Empire

03:04:39.439 --> 03:04:50.879
like the solar system, with power radiating&nbsp;
out from the center like the rays of the sun.

03:04:54.000 --> 03:04:59.439
The royal compound at Majapahit the incomparable&nbsp;
is as sun and the moon, and the courtyards of

03:04:59.439 --> 03:05:05.200
the many quarters, each with its own particular&nbsp;
charm, are rays of light in relation to it. Like

03:05:05.200 --> 03:05:11.200
the stars and planets are the various remaining&nbsp;
cities, Daha the foremost, and the other islands

03:05:11.200 --> 03:05:18.319
and all the surrounding countries which seek&nbsp;
refuge and in numbers come into the presence.

03:05:25.840 --> 03:05:35.279
It's still an open question exactly how much power&nbsp;
Majapahit exerted over its vassal states. Some

03:05:35.279 --> 03:05:43.840
empires like the Roman Empire ruled with direct&nbsp;
control. When they conquered a new area, they

03:05:43.840 --> 03:05:51.040
erased previous forms of government and social&nbsp;
structures, and imposed their own on absorbed

03:05:51.040 --> 03:05:59.840
territories. But we don't see much evidence&nbsp;
of that in the case of Majapahit. Subordinate

03:05:59.840 --> 03:06:07.840
kings and rulers on the outer islands certainly&nbsp;
paid symbolic homage to the king in Majapahit.

03:06:08.720 --> 03:06:15.520
They likely provided an agreed upon tribute each&nbsp;
year and a certain number of soldiers and ships

03:06:15.520 --> 03:06:22.800
for any military action undertaken by the empire.&nbsp;
But beyond that, it's likely that the lords of

03:06:22.800 --> 03:06:29.279
each island and tributary kingdom continued to&nbsp;
exert more or less total control over their own

03:06:29.279 --> 03:06:39.279
affairs. This arrangement suited everyone well.&nbsp;
Cultural identities on each individual island

03:06:39.279 --> 03:06:46.719
were relatively distinct and deeply entrenched.&nbsp;
Local languages and customs were different on each

03:06:46.720 --> 03:06:53.600
island, and few people around the region wanted&nbsp;
to be directly ruled by a Javanese governor,

03:06:53.600 --> 03:07:00.800
or to have Javanese troops stationed in&nbsp;
their hometowns. And for the Majapahit,

03:07:00.800 --> 03:07:06.399
appointing governors and stationing troops to&nbsp;
put down rebellions and uprisings would have

03:07:06.399 --> 03:07:13.839
been an unwelcome expense. The mandala&nbsp;
structure was cheap and efficient.

03:07:14.560 --> 03:07:19.840
While times were good and the empire was&nbsp;
strong, it ensured a maximum level of

03:07:19.840 --> 03:07:27.680
compliance and cooperation from subjects with the&nbsp;
lightest possible touch, and allowed everyone to

03:07:27.680 --> 03:07:36.960
profit from the great traffic of trade that&nbsp;
flowed along the great arteries of the sea.

03:07:36.960 --> 03:07:43.680
From the center of the mandala in Majapahit,&nbsp;
fleets of djong ships sailed back and forth

03:07:43.680 --> 03:07:53.840
among the islands, bringing trade and tribute&nbsp;
back to its booming ports. But as with any empire,

03:07:53.840 --> 03:08:05.200
sometimes its subjects had to be kept&nbsp;
in line, as the Deshawarnana describes.

03:08:10.000 --> 03:08:14.800
The other countries, all that were under the&nbsp;
care of Java, obeyed the king's commands,

03:08:14.800 --> 03:08:20.640
and each was true in what it did. Furthermore,&nbsp;
any that transgressed his commands was attacked

03:08:20.640 --> 03:08:29.920
and wiped out completely by the squadrons&nbsp;
of the various valiant naval officers.

03:08:41.760 --> 03:08:46.560
One such example occurred in the year 1375,

03:08:46.560 --> 03:08:56.640
when one of the subject kings of the empire&nbsp;
seemed to get ideas above his station.

03:09:04.000 --> 03:09:10.479
The troublesome king in question was a man&nbsp;
named Adityawarman, who had been put in power

03:09:10.479 --> 03:09:20.240
by Majapahit on the large island of Sumatra, a&nbsp;
short hop over the sea from Java. Adityawarman

03:09:20.240 --> 03:09:27.200
was something of a narcissist. He was fond&nbsp;
of commissioning massive statues of himself

03:09:27.200 --> 03:09:33.519
larger than any that have been uncovered of other&nbsp;
kings in the region, and over the preceding years,

03:09:33.520 --> 03:09:43.200
he had begun to act in an insubordinate manner.&nbsp;
At one point he had a stone tablet inscribed that

03:09:43.200 --> 03:09:53.080
showed a list of kings, and he had the insolence&nbsp;
to place his own name above King Hayam Wuruk s.

03:09:53.680 --> 03:10:01.520
This all would have caused rumbles of&nbsp;
consternation at the court of Majapahit.

03:10:01.520 --> 03:10:10.000
But Adityawarman would go too far when he sent his&nbsp;
own envoys to the court of the emperor of China,

03:10:10.000 --> 03:10:18.399
bypassing his king completely. One&nbsp;
Javanese epic poem, the Brata-Yuda,

03:10:18.399 --> 03:10:25.679
describes the rage of the demon king Ravana upon&nbsp;
getting news of the death of his sons. But we

03:10:25.680 --> 03:10:32.240
might imagine it a fitting description of Hayam&nbsp;
Wuruk's reaction when he heard that Adityawarman

03:10:32.240 --> 03:10:41.599
had been going over his head and making overtures&nbsp;
to China without consulting him. The monarch was

03:10:41.600 --> 03:10:50.000
furiously enraged. He gnawed his moustaches, his&nbsp;
countenance was inflamed, and his bosom red as the

03:10:50.000 --> 03:10:58.319
wawawari flower. Sweat exuded from every pore.&nbsp;
The angles of his mouth trembled. His eyelashes

03:10:58.319 --> 03:11:08.799
stuck together. He vaunted. He called aloud. He&nbsp;
challenged all his enemies to meet him at once.

03:11:08.800 --> 03:11:15.680
Hayam Wuruk summoned the Chinese ambassador to&nbsp;
Sumatra, and after lambasting him, apparently

03:11:15.680 --> 03:11:25.279
had the man beheaded and his head sent back to&nbsp;
China in a bag. In perhaps a sign of the power of

03:11:25.279 --> 03:11:34.319
Majapahit at this time, the Chinese didn't respond&nbsp;
in kind. Instead, they bashfully apologized for

03:11:34.319 --> 03:11:41.359
their mistake, and would eventually send a gift&nbsp;
of fine fabrics and a batch of paper money to

03:11:41.359 --> 03:11:51.439
the Javanese court to make amends. But from the&nbsp;
vain king Adityawarman, no apology would suffice.

03:11:52.240 --> 03:12:00.399
King Hayam Wuruk amassed his army, as many as&nbsp;
300 warships, and according to the chronicles,

03:12:00.399 --> 03:12:08.799
200,000 soldiers to crush this upstart king.&nbsp;
And although we don't hear what happens to him,

03:12:08.800 --> 03:12:16.800
Adityawarman never appears in any other&nbsp;
written records or inscriptions again.

03:12:18.560 --> 03:12:26.720
Now Hayam Wuruk was on the war path. After&nbsp;
crushing Adityawarman, the Majapahit fleet

03:12:26.720 --> 03:12:35.520
rounded on another problematic vassal. This&nbsp;
man ruled the walled settlement of Singapura,

03:12:35.520 --> 03:12:44.720
now the modern city of Singapore, and his name&nbsp;
was Parameswara. After a month-long siege,

03:12:44.720 --> 03:12:53.279
the Majapahit forces broke through the walls&nbsp;
and slaughtered the inhabitants. To this day,

03:12:53.279 --> 03:13:00.960
legend has it that the iron-rich reddish clay&nbsp;
soil of Singapore was stained that color by the

03:13:00.960 --> 03:13:10.800
blood spilled during this massacre. Parameswara,&nbsp;
the king of Singapura, fled north to the area of

03:13:10.800 --> 03:13:31.920
the Malacca Strait, but he would remain a thorn&nbsp;
in the side of Majapahit for some time to come.

03:13:35.439 --> 03:13:41.679
The Adityawarman incident shows that the&nbsp;
empire of Majapahit was held together by

03:13:41.680 --> 03:13:48.880
trade and diplomacy and the promise&nbsp;
of mutual peace and prosperity. But

03:13:48.880 --> 03:13:56.239
it was also very much backed by the&nbsp;
threat of force. Over this period,

03:13:56.239 --> 03:14:04.800
the royal capital would flourish. But this&nbsp;
golden age of Majapahit could not last forever,

03:14:04.800 --> 03:14:25.520
and its end would come about after the&nbsp;
death of its last great king, Hayam Wuruk.

03:14:25.520 --> 03:14:31.040
King Hayam Wuruk died likely in the year 1389,

03:14:31.040 --> 03:14:38.960
and with him the light began to&nbsp;
leave the court of Majapahit.

03:14:38.960 --> 03:14:47.200
The great statesman and general Gajah Mada had&nbsp;
died twenty-five years earlier, and Hayam Wuruk's

03:14:47.200 --> 03:14:54.319
mother, Queen Tribhuwana Wijayatungadewi,&nbsp;
had also died a decade or so after that.

03:14:55.520 --> 03:15:04.960
There now seems to have been something of&nbsp;
a vacuum of talent in the Majapahit court.

03:15:04.960 --> 03:15:11.920
Exacerbating the problem was the complexity of&nbsp;
Javanese succession laws, which had developed to

03:15:11.920 --> 03:15:19.920
deal with the fact that kings of this time always&nbsp;
had multiple wives. In ideal circumstances, the

03:15:19.920 --> 03:15:27.279
law would unambiguously favor one successor over&nbsp;
another, and there would be no confusion about who

03:15:27.279 --> 03:15:38.719
would take the throne. But fate had dealt Hayam&nbsp;
Wuruk a bad hand when it came to his succession.

03:15:42.319 --> 03:15:46.000
He had failed to have a son by his principal wife,

03:15:46.000 --> 03:15:51.840
and so his closest legal heir was&nbsp;
therefore a woman named Kusumawardhani,

03:15:51.840 --> 03:15:59.439
his daughter by a lower-ranking wife. She&nbsp;
had married a man named Wikramawardhana,

03:15:59.439 --> 03:16:07.839
and so it seemed likely that he would become king&nbsp;
despite having only married into the royal family.

03:16:07.840 --> 03:16:16.319
The Deshawarnana describes this couple in&nbsp;
glowing terms. They have as daughter Princess

03:16:16.319 --> 03:16:22.239
Kusumawardhani, a blameless young princess who's&nbsp;
paid homage in the royal compound of Kabalan and

03:16:22.239 --> 03:16:28.000
is of the utmost charm. Prince Wikramawardhana&nbsp;
will be her proper match, as everyone says they

03:16:28.000 --> 03:16:34.319
will be like a god and goddess when they marry&nbsp;
later, bringing happiness to people's hearts.

03:16:36.239 --> 03:16:44.719
But Hayam Wuruk did also have a son by a&nbsp;
secondary, less-senior wife. In the histories,

03:16:44.720 --> 03:16:53.920
he is known only by the title Bhre Wirabhumi,&nbsp;
or the lord of a place called Wirabhumi. Being a

03:16:53.920 --> 03:17:01.600
man and the direct descendant of Hayam Wuruk also&nbsp;
gave him a strong claim to the throne despite his

03:17:01.600 --> 03:17:10.560
mother being of a lower rank than Kusumawardhani&nbsp;
s, and this situation of two people having a

03:17:10.560 --> 03:17:20.000
comparable claim was a recipe for disaster. Hayam&nbsp;
Wuruk seems to have sensed the danger while he was

03:17:20.000 --> 03:17:28.640
still alive. In an attempt to remedy the situation&nbsp;
and cool off the rivalry between these two men,

03:17:28.640 --> 03:17:36.160
he divided the empire into the Western Court, the&nbsp;
capital of Majapahit, and the Eastern Court, where

03:17:36.160 --> 03:17:45.120
the lord of Wirabhumi held sway. The exact details&nbsp;
of this arrangement are unknown, but it was a

03:17:45.120 --> 03:17:54.319
clear and obvious rift now splitting the kingdom's&nbsp;
Javanese heartlands in two. The situation seems to

03:17:54.319 --> 03:18:01.439
have gotten so bad that at one point, the Chinese&nbsp;
chronicle, the Ming Shilu, notes that the two

03:18:01.439 --> 03:18:10.319
different courts were each sending their envoys to&nbsp;
meet independently with the emperor's ambassadors.

03:18:10.319 --> 03:18:17.279
Like the rumblings and earthquakes before&nbsp;
a great eruption, the signs of trouble must

03:18:17.279 --> 03:18:23.200
have been obvious to anyone who was paying&nbsp;
attention, and in the years that followed,

03:18:23.200 --> 03:18:44.639
the rift would tear apart the land&nbsp;
of Java and the empire it ruled.

03:18:50.800 --> 03:18:59.120
The name for the conflict that follows is&nbsp;
found in the chronicle of the Pararaton.

03:18:59.120 --> 03:19:07.359
There it is called the Regreg, a word that in&nbsp;
Javanese means bit by bit , and can be used

03:19:07.359 --> 03:19:16.799
to describe something falling apart piece by&nbsp;
piece. As we will see, this was a fitting name,

03:19:16.800 --> 03:19:24.239
as the conflict would bring about the steady&nbsp;
coming apart of the mandala empire of Majapahit

03:19:24.239 --> 03:19:38.399
that had held together over the last century. The&nbsp;
Pararaton describes the conflict that unfolded.

03:19:40.880 --> 03:19:49.200
They came to blows. There was division, and&nbsp;
men died in the division. After three years,

03:19:49.200 --> 03:19:55.679
there then arose again the Regreg War.&nbsp;
Both sides prepared themselves. All the

03:19:55.680 --> 03:20:05.280
lords asked themselves, "Whom shall&nbsp;
I follow?" Then the fighting began.

03:20:09.760 --> 03:20:17.520
The Regreg War would rage for two years, and&nbsp;
over this time, neither side could gain the

03:20:17.520 --> 03:20:29.200
upper hand. The Pararaton describes the&nbsp;
back and forth nature of the fighting.

03:20:29.200 --> 03:20:36.239
First the Western palace was defeated. Their&nbsp;
lords were hard pressed. In distress they wished

03:20:36.239 --> 03:20:49.200
to flee. Then they said, "Do not flee yet. Let us&nbsp;
fight them." Then the Eastern palace was defeated.

03:20:49.200 --> 03:20:54.880
The strength of the mandala empire&nbsp;
system was in how light a hold it

03:20:54.880 --> 03:21:01.760
exerted on its vassals. It was a cheap and&nbsp;
efficient way to run an imperial system,

03:21:02.319 --> 03:21:13.759
but this strength in times of stability&nbsp;
became a weakness when times got hard.

03:21:13.760 --> 03:21:21.200
For the kings who swore allegiance to Majapahit,&nbsp;
it was all too easy when the empire was distracted

03:21:21.200 --> 03:21:30.000
to simply turn away and quietly stop&nbsp;
paying its fees or sending it troops.

03:21:30.000 --> 03:21:37.439
With the power of Majapahit's fleet and army now&nbsp;
focused entirely on the war between its Western

03:21:37.439 --> 03:21:46.239
and Eastern courts, many of its formerly most&nbsp;
loyal subjects simply slipped out of its grasp.

03:21:47.120 --> 03:21:53.760
One of these subjects was in the region&nbsp;
of Malayu, which during the Regreg War,

03:21:53.760 --> 03:22:05.359
drifted under the sovereignty of the powerful&nbsp;
kingdom of Ayutthaya in what is now Thailand.

03:22:05.359 --> 03:22:13.599
On the island of Sumatra, a Chinese pirate named&nbsp;
Chen Zuyi from Guangdong even took advantage

03:22:13.600 --> 03:22:22.479
of the chaos caused by the Regreg War to seize&nbsp;
control of the wealthy port city of Palembang.

03:22:22.479 --> 03:22:29.359
This man would run Palembang as a pirate&nbsp;
fortress for the better part of a decade,

03:22:29.359 --> 03:22:35.599
using it as a base to raid the wealthy shipping&nbsp;
that passed through the Malacca Strait, and

03:22:35.600 --> 03:22:44.640
using forged bronze seals to create counterfeit&nbsp;
licenses and aid smuggling into and out of China.

03:22:45.600 --> 03:22:51.600
The Chinese-Muslim writer Ma Huan&nbsp;
wrote about the situation in his work,

03:22:51.600 --> 03:22:56.399
A Survey of the Ocean Shores.

03:22:56.399 --> 03:23:07.599
Chen Zuyi plundered ships and prayed on both&nbsp;
native and foreign merchants for several years.

03:23:07.600 --> 03:23:14.720
On the northern coast of Borneo, the Brunei&nbsp;
Kingdom had also begun to ignore the overlordship

03:23:14.720 --> 03:23:24.720
of Majapahit, and by 1405, Western Borneo had&nbsp;
also drifted away. These small kingdoms would

03:23:24.720 --> 03:23:31.600
instead seek protection under the influence of&nbsp;
another power that was now flexing its muscles

03:23:31.600 --> 03:23:57.439
with increasing confidence on the waves.&nbsp;
That was the rising Ming Dynasty of China.

03:23:57.439 --> 03:24:04.559
By this time, the Mongol Yuan Dynasty had&nbsp;
collapsed, and the Mongols had been forcibly

03:24:04.560 --> 03:24:13.201
thrown out of China. Now, a new dynasty&nbsp;
known as the Ming had taken its place.

03:24:14.640 --> 03:24:19.279
The Ming ruled over a devastated&nbsp;
land that had suffered immensely

03:24:19.279 --> 03:24:25.599
over the years of Mongol conquest&nbsp;
and misrule by the Yuan emperors,

03:24:25.600 --> 03:24:36.479
exacerbated by the ravages of the Black&nbsp;
Death. But now, China was rebuilding.

03:24:36.479 --> 03:24:42.959
Ultimately, the Ming Dynasty has been remembered&nbsp;
as a paranoid and inward-looking state,

03:24:42.960 --> 03:24:49.760
which virtually bankrupted itself with the&nbsp;
construction of its vast Great Wall in the north,

03:24:49.760 --> 03:24:56.319
sealing itself off from the world.&nbsp;
But at the start of the 15th century,

03:24:56.319 --> 03:25:02.080
Ming China looked quite different.&nbsp;
This was partly due to the reign

03:25:02.080 --> 03:25:27.519
of an ambitious and outward-looking&nbsp;
ruler known as the Yongle Emperor.

03:25:27.520 --> 03:25:35.359
The Yongle Emperor was a patron of learning, and&nbsp;
sponsored the writing of a great encyclopedia

03:25:35.359 --> 03:25:43.439
made up of 23,000 written scrolls in an&nbsp;
attempt to record all the world's knowledge.

03:25:44.239 --> 03:25:50.319
He was fascinated by everything, and&nbsp;
especially the world outside of China,

03:25:50.319 --> 03:25:58.080
as he recorded in his work Ming Taizong Baoxun,&nbsp;
or the Valuable Teachings of Emperor Yongle.

03:26:01.040 --> 03:26:06.399
Whenever I can find the time, I read history&nbsp;
books and the classics so as to avoid

03:26:06.399 --> 03:26:13.040
idol living. I constantly remind myself that&nbsp;
the world is so vast and state affairs so

03:26:13.040 --> 03:26:18.560
important that I cannot succumb to&nbsp;
laziness and complacency for even a

03:26:18.560 --> 03:26:32.399
moment. Once one has succumbed to laziness and&nbsp;
complacency, everything will become stagnant.

03:26:32.399 --> 03:26:39.120
As a result of this fascination with the outer&nbsp;
world, throughout his reign, he sent out a flurry

03:26:39.120 --> 03:26:46.960
of diplomatic missions to all corners of Asia,&nbsp;
letting everyone know that China was once again

03:26:46.960 --> 03:26:54.560
open for business. And as part of this effort,&nbsp;
he ordered the construction of a grand fleet

03:26:54.560 --> 03:27:11.359
designed to sail around the region and remind&nbsp;
the people of every land of the might of China.

03:27:11.359 --> 03:27:18.799
To build his fleet, the Yongle Emperor ordered&nbsp;
the construction of a vast shipyard at a place

03:27:18.800 --> 03:27:27.439
called Longjiang, or Dragon River. This&nbsp;
sprawling industrial complex was built at

03:27:27.439 --> 03:27:37.040
the point where the Yangtze River is met by the&nbsp;
smaller Qinhuai in the Ming capital of Nanjing.

03:27:37.040 --> 03:27:45.600
And it would be the largest shipyard anywhere&nbsp;
in the world. On its seven long, large docks,

03:27:45.600 --> 03:27:55.200
at least 100 ships could be built at any one time.&nbsp;
To help in his mammoth effort, the Yongle Emperor

03:27:55.200 --> 03:28:03.679
invited more than 20,000 craftsmen to work here on&nbsp;
his ships, and timbers of elm, cedar, and camphor

03:28:03.680 --> 03:28:10.880
were brought in enormous quantities to the city&nbsp;
of Nanjing, transported from inland ports down the

03:28:10.880 --> 03:28:19.120
rivers on flat-bottomed barges. The noise of the&nbsp;
shipwrights shouting and calling, the thump of the

03:28:19.120 --> 03:28:26.000
massive beams being lifted into place, the beating&nbsp;
of chisels and hammers on nails, all of this would

03:28:26.000 --> 03:28:34.479
have sounded over the rooftops of Nanjing in&nbsp;
the early years of the 15th century. For China,

03:28:34.479 --> 03:28:41.919
it was the sound of industry and progress, but&nbsp;
for the power of Majapahit far across the sea

03:28:41.920 --> 03:29:00.800
in the south, it might as well have been the&nbsp;
sound of nails being driven into its coffin.

03:29:00.800 --> 03:29:07.760
By the year 1405, as the Regreg&nbsp;
civil war dragged on in Majapahit,

03:29:07.760 --> 03:29:14.479
the Yongle Emperor had completed his&nbsp;
grand fleet, and now all he needed was

03:29:14.479 --> 03:29:28.239
an admiral to lead it. The man he chose&nbsp;
was a eunuch, a giant named Zheng He.

03:29:28.239 --> 03:29:34.719
In some ways, Zheng He was an unlikely&nbsp;
candidate to become China's most famous

03:29:34.720 --> 03:29:41.920
sailor and admiral. For one thing,&nbsp;
he had barely any sailing experience,

03:29:41.920 --> 03:29:47.760
but he had spent much of his life serving&nbsp;
the Yongle Emperor as a military commander,

03:29:47.760 --> 03:29:57.520
and had proved himself a capable and loyal&nbsp;
general. Zheng He was a Muslim born into a devout

03:29:57.520 --> 03:30:04.960
family in the southwest of China, but he had been&nbsp;
captured during the wars of the preceding decades,

03:30:04.960 --> 03:30:12.399
and had been castrated and brought up&nbsp;
a eunuch as a kind of military slave.

03:30:13.600 --> 03:30:24.960
One writer named Yuan Zhongche describes his&nbsp;
towering stature with perhaps some exaggeration.

03:30:24.960 --> 03:30:31.840
He was seven feet tall and had a waist&nbsp;
about five feet in circumference. His

03:30:31.840 --> 03:30:37.840
cheeks and forehead were high, but his&nbsp;
nose was small. He had glaring eyes,

03:30:37.840 --> 03:30:42.080
teeth as white and well-shaped&nbsp;
as shells, and a voice as loud

03:30:42.080 --> 03:30:54.640
as a huge bell. He knew a great deal about&nbsp;
warfare, and was well accustomed to battle.

03:30:54.640 --> 03:31:05.600
On the 11th of July 1405, under the command&nbsp;
of Admiral Zheng He, this vast fleet set sail,

03:31:05.600 --> 03:31:11.600
and it would have made an awe-inspiring site.

03:31:11.600 --> 03:31:19.600
It comprised a total of 317 vessels of&nbsp;
the latest design, including at least 60

03:31:19.600 --> 03:31:26.880
vast treasure ships that were among the&nbsp;
largest in the world. By some sources,

03:31:26.880 --> 03:31:34.960
these could have measured more than 120 meters&nbsp;
in length and 50 in breadth, making them still

03:31:34.960 --> 03:31:42.239
among the largest wooden sailing ships built&nbsp;
anywhere in the world. These vessels were each

03:31:42.239 --> 03:31:50.080
fitted with twenty-four cast bronze cannons, and&nbsp;
were beautifully decorated, too, with their prows

03:31:50.080 --> 03:32:04.239
carved with animal heads, dragons, phoenixes,&nbsp;
and eagles, all painted in bright colors

03:32:04.239 --> 03:32:11.040
Like the ancient Greeks, Egyptians, Phoenicians,&nbsp;
and Romans, the Chinese painted eyes on the fronts

03:32:11.040 --> 03:32:18.560
of their vessels so that the ships seemed to take&nbsp;
on a life of their own and see their way through

03:32:18.560 --> 03:32:28.960
mists and stormy seas. On board these hundreds&nbsp;
of ships were a total of 28,000 men, including

03:32:28.960 --> 03:32:37.439
sailors and soldiers, but also diplomats,&nbsp;
astronomers, linguists, interpreters, and doctors,

03:32:37.439 --> 03:32:45.759
as well as carpenters and shipbuilders for if&nbsp;
the vessels needed repairing while at sea. On

03:32:45.760 --> 03:32:56.239
one stone tablet that the admiral later erected&nbsp;
in Fujian, he describes this great voyage.

03:32:56.239 --> 03:33:04.160
We have traversed more than 100,000 li of immense&nbsp;
water spaces, and have beheld in the ocean huge

03:33:04.160 --> 03:33:12.000
waves like mountains rising sky high, and we&nbsp;
have set eyes on barbarian regions far away,

03:33:12.000 --> 03:33:20.960
hidden in a blue transparency of light vapors,&nbsp;
while our sails, loftily unfurled like clouds day

03:33:20.960 --> 03:33:34.160
and night, continued their course as rapidly&nbsp;
as a star traversing those savage waves.

03:33:34.160 --> 03:33:41.040
Although outfitted with cannons, the treasure&nbsp;
ships were not primarily warships. They were

03:33:41.040 --> 03:33:47.840
in fact vast cargo vessels, their holds&nbsp;
filled to the brim with Chinese silks,

03:33:47.840 --> 03:33:58.160
cotton, tea, wine, oil, iron, and candles, along&nbsp;
with other luxuries, all intended for trade with

03:33:58.160 --> 03:34:05.680
the strange lands they would encounter along the&nbsp;
way. From these lands they brought back spices,

03:34:05.680 --> 03:34:13.520
gemstones, and even in the year 1414, a live&nbsp;
giraffe, which had been brought to Bengal by

03:34:13.520 --> 03:34:21.279
some African delegates from Malindi. Impressed&nbsp;
at the sight of the treasure fleet, these men had

03:34:21.279 --> 03:34:27.759
gifted one of these animals to the Chinese,&nbsp;
who brought it home to no little amazement.

03:34:28.880 --> 03:34:35.040
The Yongle Emperor was no stranger&nbsp;
to receiving exotic animals as gifts,

03:34:35.040 --> 03:34:40.319
and there was even a part of his palace&nbsp;
devoted to keeping and caring for them;

03:34:40.319 --> 03:34:48.799
elephants and rhinoceroses from Champa, bears&nbsp;
from Siam, parrots and peacocks from Java, and

03:34:48.800 --> 03:35:03.279
ostriches from Aden. But of all these, it was the&nbsp;
giraffe that he asked his court artists to paint.

03:35:03.279 --> 03:35:10.160
Alongside the vast treasure ships sailed a&nbsp;
countless array of different support vessels,

03:35:10.160 --> 03:35:19.120
some carrying horses and troops, others warships&nbsp;
for defense. Other boats were floating water

03:35:19.120 --> 03:35:28.720
tankers, ensuring the thousands of personnel&nbsp;
wouldn't go thirsty on long voyages between ports.

03:35:28.720 --> 03:35:38.000
The ships communicated between one another using&nbsp;
large flags, signal bells, a drum, and gongs,

03:35:38.000 --> 03:35:45.600
along with ten lanterns that could signal at&nbsp;
night. They even carried carrier pigeons to

03:35:45.600 --> 03:35:53.600
communicate over longer distances. The ultimate&nbsp;
destination of the treasure fleet was the city of

03:35:53.600 --> 03:36:00.479
Kozhikode in the modern Indian state of Kerala,&nbsp;
but the fleet was ordered to stop at every large

03:36:00.479 --> 03:36:10.639
port and kingdom on the way to demonstrate&nbsp;
the might of the Ming Emperor. It was in 1406

03:36:10.640 --> 03:36:33.200
that the Ming fleet arrived in Java, while the&nbsp;
Regreg civil war was reaching its bloody height.

03:36:33.200 --> 03:36:40.239
When the Chinese treasure fleet arrived in Java,&nbsp;
they might have expected to encounter the scenes

03:36:40.239 --> 03:36:47.920
of wealth and prosperity that had been recorded&nbsp;
by previous visitors, but they would find instead

03:36:47.920 --> 03:36:56.080
a troubled land devastated by civil war.&nbsp;
The treasure ships anchored off the coast,

03:36:56.080 --> 03:37:04.399
and a group of Chinese soldiers and merchants&nbsp;
came ashore in Western Java hoping to trade.

03:37:04.399 --> 03:37:12.080
But the Western king Wikramawardhana, the husband&nbsp;
of Hayam Wuruk's daughter, believed that his rival

03:37:12.080 --> 03:37:19.920
in the east had brought the Chinese to intervene&nbsp;
in the civil war. While the Chinese visitors were

03:37:19.920 --> 03:37:27.760
trading in the marketplace, King Wikramawardhana's&nbsp;
soldiers swept down on them from all sides.

03:37:27.760 --> 03:37:33.200
The cries of the Javanese warriors and the&nbsp;
screams of the dying would have joined the

03:37:33.200 --> 03:37:39.679
pops and bangs of cannons that would have&nbsp;
sounded all around the marketplace. The

03:37:39.680 --> 03:37:47.279
acrid smell of gunpowder and blood would have&nbsp;
lingered on the air. When the smoke cleared,

03:37:47.279 --> 03:37:55.840
170 Chinese merchants and sailors were dead,&nbsp;
and the rest had fled back to their ships.

03:37:57.680 --> 03:38:04.560
Admiral Zheng He had enough soldiers with him&nbsp;
that he could have invaded Java in response,

03:38:04.560 --> 03:38:08.399
and perhaps like the Mongols more&nbsp;
than a hundred years earlier,

03:38:08.399 --> 03:38:16.559
he could have decisively tipped the balance&nbsp;
in this civil war. But he was a pragmatic man,

03:38:16.560 --> 03:38:24.560
and he decided to tread a more considered path.&nbsp;
He treated his wounded on board the ships,

03:38:24.560 --> 03:38:34.239
and sent envoys back to the Yongle Emperor asking&nbsp;
him what to do. In the time of Hayam Wuruk thirty

03:38:34.239 --> 03:38:41.439
years earlier, China had steered clear of&nbsp;
conflict with the powerful Majapahit Empire,

03:38:41.439 --> 03:38:50.639
sending money and goods in order to avoid a&nbsp;
war. But now Majapahit was weakened and divided.

03:38:50.640 --> 03:38:58.239
This time it was King Wikramawardhana who would&nbsp;
send a groveling apology to the Chinese court,

03:38:58.239 --> 03:39:09.120
and the Ming Shilu, or Records of the Ming&nbsp;
Dynasty, records what the emperor said in reply.

03:39:09.120 --> 03:39:15.519
There is no doubt as to your guilt. You&nbsp;
acted on the basis of greed and anger,

03:39:15.520 --> 03:39:18.399
and you have acted against heaven.

03:39:18.399 --> 03:39:24.639
Is there anything more serious than this? Just&nbsp;
as I was about to raise an army to punish you,

03:39:24.640 --> 03:39:32.160
I have seen that you are capable of repenting your&nbsp;
mistakes, and I have thus leniently stopped the

03:39:32.160 --> 03:39:40.960
dispatch of troops. But what of the 170 people&nbsp;
who died innocently? You must immediately pay

03:39:40.960 --> 03:39:47.439
60,000 liang of gold as compensation for&nbsp;
their lives and to atone for your crime.

03:39:54.239 --> 03:40:01.200
A year later, a Javanese ship arrived in&nbsp;
China with only a sixth of the sum of gold

03:40:01.200 --> 03:40:07.439
that the Chinese emperor had demanded, along&nbsp;
with a letter explaining that the bankrupt

03:40:07.439 --> 03:40:16.160
kingdom could not afford to pay any more.&nbsp;
The Chinese emperor responded magnanimously.

03:40:18.319 --> 03:40:22.479
What I want from those people&nbsp;
who live far away is that they

03:40:22.479 --> 03:40:30.239
acknowledge their guilt. But I do not&nbsp;
want to enrich myself with their gold.

03:40:30.239 --> 03:40:35.279
The point had been made, and&nbsp;
the humiliation of Majapahit

03:40:35.279 --> 03:40:42.559
was total. Everyone in the region took notice.

03:40:42.560 --> 03:40:49.520
In 1424, Chinese records show that&nbsp;
when one king in Sumatra died,

03:40:49.520 --> 03:40:57.200
his son sent out a letter asking permission to&nbsp;
succeed him as king, and he sent it not to the

03:40:57.200 --> 03:41:07.120
kings of Majapahit in Java, but to the Chinese&nbsp;
emperor in the Ming court. This shows just how

03:41:07.120 --> 03:41:32.000
far the star of Majapahit had fallen, and&nbsp;
how the might of China was on the rise.

03:41:35.920 --> 03:41:42.560
Although Admiral Zheng He had declined to fight&nbsp;
on this occasion, the treasure fleet was more than

03:41:42.560 --> 03:41:50.880
capable of doing battle on the seas, and alongside&nbsp;
their main diplomatic and mercantile mission, its

03:41:50.880 --> 03:41:59.279
admiral had been given some less peaceful tasks&nbsp;
to complete on their voyage. Chief among these

03:41:59.279 --> 03:42:08.160
was a mission to destroy the pirate fortress that&nbsp;
still held out at the Sumatran port of Palembang,

03:42:08.160 --> 03:42:16.880
which had risen up due to the weakness of the&nbsp;
Majapahit Empire. The Mingshi, or History of Ming,

03:42:16.880 --> 03:42:25.520
describes how the pirate chief Chen Zuyi had been&nbsp;
playing a clever double game attempting to mollify

03:42:25.520 --> 03:42:33.439
the Ming emperor. In the fourth year of the Yongle&nbsp;
reign, the pirate chieftain of the old port,

03:42:33.439 --> 03:42:40.399
Chen Zuyi, sent his son to the Ming court.&nbsp;
However, although he sent tributes to the court,

03:42:40.399 --> 03:42:49.439
he was plundering the seas, and thus obstructing&nbsp;
the comings and goings of the tributes envoys.

03:42:49.439 --> 03:42:57.919
On its way home through the straits of Malacca,&nbsp;
the enormous treasure fleet took its chance.

03:42:57.920 --> 03:43:04.479
They sailed along the mangrove-crusted&nbsp;
coast of Sumatra towards Palembang,

03:43:04.479 --> 03:43:12.639
which the Chinese called the old port. We can&nbsp;
only imagine the sight that greeted the pirates

03:43:12.640 --> 03:43:21.439
of Palembang as the hundreds of colorful&nbsp;
ships bore down on them on the horizon.

03:43:21.439 --> 03:43:27.120
When they arrived at the port city, they&nbsp;
demanded the surrender of the pirate chief

03:43:27.120 --> 03:43:35.199
Chen Zuyi. According to the Ming Shilu, or Records&nbsp;
of the Ming Dynasty, the pirate chief attempted to

03:43:35.199 --> 03:43:42.800
use deception to gain the upper hand. When&nbsp;
he arrived at the old port of Palembang,

03:43:42.800 --> 03:43:49.760
he came across Zuyi and others, and sent people&nbsp;
to bring them to negotiate their pacification.

03:43:49.760 --> 03:43:56.239
Zuyi and the others feigned surrender, but&nbsp;
secretly plotted to attack the imperial army.

03:43:56.239 --> 03:44:07.199
Zheng He and the others found out about this and,&nbsp;
marshalling the troops, prepared their defences.

03:44:07.199 --> 03:44:13.760
The ensuing battle would be the first test&nbsp;
of the power of the Yongle Emperor's fleet.

03:44:14.640 --> 03:44:22.319
The Ming ships would have rammed into the pirate&nbsp;
vessels, hurled gunpowder bombs from catapults,

03:44:22.319 --> 03:44:29.439
and fired special kinds of fireworks filled&nbsp;
with scraps of paper designed to spread fire

03:44:29.439 --> 03:44:38.960
and terror and ignite enemy sails. They would&nbsp;
have also sent volleys of flaming arrows flying

03:44:38.960 --> 03:44:52.640
towards the pirate ships. The result was a&nbsp;
resounding victory for the Ming treasure fleet.

03:44:52.640 --> 03:44:59.439
The pirate Zuyi suffered a great defeat.&nbsp;
Over 5,000 of the bandit gang were killed,

03:44:59.439 --> 03:45:06.000
while ten of the bandit ships were burnt and&nbsp;
seven were captured. Further, two counterfeit

03:45:06.000 --> 03:45:12.800
bronze seals were seized, and three prisoners,&nbsp;
including Zuyi, were taken alive. When they

03:45:12.800 --> 03:45:22.640
arrived at the capital in chains, it was ordered&nbsp;
that all of the prisoners were to be beheaded.

03:45:22.640 --> 03:45:27.840
With this pirate threat crushed,&nbsp;
the Ming Chinese quickly moved to

03:45:27.840 --> 03:45:33.279
establish a governor of the area to&nbsp;
prevent a resurgence of any piracy.

03:45:34.479 --> 03:45:45.679
The man they chose, a Muslim named Shi Jinching,&nbsp;
had once been an official of the Majapahit Empire.

03:45:45.680 --> 03:45:52.479
The old port pacification superintendency&nbsp;
was established, and it was ordered that Shi

03:45:52.479 --> 03:45:59.359
Jinching be appointed as the pacification&nbsp;
superintendent. A seal, a title patent,

03:45:59.359 --> 03:46:07.839
headwear, and a belt, patterned fine silks,&nbsp;
and silk gauzes were conferred upon him.

03:46:07.840 --> 03:46:13.600
While the rulers of Majapahit had their&nbsp;
backs turned, fighting amongst themselves,

03:46:13.600 --> 03:46:23.680
the Ming Chinese had swept in and stamped&nbsp;
their seal of authority on the Malacca Strait.

03:46:31.680 --> 03:46:37.359
To strengthen this grip over the narrow&nbsp;
and crucial waterway, the Chinese would

03:46:37.359 --> 03:46:44.000
also bring under their wing the young city of&nbsp;
Malacca, which had recently been growing in

03:46:44.000 --> 03:46:52.160
wealth and importance. Malacca was a hub&nbsp;
for another tide of change that was now

03:46:52.160 --> 03:47:08.559
sweeping through the archipelago. That was&nbsp;
the steady spread of the religion of Islam.

03:47:15.120 --> 03:47:22.640
Islam had long had a presence in the Indonesian&nbsp;
archipelago, and had arrived with traders who

03:47:22.640 --> 03:47:28.319
traveled back and forth across the Indian&nbsp;
Ocean, bringing nutmeg and cloves to the

03:47:28.319 --> 03:47:37.439
markets of Egypt and Arabia. But now this young&nbsp;
religion began to make advances in all directions.

03:47:39.279 --> 03:47:44.319
The European traveller Marco Polo&nbsp;
remarked on the change already

03:47:44.319 --> 03:47:53.199
underway on the island of Sumatra when he&nbsp;
passed through the town of Perlak in 1292.

03:47:55.120 --> 03:48:01.359
The people of Perlak used to be idolaters,&nbsp;
but owing to contact with Saracen merchants,

03:48:01.359 --> 03:48:11.599
who continually resort here in their ships, they&nbsp;
have all been converted to the law of Mohammed.

03:48:15.840 --> 03:48:22.319
Part of the reason for the spread of Islam on&nbsp;
the archipelago was the prestige and protection

03:48:22.319 --> 03:48:30.799
that the religion brought. One example of&nbsp;
this in action is the king of Parameswara,

03:48:30.800 --> 03:48:38.000
the ruler of Singapura, who had been besieged&nbsp;
by Majapahit under the reign of Hayam Wuruk,

03:48:38.000 --> 03:48:47.600
and had fled north to the coast of the Malacca&nbsp;
Strait. There he had founded the city of Malacca,

03:48:47.600 --> 03:48:53.760
and in order to add an heir of legitimacy&nbsp;
to his new rule, Parameswara seems to have

03:48:53.760 --> 03:49:00.000
ditched his original Hindu-Buddhist&nbsp;
name and adopted a new Persian one,

03:49:00.000 --> 03:49:08.720
calling himself Sultan Iskander Shah instead.&nbsp;
Muslim traders preferred where possible to

03:49:08.720 --> 03:49:15.680
trade with other Muslims, who were more likely&nbsp;
to share their languages of Arabic or Persian,

03:49:15.680 --> 03:49:22.399
keep the same trading and prayer hours,&nbsp;
and shared a common moral code. And so,

03:49:22.399 --> 03:49:31.040
this connection helped Malacca to rapidly expand.&nbsp;
The new city's strategic location right on the

03:49:31.040 --> 03:49:38.319
narrow sea corridor meant the Ming Chinese were&nbsp;
also very interested to bring its rulers under

03:49:38.319 --> 03:49:47.599
their patronage and protection. When Admiral Zheng&nbsp;
He arrived at the port town of Malacca, he left a

03:49:47.600 --> 03:49:56.000
stone tablet inscribed with a poem that officially&nbsp;
declared it under the protection of China.

03:49:59.120 --> 03:50:07.040
The vast southwestern sea reaches unto the Middle&nbsp;
Kingdom, its waves cresting high as the heavens,

03:50:07.040 --> 03:50:15.040
watering the earth the same way for countless&nbsp;
eons. Washed by sunlight, bathed in the moonlight,

03:50:15.040 --> 03:50:23.439
rain-swept crags and dewy rocks in its midst&nbsp;
luxuriate with vegetation. And in such a place,

03:50:23.439 --> 03:50:29.839
people live in harmony. Its righteous king,&nbsp;
paying his respects to imperial power,

03:50:29.840 --> 03:50:36.720
wishes his country to be treated as one of our&nbsp;
imperial domains, and to follow the Chinese way.

03:50:36.720 --> 03:50:45.760
So here, writ large on everlasting stone, we do&nbsp;
proclaim your loyalty. Posterity will smile upon

03:50:45.760 --> 03:50:53.760
your deed as time passes, and your sons and&nbsp;
grandsons will have limitless good fortune.

03:50:56.640 --> 03:51:04.319
In this, the Chinese were right. The port town&nbsp;
of Malacca would grow under Ming protection to

03:51:04.319 --> 03:51:12.799
become the largest and most important in the whole&nbsp;
region. When Zheng He returned a few years later,

03:51:12.800 --> 03:51:19.680
he would build a fort at Malacca to protect it,&nbsp;
and in return, the people of the city would ensure

03:51:19.680 --> 03:51:28.640
that this crucial waterway would be controlled&nbsp;
by China and China alone. Wealth and trade that

03:51:28.640 --> 03:51:35.760
would have once flowed through the ports of Java&nbsp;
now passed through the new trading hub of Malacca

03:51:35.760 --> 03:51:45.600
instead. From this hub of commerce, the influence&nbsp;
of Islam would spread all across the islands.

03:51:47.199 --> 03:51:52.800
For the kings of Java who ruled according to&nbsp;
legitimacy derived from their connection to

03:51:52.800 --> 03:52:01.199
the Buddha and the god Shiva, each new&nbsp;
convert reduced their overall power.

03:52:02.960 --> 03:52:11.120
Subsequent voyages by Zheng He's treasure fleets&nbsp;
would sail as far west as the Strait of Hormuz,

03:52:11.120 --> 03:52:18.399
the city of Mecca, and Mogadishu and&nbsp;
Mombasa on the East African coast.

03:52:18.399 --> 03:52:27.359
But eventually the Ming would turn&nbsp;
away from oceangoing exploration.

03:52:34.720 --> 03:52:42.640
When the adventurous Yongle Emperor died, he was&nbsp;
replaced by more inward-looking rulers who focused

03:52:42.640 --> 03:52:50.399
instead on the construction of the Great Wall in&nbsp;
the north. At one point, the Ming even outlawed

03:52:50.399 --> 03:52:59.679
the construction of large ships, dismantling some,&nbsp;
and perhaps even burning some vessels in harbor.

03:52:59.680 --> 03:53:06.560
But the decades when the vast treasure fleets&nbsp;
had sailed the seas left a lasting impression

03:53:06.560 --> 03:53:16.319
on every culture that they touched, and the damage&nbsp;
done to the image of Javanese power was immense.

03:53:16.319 --> 03:53:29.920
Eventually the Regreg civil war would come to an&nbsp;
end in Java, and the Western king Wikramawardhana,

03:53:29.920 --> 03:53:38.080
Hayam Wuruk's son-in-law, would be victorious.&nbsp;
Despite his humiliating and bankrupting experience

03:53:38.080 --> 03:53:44.960
of angering the Chinese emperor, he managed to&nbsp;
close in on his enemies in the Eastern Court,

03:53:44.960 --> 03:53:51.120
and in the final bloody confrontation, the&nbsp;
Western forces broke through the walls of

03:53:51.120 --> 03:54:00.960
the Eastern palace. The lord of Wirabhumi fled&nbsp;
in the night and escaped by boat. He was hunted

03:54:00.960 --> 03:54:08.960
down and beheaded, and his severed head was&nbsp;
brought back to the capital of Majapahit.

03:54:10.080 --> 03:54:18.960
The civil war was over and the kingdom was united&nbsp;
once more, but the conflict had exacted a terrible

03:54:18.960 --> 03:54:28.080
cost. In only two years of fighting, the manpower&nbsp;
of Majapahit had been severely depleted. Its

03:54:28.080 --> 03:54:35.279
treasury was on the verge of bankruptcy, and its&nbsp;
influence had been drastically curtailed right

03:54:35.279 --> 03:54:42.319
at the point when powerful rivals were rising in&nbsp;
the north and the religion of Islam was spreading

03:54:42.319 --> 03:54:52.559
from port to port. The center of the mandala of&nbsp;
power had now shifted irrevocably away from Java,

03:54:52.560 --> 03:55:02.161
and the age of Majapahit's Empire of the&nbsp;
Islands would slowly but surely come to an end.

03:55:10.160 --> 03:55:17.760
While Majapahit lost much of its overseas empire&nbsp;
through the beginning of the 15th century,

03:55:17.760 --> 03:55:22.720
it still remained the preeminent&nbsp;
power on the island of Java,

03:55:22.720 --> 03:55:31.199
but steadily its loss of regional status began&nbsp;
to erode its power even in its heartlands.

03:55:33.680 --> 03:55:40.479
When King Wikramawardhana died in&nbsp;
1429, his daughter Suhita took to

03:55:40.479 --> 03:55:47.759
the throne. She was prepared to rule in the&nbsp;
tradition of the great Javanese queens who

03:55:47.760 --> 03:55:55.279
had preceded her a century earlier, Dyah&nbsp;
Gayatri and Tribhuwana Wijaytungadewi,

03:55:55.279 --> 03:56:03.439
but for a number of reasons, her&nbsp;
rule would be beset by problems.

03:56:03.439 --> 03:56:11.519
Chief among these was a terrible famine&nbsp;
that descended on Java around the year 1426,

03:56:11.520 --> 03:56:20.000
caused by a drought. But the only source&nbsp;
for this is the chronicle of the Pararaton,

03:56:20.000 --> 03:56:35.520
and by this point its reports are becoming&nbsp;
sparse and scarce, and contain little detail.

03:56:35.520 --> 03:56:39.199
Next, there was a period of&nbsp;
food shortage that was very

03:56:39.199 --> 03:56:43.519
long in the year of the Serpents Biting People.

03:56:44.960 --> 03:56:52.640
Unfortunately for historians, the decline of royal&nbsp;
power in Java also resulted in a disruption of

03:56:52.640 --> 03:57:01.359
the major sources that we have relied on up to&nbsp;
this point. Narrative texts like the Pararaton

03:57:01.359 --> 03:57:09.439
or Book of Kings were crucial sources during the&nbsp;
golden age of Majapahit. But as we enter the 15th

03:57:09.439 --> 03:57:18.000
century, the text becomes increasingly obscure and&nbsp;
difficult to understand. The chronicle's notes get

03:57:18.000 --> 03:57:25.279
shorter, concerning only earthquakes and eruptions&nbsp;
and the deaths of kings, and they come further

03:57:25.279 --> 03:57:34.479
and further apart. The final three pages of the&nbsp;
Javanese Book of Kings are just a few scraps of

03:57:34.479 --> 03:57:48.000
seemingly unrelated information separated by&nbsp;
decades, with its final note coming in 1481.

03:57:51.359 --> 03:57:54.880
Then a volcanic eruption occurred in the week of

03:57:54.880 --> 03:58:01.920
Watu Ganung in the Saka year&nbsp;
Action of the Sky Sea Ekor.

03:58:01.920 --> 03:58:08.800
Perhaps in an attempt to alleviate the effects&nbsp;
of the drought, Suhita had constructed a vast

03:58:08.800 --> 03:58:16.000
reservoir named Kolam Seagaran just&nbsp;
to the north of the city of Majapahit.

03:58:16.000 --> 03:58:25.439
This great pool was 800 meters by 500, and&nbsp;
ringed all around by banks of red brick.

03:58:25.439 --> 03:58:31.519
Whether or not this alleviated the famine,&nbsp;
we will never know, but it shows the drastic

03:58:31.520 --> 03:58:38.319
situation that the empire was facing around&nbsp;
this time under pressure from the environment,

03:58:38.319 --> 03:58:48.719
from internal divisions, and increasingly from&nbsp;
powerful rivals. When Suhita died in 1447,

03:58:48.720 --> 03:58:57.359
her brother Kritavijaya ruled for only four&nbsp;
years. He was followed by a further four kings,

03:58:57.359 --> 03:59:05.359
all of whom ruled for only a short time, and&nbsp;
the relationships between them are unclear.

03:59:06.960 --> 03:59:15.279
The kingdom erupted in civil war again in&nbsp;
the 1470s, and in 1478, one pretender broke

03:59:15.279 --> 03:59:23.519
through the defences of Majapahit and killed&nbsp;
his brother who sat on the throne. That year

03:59:23.520 --> 03:59:30.560
in Java would be given a name that seems to&nbsp;
convey the mood in the kingdom at the time;

03:59:30.560 --> 03:59:37.760
sirna ilang kretaning bhumi , which has been&nbsp;
translated as Vanished and Lost is the Order

03:59:37.760 --> 03:59:47.199
of the World. Upon his victory, this new king&nbsp;
had inscribed a series of stone inscriptions

03:59:47.199 --> 03:59:56.000
that made dire warnings of the fate that would&nbsp;
befall any who failed to support his rule.

03:59:58.319 --> 04:00:05.920
They will end up in the realm of Yama, the king&nbsp;
of the dead. They will be beaten by Yama's army

04:00:05.920 --> 04:00:14.960
for years, innumerable. They will fall in the&nbsp;
cauldron of hell. They will not be reborn. So,

04:00:14.960 --> 04:00:18.720
not only will they end up in&nbsp;
hell, but they will stay there,

04:00:18.720 --> 04:00:24.720
and even if they are reborn, they will&nbsp;
be reborn under miserable circumstances.

04:00:27.680 --> 04:00:33.120
It seems clear from these inscriptions&nbsp;
that the new king didn't feel at all

04:00:33.120 --> 04:00:40.160
secure on the throne. Throughout&nbsp;
this period of political instability,

04:00:40.160 --> 04:00:46.559
the situation for Majapahit would be made&nbsp;
increasingly worse by the rise of a new kind

04:00:46.560 --> 04:01:14.240
of power on the coasts of Java. That is the&nbsp;
ever-increasing influence of Muslim traders.

04:01:17.760 --> 04:01:24.000
With the powerful rival of Malacca&nbsp;
now exerting its influence,

04:01:24.000 --> 04:01:32.080
Muslim merchants had become wealthier and more&nbsp;
influential in Java than ever before, and in

04:01:32.080 --> 04:01:40.239
the vacuum left by royal power, these wealthy&nbsp;
merchants became politically powerful, too.

04:01:41.199 --> 04:01:47.760
Now, more and more people on the coasts all&nbsp;
around the archipelago began to flirt with

04:01:47.760 --> 04:01:56.800
the idea of this new religion. The Portuguese&nbsp;
traveller Tom Pires describes the steady advance

04:01:56.800 --> 04:02:08.399
of Islamic power in Java at the expense of&nbsp;
the Hindu-Buddhists, who he calls heathens.

04:02:08.399 --> 04:02:14.799
At the time when there were heathens along the&nbsp;
sea coast of Java, many merchants used to come;

04:02:14.800 --> 04:02:22.399
Parsees, Arabs, Gujaratees, Bengalees, Malays,&nbsp;
and other nationalities, there being many

04:02:22.399 --> 04:02:29.759
Moors among them. They began to trade in the&nbsp;
country and to grow rich. They succeeded in

04:02:29.760 --> 04:02:36.800
way of making mosques, and mollahs came from&nbsp;
outside. In some places the heathen Javanese

04:02:36.800 --> 04:02:47.279
lords themselves turned Mohammedan, and these&nbsp;
merchant Moors took possession of these places.

04:02:47.279 --> 04:02:55.199
The growing influence of these coastal traders&nbsp;
would culminate in 1475 with the foundation of a

04:02:55.199 --> 04:03:03.679
true Muslim kingdom on the island of Java, a power&nbsp;
that would call itself the Sultanate of Demak.

04:03:12.160 --> 04:03:19.920
A port town looking north into the Java Sea,&nbsp;
Demak had been founded in the late 15th century,

04:03:19.920 --> 04:03:25.520
possibly by Chinese merchants, and its&nbsp;
people had built a grand mosque there,

04:03:25.520 --> 04:03:31.121
combining elements of Muslim&nbsp;
and Javanese architecture.

04:03:32.479 --> 04:03:38.719
The ruler of Damak was a man named&nbsp;
Raden Patah, who was a vassal swearing

04:03:38.720 --> 04:03:50.319
allegiance to Majapahit. But he soon declared&nbsp;
independence and claimed the title of Sultan.

04:03:50.319 --> 04:03:56.960
As Majapahit declined, the&nbsp;
Sultanate of Demak would expand.

04:03:56.960 --> 04:04:04.160
It conquered the coastal region of Tuban&nbsp;
by 1527, and was spreading inland as far

04:04:04.160 --> 04:04:25.279
as Malang by 1546. For the dwindling power&nbsp;
of Majapahit, this was the final death nail.

04:04:28.800 --> 04:04:34.960
Due to the slow petering out of royal&nbsp;
chronicles in Java, we have few details about

04:04:34.960 --> 04:04:42.640
what exactly happened to lead to the eventual&nbsp;
destruction of Majapahit. But at this point,

04:04:42.640 --> 04:04:54.720
we are able to turn to the writings of some&nbsp;
outside visitors to the island of Java.

04:04:54.720 --> 04:05:01.199
Around 1513, about a hundred years&nbsp;
after the end of the Regreg War,

04:05:01.199 --> 04:05:06.800
the Portuguese traveller Tom Pires visited Java.

04:05:08.399 --> 04:05:16.080
He wrote in glowing terms about the beauty of the&nbsp;
landscape and the abundance of the island, but he

04:05:16.080 --> 04:05:27.120
found the power of Majapahit already a shadow of&nbsp;
its former glory, as he recounts in his memoirs.

04:05:27.120 --> 04:05:31.760
They say that the island of Java&nbsp;
used to rule as far as the Spice

04:05:31.760 --> 04:05:36.479
Islands on the eastern side and&nbsp;
over a great part of the west,

04:05:36.479 --> 04:05:41.679
and that it had almost all the islands&nbsp;
of Sumatra under its dominion and all the

04:05:41.680 --> 04:05:50.239
islands known to the Javanese, and that it had&nbsp;
all this for a long time passed until about a

04:05:50.239 --> 04:06:00.160
hundred years ago, when its power began to&nbsp;
diminish and it came to its present state.

04:06:00.160 --> 04:06:07.920
Pires also alludes to some degree of political&nbsp;
instability in Java. According to his account,

04:06:07.920 --> 04:06:12.800
the power of its kings had diminished&nbsp;
so that they were now little more than

04:06:12.800 --> 04:06:22.239
a figurehead with the mahapatih, or prime&nbsp;
minister, appearing to wield greater power.

04:06:22.239 --> 04:06:27.439
Because the Javanese have lost a great part&nbsp;
of their lands, the kings do not command,

04:06:27.439 --> 04:06:34.399
nor are they taken into account, but only the&nbsp;
viceroy and chief captain. This man is known

04:06:34.399 --> 04:06:42.399
and honored like the real king. All the lords&nbsp;
of Java obey him. Him they honor. This governor

04:06:42.399 --> 04:06:49.199
commands in everything. He holds the king of Java&nbsp;
in his hands. He orders him to be given food.

04:06:49.840 --> 04:06:59.120
The king has no voice in anything,&nbsp;
nor is he of any importance.

04:06:59.120 --> 04:07:04.640
Alongside this weakening of royal&nbsp;
authority came the fragmentation

04:07:04.640 --> 04:07:13.120
of the kingdom. Local lords became steadily&nbsp;
more powerful, with some even breaking away,

04:07:13.120 --> 04:07:18.640
and now the Muslim sultanates&nbsp;
began to taste blood in the water.

04:07:31.680 --> 04:07:39.840
The sultan of the city of Demak, Raden Patah,&nbsp;
knew that only one thing stood between him and

04:07:39.840 --> 04:07:49.920
ultimate power on the island of Java; that&nbsp;
was the lingering remnants of Majapahit.

04:07:49.920 --> 04:07:57.840
After only a few years, he gathered his&nbsp;
forces and marched on the now isolated city.

04:07:58.960 --> 04:08:05.439
The later Muslim epic verse chronicle, the&nbsp;
Babad Tanah Jawi, recounts what happened

04:08:05.439 --> 04:08:15.759
next in heroic terms as the Muslim forces&nbsp;
bore down on the once great capital city.

04:08:15.760 --> 04:08:21.120
Then they agree to gather together all&nbsp;
Muslims with their arms. Then they set

04:08:21.120 --> 04:08:27.199
out to Majapahit. The number of troops&nbsp;
was immeasurable. The city of Majapahit

04:08:27.199 --> 04:08:38.319
was encircled. Many inhabitants submitted to&nbsp;
Raden Patah. Nobody dared to meet him in battle.

04:08:38.319 --> 04:08:46.880
Marching under blue and yellow banners,&nbsp;
they soon arrived at the walls of Majapahit.

04:08:46.880 --> 04:08:54.479
The later Muslim chronicles imitated elements of&nbsp;
previous Javanese legends, and claimed that the

04:08:54.479 --> 04:09:01.919
last king of Majapahit was so awed at the sight&nbsp;
of the army approaching the city that he simply

04:09:01.920 --> 04:09:11.600
ascended to heaven right there on the spot, and&nbsp;
the city was captured peacefully. At the moment

04:09:11.600 --> 04:09:18.720
Prabu Brawijaya ascended to heaven, something&nbsp;
like a fireball was seen coming out of the palace

04:09:18.720 --> 04:09:26.800
of Majapahit. It flashed like a bolt of lightning&nbsp;
and sounded like a terrifying thunderclap. It fell

04:09:26.800 --> 04:09:34.479
down in Bintara. Raden Patah entered the palace.&nbsp;
He was astonished that he did not encounter a

04:09:34.479 --> 04:09:47.120
single person. He wept in his heart, then left the&nbsp;
palace and returned to Bintara with his troops.

04:09:47.120 --> 04:09:54.080
But we can assume that the end of Majapahit was&nbsp;
not as peaceful as these later chroniclers make

04:09:54.080 --> 04:10:05.760
out. The historian Herald Van Der Linde imagines&nbsp;
an extreme version of the scenes that unfolded.

04:10:05.760 --> 04:10:12.160
The soldiers met almost no resistance, and within&nbsp;
an hour they had taken control of the royal

04:10:12.160 --> 04:10:21.279
palace. It was a massacre. Priests were drowned&nbsp;
in lotus ponds. Stable boys were stabbed as they

04:10:21.279 --> 04:10:27.920
tried to protect the king's horses. Everything&nbsp;
was burned to the ground; the royal residences,

04:10:27.920 --> 04:10:33.840
the watchtower, the pavilion with its&nbsp;
intricate wood carvings, the army barracks,

04:10:33.840 --> 04:10:45.120
the elephant stalls, and the library. By noon, all&nbsp;
that was left of Majapahit was a heap of rubble.

04:10:47.600 --> 04:10:53.600
However, there is little evidence of&nbsp;
widespread burning at the site of the city,

04:10:53.600 --> 04:11:01.600
and no heaps of bodies or mass graves have&nbsp;
ever been found. But what's clear is that

04:11:01.600 --> 04:11:11.121
at this point, human habitation at the&nbsp;
city of Majapahit largely came to an end.

04:11:14.000 --> 04:11:18.880
Its people fled, and all of&nbsp;
its houses and workshops,

04:11:18.880 --> 04:11:26.560
its temples and palaces, were&nbsp;
left to fade and crumble.

04:11:26.560 --> 04:11:32.880
For many ordinary people, life simply&nbsp;
went on. But it must have been a time

04:11:32.880 --> 04:11:40.960
of great upheaval and confusion.&nbsp;
The Javanese text, the Niti Sastra,

04:11:40.960 --> 04:11:46.720
records one vision of the end of the&nbsp;
world that must have captured the mood

04:11:46.720 --> 04:11:57.600
of many on the island of Java as their&nbsp;
world came to an end and another began.

04:11:57.600 --> 04:12:03.520
The signs of the approaching end of this&nbsp;
world will be all kinds of depravity among

04:12:03.520 --> 04:12:10.560
mankind. That is to say, the wise will&nbsp;
turn foolish. The holy men will become

04:12:10.560 --> 04:12:19.120
worldly. Children will abandon their parents.&nbsp;
Princes will lose their empires. The little

04:12:19.120 --> 04:12:30.880
will become great and commit depredations.&nbsp;
In short, everything will be in confusion.

04:12:42.239 --> 04:12:50.319
From this moment on, the Sultanate of Demak would&nbsp;
be the most powerful force on the island of Java,

04:12:50.319 --> 04:12:56.639
and the age of the Hindu-Buddhist kingdoms&nbsp;
on the island would come to an end.

04:12:57.680 --> 04:13:03.520
Most remaining Hindus and Buddhists&nbsp;
would flee east, and took refuge on

04:13:03.520 --> 04:13:12.960
the small island of Bali, which became&nbsp;
a sanctuary for their ancient culture.

04:13:12.960 --> 04:13:20.640
Epic Hindu poems like the Bomantaka were&nbsp;
largely forgotten on the mainland of Java,

04:13:20.640 --> 04:13:29.279
but in Bali they were preserved. One verse&nbsp;
in this poem describes the destruction

04:13:29.279 --> 04:13:35.679
wrought by a demon king on the religious&nbsp;
sanctuaries of a kingdom, and we might find

04:13:35.680 --> 04:13:46.239
it a fitting description for the final days&nbsp;
of Java's last Hindu-Buddhist sanctuaries.

04:13:46.239 --> 04:13:54.399
Heaven is being destroyed and hermitages swept&nbsp;
away because of the swarming demons. The hermits

04:13:54.399 --> 04:14:03.359
have left, have abandoned all and fled far away,&nbsp;
constantly pursued. The miserable wretches. Their

04:14:03.359 --> 04:14:13.759
bodies are lying about in the monasteries, with&nbsp;
their bark clothing and woven bags flung away.

04:14:13.760 --> 04:14:19.279
With its people gone, the city of&nbsp;
Majapahit would have been swiftly

04:14:19.279 --> 04:14:26.800
reclaimed by the fast-growing forest around it.

04:14:26.800 --> 04:14:32.960
The sophisticated drainage system that its&nbsp;
engineers had spent generations perfecting

04:14:32.960 --> 04:14:38.479
the channels, the sluices, and great&nbsp;
reservoirs would have begun to silt up

04:14:38.479 --> 04:14:44.559
within a decade without maintenance.&nbsp;
The water, with nowhere left to go,

04:14:44.560 --> 04:14:53.199
would have pooled and spread, turning streets&nbsp;
into marshland, and marshland into swamp. The

04:14:53.199 --> 04:15:00.000
red bricks that gave the ruins of Majapahit&nbsp;
its distinctive character were fired from the

04:15:00.000 --> 04:15:08.720
volcanic clay of the Brantas River plain, and&nbsp;
in the wet heat of Java, they didn't last long.

04:15:09.680 --> 04:15:19.279
Mosses would take hold first, then lichens and&nbsp;
ferns. The mortar softened and dissolved. The

04:15:19.279 --> 04:15:26.080
strangler figs and banyan trees would move in&nbsp;
next, clambering over the red brick courtyards

04:15:26.080 --> 04:15:33.120
and ceremonial gateways of the Majapahit&nbsp;
capital, their roots clenching the walls

04:15:33.120 --> 04:15:42.960
in their grasp and steadily but surely pulverizing&nbsp;
the brick. Slower-growing teak and mahogany trees

04:15:42.960 --> 04:15:51.920
would arrive next, shading the crumbling walls of&nbsp;
Majapahit beneath their canopies. As all this went

04:15:51.920 --> 04:16:00.080
on, the great rumblings of Java's volcanoes would&nbsp;
have conspired to hide the ruins even further.

04:16:01.680 --> 04:16:09.600
More than ten major eruptions of the nearby&nbsp;
Arjuno-Welirang and Anjasmoro volcanoes took

04:16:09.600 --> 04:16:17.199
place in the centuries that followed, each one&nbsp;
burying the site of Majapahit in ever more ash,

04:16:17.199 --> 04:16:27.439
until some of its structures lay as much as&nbsp;
four meters beneath the surface of the earth.

04:16:27.439 --> 04:16:34.159
In the year 1849, a Dutch traveller called&nbsp;
Wolter Robert van Hoevell passed through

04:16:34.159 --> 04:16:44.960
the site of the old city, and described&nbsp;
the overgrown ruins that still remained.

04:16:44.960 --> 04:16:50.080
The entire ruins were shrouded in a dark&nbsp;
forest and covered with shrubs and trees.

04:16:50.640 --> 04:16:56.319
Majestic banyan trees have rooted their roots&nbsp;
between the stones and pushed walls apart,

04:16:56.319 --> 04:17:00.719
raising their crowns high above&nbsp;
the ruins. That is why nowadays

04:17:00.720 --> 04:17:16.159
the Javanese also call the place&nbsp;
Warningin Lawang, or Banyan Gate.

04:17:16.159 --> 04:17:24.080
The Muslim sultanates that destroyed Majapahit&nbsp;
would not remain the most powerful force in Java

04:17:24.080 --> 04:17:33.279
for long. The next century would see the arrival&nbsp;
of other sea-born powers that would soon form

04:17:33.279 --> 04:17:44.239
their own thalassocracies, or empires of the sea.&nbsp;
These were the powers of European colonialism.

04:17:46.640 --> 04:17:52.960
In 1511, the port town of Malacca&nbsp;
was seized by the Portuguese,

04:17:52.960 --> 04:18:00.319
and from there their influence would&nbsp;
spread all over the archipelago.

04:18:00.319 --> 04:18:06.479
Soon other European powers would arrive,&nbsp;
and they would all begin to influence the

04:18:06.479 --> 04:18:12.080
fractious political life of the island&nbsp;
of Java, and introduced the beginnings

04:18:12.080 --> 04:18:21.840
of an ideology more seductive and powerful&nbsp;
than any religion, the force of capitalism.

04:18:21.840 --> 04:18:29.520
The sultanates of Java would soon come under the&nbsp;
sway of a massive international conglomerate and

04:18:29.520 --> 04:18:38.399
one of the first joint stock companies in the&nbsp;
world, the Dutch East India Company. In 1598,

04:18:38.399 --> 04:18:45.120
a Dutch fleet of eight ships under Jacob van&nbsp;
Neck became the first from the Netherlands to

04:18:45.120 --> 04:18:54.239
reach the Spice Islands of Maluku. In doing&nbsp;
so, they bypassed the Javanese merchants,

04:18:54.239 --> 04:19:04.399
who had long dominated the trade. When these Dutch&nbsp;
ships returned to Europe in the year 1599, laden

04:19:04.399 --> 04:19:15.199
with cloves, nutmeg, and mace, the expedition&nbsp;
yielded an enormous profit of around 400%. One

04:19:15.199 --> 04:19:25.439
Dutch observer would reportedly make the following&nbsp;
remark upon seeing the arrival of this fleet.

04:19:25.439 --> 04:19:34.719
As long as Holland has been Holland, there have&nbsp;
never arrived ships as richly laden as these.

04:19:34.720 --> 04:19:46.159
The age of European colonialism in Southeast&nbsp;
Asia had now begun. Between 1602 and 1796,

04:19:46.159 --> 04:19:52.479
the Dutch East India Company dispatched&nbsp;
almost a million Europeans to Asia aboard

04:19:52.479 --> 04:20:00.479
nearly 5,000 ships, dwarfing the traffic of its&nbsp;
nearest rival, the British East India Company,

04:20:00.479 --> 04:20:11.120
returning with more than 2.5 million&nbsp;
tons of Asian goods and enslaved people.

04:20:11.120 --> 04:20:18.319
Throughout this colonial period, which would&nbsp;
ultimately see Java become a European colony,

04:20:18.319 --> 04:20:26.479
the legacy of the ancient Javanese kingdoms&nbsp;
was largely ignored or intentionally obscured.

04:20:27.120 --> 04:20:33.519
The Dutch in particular had no desire to see&nbsp;
the people of their new colonies rally around

04:20:33.520 --> 04:20:39.840
the memory of any ancient kingdoms, and&nbsp;
mostly declined to investigate the ruins

04:20:39.840 --> 04:20:48.159
that could be found dotted around Java.&nbsp;
The erasure was so extensive that in his

04:20:48.159 --> 04:20:55.840
History of the Indian Archipelago, published&nbsp;
in 1820, the British writer John Crawfurd was

04:20:55.840 --> 04:21:02.319
able to write the following. The theory of&nbsp;
a great monarchy and of an antecedent state

04:21:02.319 --> 04:21:07.840
of high civilization and improvement has been&nbsp;
forged by the national vanity of the Javanese,

04:21:07.840 --> 04:21:17.840
unsupported by a shadow of proof, and&nbsp;
contradicted by unquestionable internal evidence.

04:21:17.840 --> 04:21:27.040
But some memory of Majapahit's glory lived on&nbsp;
in the minds of Java's people in their songs,

04:21:27.040 --> 04:21:39.760
their poetry, their shadow puppet plays, and in&nbsp;
the manuscripts they kept in their libraries.

04:21:39.760 --> 04:21:46.159
In 1894, in a typical expansion&nbsp;
of European power in East Asia,

04:21:46.159 --> 04:21:53.120
the Dutch colonialist government intervened&nbsp;
on the island of Lombok to the east of Bali

04:21:53.120 --> 04:22:04.479
to depose its king, a man named Karangasem,&nbsp;
who was a renowned collector of ancient texts.

04:22:04.479 --> 04:22:09.439
After a protracted war in which&nbsp;
the local people resisted fiercely,

04:22:09.439 --> 04:22:19.199
the Maharaja's palace was finally stormed by&nbsp;
Dutch marines on the 19th of November, 1894.

04:22:19.199 --> 04:22:26.960
The Dutch poured into the palace, and began&nbsp;
a slaughter in which 2,000 were massacred.

04:22:27.760 --> 04:22:36.479
The Maharaja was captured and died a few months&nbsp;
later in a colonial prison. During the fighting,

04:22:36.479 --> 04:22:44.319
Dutch artillery set fire to the Maharaja's palace,&nbsp;
and while its towers burned and its roofs fell in,

04:22:44.319 --> 04:22:53.120
the colonial troops looted the king's treasury,&nbsp;
and carried away more than 130 kilograms of gold,

04:22:53.120 --> 04:23:06.319
3,000 of silver, and countless works of art.&nbsp;
This would become known as the Lombok treasure.

04:23:06.319 --> 04:23:13.279
But among the looters there was also a&nbsp;
Dutchman named Jan Laurens Andries Brandes,

04:23:13.279 --> 04:23:21.199
an expert in Sanskrit and Old Javanese.&nbsp;
This man had heard that the Maharaja

04:23:21.199 --> 04:23:27.599
Karangasem was an avid scholar and&nbsp;
a collector of rare manuscripts.

04:23:28.720 --> 04:23:40.640
As the Maharaja's palace burned, this man ran&nbsp;
to the ruler's library and saved what he could.

04:23:40.640 --> 04:23:47.439
While others ran with gold heaped in their arms,&nbsp;
Brandes frantically gathered all of the ancient

04:23:47.439 --> 04:23:57.040
books he could carry, and fled before the roof of&nbsp;
the palace could collapse. Much of the Maharaja's

04:23:57.040 --> 04:24:03.680
collection went up in flames, and we will never&nbsp;
be able to read what was contained within it,

04:24:03.680 --> 04:24:12.560
but what Brandes was able to save was packed&nbsp;
into a box and sent back to the colonial capital.

04:24:14.960 --> 04:24:22.399
Inside he would later find the only existing&nbsp;
copy of the poem the Deshawarnana, written by

04:24:22.399 --> 04:24:31.199
the self-effacing scribe Prapanca some 500&nbsp;
years earlier. In the pages of that work,

04:24:31.199 --> 04:24:37.840
the voice of the poet could once more bring to&nbsp;
life the description of its great king on his

04:24:37.840 --> 04:24:44.880
journey around the island, the lives and&nbsp;
concerns of the people of medieval Java,

04:24:44.880 --> 04:25:08.640
and at least in words, the kingdom&nbsp;
of Majapahit could live once more.

04:25:17.279 --> 04:25:26.479
I'd like to end this episode with an extract from&nbsp;
an old Javanese poem called the Siwaratrikalpa,

04:25:26.479 --> 04:25:34.399
or the Night Ritual of Shiva, written during&nbsp;
the years of the decline of the Majapahit

04:25:34.399 --> 04:25:43.839
kingdom. In this passage, a hunter named Lubdhaka&nbsp;
stumbles upon the ruins of an ancient temple,

04:25:43.840 --> 04:25:50.319
and like Thomas Stamford Raffles centuries&nbsp;
later, would be struck by the strange

04:25:50.319 --> 04:26:04.799
melancholy of these places where once so&nbsp;
many lives passed, now overgrown with jungle.

04:26:04.800 --> 04:26:11.359
As you listen, imagine how it would feel to&nbsp;
watch the palaces and temples of the kingdom

04:26:11.359 --> 04:26:18.239
of Majapahit crumbling into the earth&nbsp;
as its people left and its reservoirs

04:26:18.239 --> 04:26:25.199
ran dry. Imagine wandering beneath the&nbsp;
shade of that teak forest and watching

04:26:25.199 --> 04:26:33.199
the roots of banyans and silk cotton trees&nbsp;
slowly bind their way over the ruins, as the

04:26:33.199 --> 04:26:42.319
layers of ash fell from the sky season after&nbsp;
season, and covered the works of man. Imagine

04:26:42.319 --> 04:26:58.239
turning away and setting off into the forest&nbsp;
as the sun finally set on Java's golden age.

04:26:58.239 --> 04:27:04.880
A great temple complex from ancient&nbsp;
times rose near a mountain stream,

04:27:04.880 --> 04:27:12.479
and the path there was lonely. The curved trunks&nbsp;
of the water elephants had fallen and crumbled,

04:27:12.479 --> 04:27:20.000
and for lack of care, its wall had almost tumbled&nbsp;
down. The monster heads seemed to be weeping, as

04:27:20.000 --> 04:27:26.720
their covered faces were overgrown with creepers,&nbsp;
and as though sad and weary, the temple guardians

04:27:26.720 --> 04:27:34.479
lay rolled over on the ground. The pavilions in&nbsp;
its courtyard were in ruins. Some of the buildings

04:27:34.479 --> 04:27:41.599
were now only wreckage, while others were rotting&nbsp;
away. Their roofs were broken and had fallen in,

04:27:41.600 --> 04:27:49.279
and beyond repair their pillars stood askew,&nbsp;
swaying back and forth. Heartrending was the

04:27:49.279 --> 04:27:57.920
spectacle of the reliefs. Young maidens in stone&nbsp;
stood, gazing skywards, full of sorrow, because

04:27:57.920 --> 04:28:05.760
now no wandering poets ever came to visit them.&nbsp;
The tower temple soared on high, and its pinnacles

04:28:05.760 --> 04:28:13.199
served as a gathering place for weeds. The sides&nbsp;
were cracked, overgrown by a shady fig tree which

04:28:13.199 --> 04:28:21.679
spread luxuriantly. All the statues were cracked&nbsp;
by a fearsome tangle of vines, and only the main

04:28:21.680 --> 04:28:29.520
deity within was immovably in place, standing&nbsp;
firm in the center of its pedestal. Pandan trees

04:28:29.520 --> 04:28:37.520
hung down over the rocks, with an air of sadness&nbsp;
quite wrapped in the mist. How they longed to be

04:28:37.520 --> 04:28:44.239
visited by the thunder, which would bring with&nbsp;
it the rushing of the rain! As if in sorrow,

04:28:44.239 --> 04:28:50.239
they let their petals droop, petals on which&nbsp;
someone had jotted down some words in a moment

04:28:50.239 --> 04:29:16.399
of inspiration. For is this not an admirable&nbsp;
place for a wandering poet to weave his verses?

04:29:16.399 --> 04:29:21.439
Thank you once again for listening&nbsp;
to the Fall of Civilizations podcast.

